/* BlueForge training content – original questions and lessons written and independently checked for this app (Sept 2026).
   Baseline BS 7671:2018+A2:2022 / GN3. AM2E and AM2 checklists from NET candidate guidance, in our own words. */
window.BF_QB = [{"id":"a001","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"What is the legal status of BS 7671:2018?","options":["A statutory instrument, enforced directly by the HSE with its own penalties","Guidance under the HSW Act 1974 that has the full force of law","A legal requirement for new-build dwellings only, under the Building Act","Non-statutory, but compliance is likely to satisfy relevant statutory duties such as the EAWR 1989"],"answer":3,"explain":"BS 7671 is not itself law. It is a code of practice that courts and enforcing bodies accept as evidence of good practice, and it is referred to by documents such as the Memorandum of Guidance on the EAWR and Approved Document P.","ref":"BS 7671 Introduction / Appendix 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a002","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"In which Part of BS 7671:2018 would you find the definitions of terms such as 'exposed-conductive-part' and 'skilled person'?","options":["Part 3","Part 1","Part 2","Part 5"],"answer":2,"explain":"Part 2 is Definitions. Part 1 covers scope, object and fundamental principles; Part 3 covers assessment of general characteristics; Part 5 covers selection and erection of equipment.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a003","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"Requirements for initial verification and periodic inspection and testing are contained in which Part of BS 7671?","options":["Part 6","Part 5","Part 7","Part 4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Part 6 is Inspection and testing (Chapter 64 initial verification, Chapter 65 periodic inspection and testing). Part 7 covers special installations or locations.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 6","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a004","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"In the regulation number 411.3.3, what does '41' identify?","options":["Appendix 41","Part 41","Section 41","Chapter 41"],"answer":3,"explain":"BS 7671 numbering runs Part–Chapter–Section–Regulation: '4' is Part 4, '41' is Chapter 41 (Protection against electric shock), '411' is Section 411 (ADS) and 411.3.3 is the individual regulation.","ref":"BS 7671 numbering convention","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a005","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"A regulation such as 522.6.202 or 421.1.201 has a final number in the 200 series. What does this indicate?","options":["It applies only to Part 7 special locations","It was introduced by Amendment 2:2022","It is a recommendation, not a requirement","It is a UK-specific (national) regulation"],"answer":3,"explain":"Regulations numbered 200 and upward in their final group are national (UK) additions that do not appear in the harmonised European/IEC documents on which BS 7671 is based.","ref":"BS 7671 numbering convention","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a006","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"Which of the following is excluded from the scope of BS 7671?","options":["Construction and demolition site installations","Electric vehicle charging installations","Solar photovoltaic power supply systems","Electrical equipment of aircraft"],"answer":3,"explain":"Regulation 110.2 excludes items such as equipment of aircraft, railway traction equipment, systems for distribution of electricity to the public and equipment on ships. Construction sites, PV and EV charging all have their own sections in Part 7.","ref":"Reg 110.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a007","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"Extra-low voltage (ELV) is defined as not exceeding:","options":["25 V a.c. or 60 V ripple-free d.c.","50 V a.c. or 120 V ripple-free d.c.","110 V a.c. or 150 V ripple-free d.c.","50 V a.c. or 50 V ripple-free d.c."],"answer":1,"explain":"Part 2 defines ELV as not exceeding 50 V a.c. or 120 V ripple-free d.c., whether between conductors or to earth. 25 V a.c./60 V d.c. are lower thresholds used elsewhere (e.g. where SELV basic protection may be omitted in dry conditions).","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a008","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"On a job you are connecting a Class I immersion heater. Its metal casing, which can be touched and is not normally live but could become live under fault conditions, is classed as:","options":["An extraneous-conductive-part","A protective conductor","A live part","An exposed-conductive-part"],"answer":3,"explain":"An exposed-conductive-part is a conductive part of equipment that can be touched, is not normally live, but can become live under fault conditions. It is part of the electrical equipment, which is what distinguishes it from an extraneous-conductive-part.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a009","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"A metallic water service pipe entering a building from the ground, not forming part of the electrical installation but able to introduce Earth potential, is classed as:","options":["An exposed-conductive-part","A functional earth","An extraneous-conductive-part","A circuit protective conductor"],"answer":2,"explain":"An extraneous-conductive-part is liable to introduce a potential, generally Earth potential, and does not form part of the electrical installation. Incoming metal services are the classic example and require main protective bonding.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a010","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"'Basic protection' is defined as protection against electric shock:","options":["Provided by an RCD of 30 mA or less","Under fault-free conditions","Under single-fault conditions","By automatic disconnection only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Basic protection (formerly direct contact protection) prevents contact with live parts in normal service, e.g. by insulation, barriers or enclosures. Protection under single-fault conditions is fault protection.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a011","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"The definition of 'arm's reach' extends vertically upwards from the surface on which a person stands to a height of:","options":["1.25 m","2.5 m","3.0 m","2.25 m"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arm's reach extends 2.5 m above the standing surface, 1.25 m horizontally and 0.75 m below. 2.25 m is the height limit of bathroom zone 1, a common distractor.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2 (arm's reach)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a012","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"A person adequately advised or supervised by electrically skilled persons so that they can perceive risks and avoid hazards is defined as:","options":["An instructed person (electrically)","A competent person","An ordinary person","A skilled person (electrically)"],"answer":0,"explain":"An instructed person is advised or supervised by skilled persons. A skilled person has the relevant education, training and practical skills themselves. An ordinary person is neither.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a013","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"You run a cable from the main consumer unit in a house to a separate consumer unit in a detached garage. In BS 7671 terms this cable forms:","options":["A ring final circuit","An unfused spur","A final circuit","A distribution circuit"],"answer":3,"explain":"A distribution circuit connects the origin (or an upstream board) to another distribution board or consumer unit. A final circuit connects directly to current-using equipment or socket-outlets. The distinction matters because distribution circuits have 5 s (TN) / 1 s (TT) disconnection times.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a014","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"In the classification of external influences, the code category 'AD' relates to:","options":["Mechanical impact","Presence of foreign solid bodies","Ambient temperature","Presence of water"],"answer":3,"explain":"Appendix 5 codes: AA ambient temperature, AD presence of water, AE foreign solid bodies, AG impact. The first letter A denotes environment, B utilisation, C construction of buildings.","ref":"Appendix 5","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a015","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"For a 32 A final circuit in a TN system with U0 = 230 V, what is the maximum disconnection time required by Table 41.1?","options":["0.2 s","0.4 s","5 s","1 s"],"answer":1,"explain":"Table 41.1 gives 0.4 s for TN systems with 120 V < U0 ≤ 230 V. 0.2 s is the TT value; 5 s and 1 s apply to distribution circuits in TN and TT systems respectively.","ref":"Table 41.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a016","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"A 20 A socket-outlet final circuit forms part of a TT system at 230 V. What maximum disconnection time applies?","options":["0.07 s","0.4 s","1 s","0.2 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"For TT systems with 120 V < U0 ≤ 230 V, Table 41.1 requires 0.2 s. In practice this is achieved with an RCD because the earth electrode resistance is usually too high for an overcurrent device to operate fast enough.","ref":"Table 41.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a017","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"What is the maximum disconnection time permitted for a distribution circuit in a TN system?","options":["0.2 s","1 s","0.4 s","5 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"Distribution circuits, and final circuits outside the scope of Table 41.1, are allowed up to 5 s in a TN system (411.3.2.3). The TT equivalent is 1 s (411.3.2.4).","ref":"Reg 411.3.2.3","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a018","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"For a new socket-outlet circuit in a dwelling, 411.3.3 requires additional protection by an RCD with a rated residual operating current not exceeding 30 mA for socket-outlets rated up to:","options":["20 A","13 A","32 A","63 A"],"answer":2,"explain":"411.3.3 requires 30 mA RCD additional protection for socket-outlets with a rated current not exceeding 32 A (and for mobile equipment up to 32 A used outdoors).","ref":"Reg 411.3.3","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a019","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"A client asks you to omit RCD protection from a new 32 A radial socket circuit in their house, saying they'll sign a risk assessment. What is the correct position?","options":["Permitted if the circuit uses a Type C circuit-breaker instead","Permitted if the written risk assessment is attached to the EIC","Not permitted – the risk-assessment exception doesn't apply to dwellings","Permitted where all the sockets are mounted above 1.2 m"],"answer":2,"explain":"411.3.3 allows omission of RCD protection only where a documented risk assessment says it is unnecessary, and only for installations other than dwellings. In a house the 30 mA RCD is mandatory.","ref":"Reg 411.3.3","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a020","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Regulation 411.3.4 requires additional protection by a 30 mA RCD for which circuits in domestic (household) premises?","options":["Only circuits supplying bathroom luminaires","AC final circuits supplying luminaires","Distribution circuits to outbuildings","Only circuits run in metal conduit"],"answer":1,"explain":"411.3.4 was introduced in the 18th Edition and requires 30 mA RCD additional protection for all a.c. final circuits supplying luminaires within domestic premises.","ref":"Reg 411.3.4","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a021","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Under Regulation 415.1.1, an RCD providing additional protection in an a.c. system must have a rated residual operating current (IΔn) not exceeding:","options":["10 mA","30 mA","100 mA","300 mA"],"answer":1,"explain":"415.1.1 recognises RCDs with IΔn not exceeding 30 mA as additional protection. Note: the 17th Edition also stated 40 ms at 5 × IΔn here; BS 7671:2018 dropped that, although general-type RCDs to BS EN 61008/61009 are still made to trip within 40 ms at 5 × IΔn.","ref":"Reg 415.1.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a022","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Which statement about RCDs used for additional protection is correct?","options":["They may replace basic protection where the RCD is rated at 30 mA","They remove the need for a circuit protective conductor to each point","They are not a sole means of protection; ADS or another measure is still needed","They are only required where the maximum Zs values cannot be met"],"answer":2,"explain":"415.1.2 states that an RCD for additional protection is not a sole means of protection; one of the protective measures of Sections 411 to 414 (e.g. ADS) must still be applied.","ref":"Reg 415.1.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a023","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"What is the key difference between SELV and PELV?","options":["PELV requires a safety isolating transformer; SELV does not","SELV may only be used outdoors","SELV operates at up to 120 V a.c.; PELV at up to 50 V a.c.","PELV circuits are connected to earth; SELV circuits are not"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both are ELV systems supplied from a safety source such as a safety isolating transformer. In SELV the live parts are not connected to earth or to protective conductors of other systems; in PELV the circuit and/or exposed parts may be earthed.","ref":"Section 414","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a024","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Socket-outlets in a SELV system must:","options":["Be protected by a 30 mA RCD in addition to the safety source","Be BS 1363 13 A socket-outlets identified with a yellow label","Have no protective conductor contact and not accept other systems' plugs","Have a protective conductor contact connected to the main earthing terminal"],"answer":2,"explain":"414.4.4 requires SELV plugs and socket-outlets to be incompatible with those of other systems and SELV socket-outlets not to have a protective conductor contact, since SELV must not be connected to earth.","ref":"Reg 414.4.4","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a025","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"You are fitting a Class II luminaire with no earth terminal to an existing lighting point. What should you do with the circuit protective conductor at that point?","options":["Cut it back flush with the sheath, as a Class II luminaire has no use for it","Terminate it in a suitable terminal at the point, not connected to the luminaire","Connect it to the luminaire's metal fixing screw to earth the fitting","Fold it back and tape it to the neutral inside the ceiling rose"],"answer":1,"explain":"411.3.1.1 requires a cpc to be run to and terminated at each point in wiring and at each accessory. Terminating it allows a Class I luminaire to be fitted safely later; it must not be connected to the Class II equipment itself.","ref":"Reg 411.3.1.1","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a026","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Which protective measure is permitted only in installations controlled or supervised by skilled or instructed persons?","options":["Automatic disconnection of supply","Obstacles and placing out of reach","Double or reinforced insulation","SELV from a safety isolating transformer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Section 417 (obstacles and placing out of reach) gives only basic protection and is restricted to installations under the control or supervision of skilled or instructed persons. ADS, double insulation and SELV are generally permitted.","ref":"Section 417","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a027","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"The readily accessible top horizontal surface of an enclosure containing live parts must provide a degree of protection of at least:","options":["IPXXD or IP4X","IPXXB or IP2X","IP65","IPX4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Barriers and enclosures generally need at least IPXXB/IP2X (finger-safe), but readily accessible top horizontal surfaces need at least IPXXD/IP4X (1 mm wire) because small objects can be dropped onto them.","ref":"Regs 416.2.1 / 416.2.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a028","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Within the protective measure 'automatic disconnection of supply', which of the following provides BASIC protection rather than fault protection?","options":["Basic insulation of live parts","Protective earthing","Automatic disconnection by a circuit-breaker","Main protective bonding"],"answer":0,"explain":"In ADS, basic protection is by insulation, barriers or enclosures. Fault protection is by protective earthing, protective equipotential bonding and automatic disconnection in case of a fault.","ref":"Section 411","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a029","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"In a TT system, the condition RA x IΔn ≤ 50 V must be met. What is the maximum earth electrode resistance (RA, including protective conductor) for a 100 mA RCD?","options":["167 Ω","1667 Ω","500 Ω","200 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rearranging: RA ≤ 50 / IΔn. RA ≤ 50 / 0.1 = 500 Ω. (1667 Ω would be for 30 mA and 167 Ω for 300 mA.)","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a030","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"A domestic supply where the distributor provides the earth terminal from the combined neutral and earth (PEN) conductor of the supply cable is which type of system?","options":["TN-S","TN-C-S (PME)","TT","IT"],"answer":1,"explain":"In TN-C-S the neutral and protective functions are combined in a PEN conductor in the supply (PME) and separated at the origin of the installation. TN-S uses a separate earth, typically the supply cable sheath; TT uses the consumer's own earth electrode.","ref":"Part 3 / Part 2 (system types)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a031","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"In a TN-S system the means of earthing for the installation is typically provided by:","options":["The combined neutral and earth (PEN) conductor of the supply","A separate protective conductor, usually the supply cable sheath or armour","An earth electrode driven into the ground by the consumer","The incoming metallic water service pipe at its point of entry"],"answer":1,"explain":"TN-S keeps neutral and protective conductors separate throughout the system; the distributor's earth is often the lead sheath or armour of the service cable. Metal water pipes must never be used as the means of earthing.","ref":"Part 2 (system types)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a032","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"In the system designation 'TT', what does the second letter 'T' indicate?","options":["The source has one point connected directly to earth","Exposed-conductive-parts are earthed independently of the source earth","The source is isolated from earth or earthed via an impedance","Neutral and protective functions are combined in one conductor"],"answer":1,"explain":"First letter = relationship of the source to earth (T = directly earthed). Second letter = relationship of exposed-conductive-parts to earth (T = directly earthed independently of the source, N = connected to the earthed point of the source).","ref":"Part 2 (system types)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a033","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"What maximum external earth fault loop impedance (Ze) does a distributor typically quote for a TN-C-S (PME) supply?","options":["0.35 Ω","21 Ω","0.8 Ω","1.37 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"Typical distributor maxima are 0.35 Ω for TN-C-S, 0.8 Ω for TN-S and 21 Ω for TT (the latter excluding the consumer's electrode).","ref":"Distributor typical values (OSG/GN3)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a034","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"A PME supply has a 25 mm² copper supply neutral. What is the minimum copper csa for the main protective bonding conductors?","options":["10 mm²","25 mm²","6 mm²","16 mm²"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 54.8: for a copper-equivalent supply neutral up to and including 35 mm², main bonding is 10 mm². 25 mm² ≤ 35 mm², so 10 mm² copper.","ref":"Table 54.8","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a035","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"A commercial premises has a PME supply with a 70 mm² copper-equivalent neutral. What minimum copper main protective bonding conductor is required?","options":["16 mm²","25 mm²","10 mm²","35 mm²"],"answer":1,"explain":"Table 54.8 bands: ≤35 mm² → 10; >35–50 → 16; >50–95 → 25; >95–150 → 35; >150 → 50. 70 mm² falls in the >50–95 band, so 25 mm².","ref":"Table 54.8","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a036","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"For a TN-S installation (not PME) with a 16 mm² copper earthing conductor, what size copper main protective bonding conductor would normally be installed?","options":["10 mm²","4 mm²","6 mm²","16 mm²"],"answer":0,"explain":"544.1.1: main bonding must be at least half the csa required for the earthing conductor, minimum 6 mm². 16 / 2 = 8 mm²; the next standard size up is 10 mm².","ref":"Reg 544.1.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a037","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"Using Table 54.7 (protective conductor of the same material as the line conductor), what is the minimum cpc size for a 25 mm² line conductor?","options":["10 mm²","12.5 mm²","25 mm²","16 mm²"],"answer":3,"explain":"Table 54.7: S ≤ 16 → same as S; 16 < S ≤ 35 → 16 mm²; S > 35 → S/2. 25 mm² is in the middle band, so 16 mm².","ref":"Table 54.7","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a038","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"A separate protective conductor that does not form part of a cable or enclosure and is NOT mechanically protected must be at least:","options":["4 mm² copper","6 mm² copper","2.5 mm² copper","1.5 mm² copper"],"answer":0,"explain":"543.1.1: a protective conductor not forming part of a cable or cable enclosure must be at least 2.5 mm² copper if mechanically protected, or 4 mm² copper if not.","ref":"Reg 543.1.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a039","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"A supplementary bonding conductor connecting two extraneous-conductive-parts, without mechanical protection, must be at least:","options":["2.5 mm² copper","4 mm² copper","6 mm² copper","10 mm² copper"],"answer":1,"explain":"544.2.3: a supplementary bonding conductor between two extraneous-conductive-parts must be at least 2.5 mm² copper with mechanical protection or 4 mm² copper without.","ref":"Reg 544.2.3","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a040","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"Where supplementary bonding is used in a circuit protected by a 30 mA RCD, the resistance R between simultaneously accessible parts must not exceed 50 V / IΔn. What is the maximum R?","options":["167 Ω","500 Ω","1667 Ω","50 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"R ≤ 50 / IΔn = 50 / 0.03 = 1666.7 Ω, i.e. 1667 Ω.","ref":"Reg 415.2.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a041","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"Where a gas meter is inside the premises, the main protective bonding connection to the gas installation pipe should, where practicable, be made:","options":["At any convenient point on the gas pipework inside the building","At the boiler connection, where the pipework is usually most accessible to test","On the supply side of the meter, just before the emergency control valve","Consumer's side, before any branch, within 600 mm of the meter outlet union"],"answer":3,"explain":"544.1.2 requires the connection on the consumer's hard metal pipework before any branch, where practicable within 600 mm of the meter outlet union, or at the point of entry if the meter is external. Bonding on the supply side of the meter is not permitted.","ref":"Reg 544.1.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a042","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"What wording must be on the permanent label fixed at the connection of a main protective bonding conductor to a gas pipe?","options":["Safety Electrical Connection – Do Not Remove","Warning – Protective Conductor – Isolate Before Removing","Danger – Earth Bonding – Keep Clear","Earth Connection – Test Annually"],"answer":0,"explain":"514.13.1 requires a label 'Safety Electrical Connection – Do Not Remove' at the connection of every earthing conductor to an earth electrode and every bonding conductor to an extraneous-conductive-part.","ref":"Reg 514.13.1","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a043","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"The joint provided for disconnecting the earthing conductor (e.g. to measure Ze or RA) must be:","options":["Fitted with a finger-operated quick-release clamp","Located outside the building for access by the DNO","Permanently sealed so that it cannot be disconnected","Accessible, and removable only by means of a tool"],"answer":3,"explain":"542.4.2 requires a means of disconnecting the earthing conductor at an accessible position, mechanically strong, and able to be disconnected only with a tool, so testing can be carried out but casual removal is prevented.","ref":"Reg 542.4.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a044","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"Why are PME earthing terminals restricted for supplies to locations such as caravan pitches and outdoor EV charge points?","options":["PME supplies cannot provide a low enough Ze to achieve 0.4 s disconnection","PME earthing prevents surge protective devices from operating correctly","PME supplies are incompatible with the use of 30 mA RCDs on final circuits","An open PEN could raise connected metalwork to a dangerous voltage relative to true earth"],"answer":3,"explain":"With an open PEN, load current returns via the protective conductors, so connected metalwork can approach line voltage. Outdoors, people standing on true earth (often wet, possibly barefoot) are at greater risk, so Part 7 and ESQCR restrict PME use.","ref":"Part 7 / ESQCR (PME)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a045","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"Which of the following would require main protective bonding in a typical dwelling?","options":["An aluminium window frame fixed in masonry","A stainless steel sink supplied by plastic pipework only","A metallic gas installation pipe entering the building","A metal door handle"],"answer":2,"explain":"411.3.1.2 requires bonding of extraneous-conductive-parts such as metallic water, gas and oil services, central heating and structural steel. Isolated metalwork that cannot introduce a potential is not an extraneous-conductive-part.","ref":"Reg 411.3.1.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a046","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"Which of the following is NOT permitted as a device for isolation?","options":["A double-pole switch-disconnector to BS EN 60947-3","A fuse carrier that can be withdrawn from its base","A semiconductor device such as an electronic dimmer","A linked circuit-breaker suitable for isolation"],"answer":2,"explain":"Semiconductor (solid-state) devices must not be used as isolating devices because they do not provide a visible or reliable contact gap. They may be used for functional switching.","ref":"Section 537","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a047","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"An off-load isolator for a motor is mounted out of sight of the motor. What must be provided?","options":["A 30 mA RCD mounted immediately beside the isolator","A means of securing it in the off position, e.g. a padlock","An indicator lamp to show when the motor is running","A second identical isolator installed in parallel with it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Where an isolator is remote from the equipment, or could be operated inadvertently, provision must be made to secure it in the open position (e.g. padlock) to prevent unintentional re-energising.","ref":"Section 537","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a048","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"For a single-phase installation intended for use by ordinary persons (e.g. a house), the main switch must:","options":["Be an RCD of 100 mA","Interrupt the line conductor only","Interrupt the line, neutral and protective conductors","Interrupt both the line and neutral conductors"],"answer":3,"explain":"BS 7671 requires the main linked switch for a household-type single-phase installation to be double-pole so that both live conductors are disconnected. The protective conductor must never be switched.","ref":"Chapter 46","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a049","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"In which earthing system must the neutral conductor always be isolated along with the line conductors?","options":["TN-C-S","TT","It never needs isolating","TN-S"],"answer":1,"explain":"In TN systems the neutral may in some cases be regarded as reliably at earth potential and need not be isolated. In a TT system the neutral cannot be assumed to be at earth potential, so all live conductors including the neutral must be isolated.","ref":"Section 461","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a050","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"A device for emergency switching should be:","options":["Readily accessible and cut off the supply directly with a single action","A plug and socket-outlet located close to the machine","Located behind a locked door to prevent misuse by staff","A functional switch that operates a control relay after a delay"],"answer":0,"explain":"Emergency switching must act quickly and directly on the supply with a single action, be readily accessible and clearly identified. A plug and socket-outlet must not be selected as a means of emergency switching.","ref":"Section 537","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a051","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"Where a push-button is used for emergency switching, it should normally be coloured:","options":["Black, on a grey background with a white legend","Green, on a contrasting white background","Red, on a contrasting (usually yellow) background","Yellow, on a contrasting red background"],"answer":2,"explain":"Emergency switching devices, including push-buttons, should be red with a contrasting background, typically yellow, so they are instantly identifiable.","ref":"Section 537","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a052","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"What is the purpose of functional switching?","options":["To remove an unexpected danger as quickly as possible","To make equipment dead so electrical work can be done safely","To control equipment on/off (or vary it) in normal use","To protect the circuit wiring against overcurrent"],"answer":2,"explain":"Functional switching controls normal operation (e.g. a light switch or dimmer). Isolation makes equipment safe for electrical work; emergency switching removes unexpected danger.","ref":"Section 537","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a053","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"A switch provided for mechanical maintenance of a machine must be designed or installed so that:","options":["It cannot be inadvertently reclosed while work is in progress","It interrupts only the neutral conductor of the motor supply","It is a semiconductor switch so that it operates very quickly","It automatically recloses after a set time delay has elapsed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Switching off for mechanical maintenance must prevent the machine restarting during non-electrical work, e.g. by locking or placing the switch under the control of the person doing the work.","ref":"Section 537","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a054","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"Where unexpected restarting of a motor after a supply failure could cause danger, the motor control must:","options":["Prevent automatic restarting when the supply is restored","Restart the motor automatically as quickly as possible","Include a time delay of at least 5 s before restarting","Use a Type D circuit-breaker"],"answer":0,"explain":"Section 552 requires motors, where unexpected restarting could cause danger, to have means (e.g. a contactor with no-volt release) to prevent automatic restarting after a drop or loss of voltage.","ref":"Section 552","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a055","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"Single-pole switching devices (e.g. a one-way light switch) must be connected in:","options":["The line conductor only","Either line or neutral","The protective conductor","The neutral conductor only"],"answer":0,"explain":"A single-pole switch in the neutral would leave the equipment live when switched off, creating danger during lamp changing or maintenance. Single-pole switching must be in the line conductor.","ref":"Chapter 53","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a056","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"The position indicator of a device for isolation must:","options":["Show OFF only when every pole's contacts have fully opened","Show a green lamp whenever the load is energised","Display the load current flowing through the device","Be visible only when the enclosure cover is removed"],"answer":0,"explain":"An isolating device must reliably show its contact position; the OFF indication must only appear when every pole has actually opened to the required isolating distance.","ref":"Section 537","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a057","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"A cable concealed in a wall at a depth of less than 50 mm may run horizontally within a prescribed zone measured from the top of the wall or partition. How wide is that zone?","options":["200 mm","100 mm","150 mm","300 mm"],"answer":2,"explain":"522.6.202 allows cables within 150 mm of the top of the wall or partition, within 150 mm of an angle formed by two adjoining walls, or horizontally/vertically in line with an accessory.","ref":"Reg 522.6.202","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a058","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"You chase a new cable vertically down a plastered brick wall to a socket-outlet. Which route complies with the prescribed zones without extra protection being needed from mechanical cover?","options":["Vertically 300 mm to the side of the socket","Diagonally from the ceiling corner to the socket","Horizontally 400 mm from the ceiling across the wall","Vertically in line with the socket-outlet box"],"answer":3,"explain":"Permitted zones are within 150 mm of the top of the wall, within 150 mm of a corner, or horizontally/vertically in line with the accessory. Diagonal runs and runs offset from the accessory are outside the zones.","ref":"Reg 522.6.202","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a059","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"In a dwelling, a cable without an earthed metallic covering is concealed less than 50 mm deep in a wall within a prescribed zone. What else is required?","options":["A label on the wall indicating the cable route","A minimum 4 mm² cpc","Additional protection by a 30 mA RCD","Nothing further – being in the zone is sufficient"],"answer":2,"explain":"Zones reduce the chance of penetration but do not prevent it. For cables in zones without earthed metallic covering, earthed conduit or mechanical protection, 522.6.202 requires 30 mA RCD additional protection (for installations not under skilled supervision, which includes dwellings).","ref":"Reg 522.6.202","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a060","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"A cable is run in a partition that has internal metal studs (other than fixings). Regardless of its depth, what does 522.6.203 require if the cable has no earthed metallic covering or mechanical protection?","options":["Installation only in the 150 mm zones","Additional protection by a 30 mA RCD","Supplementary bonding of the metal studs","Use of fire-resistant cable"],"answer":1,"explain":"Metal parts in a partition can damage a cable when screws or nails are driven in, whatever the depth. 522.6.203 therefore requires 30 mA RCD protection unless the cable is otherwise protected (earthed metallic covering, earthed conduit, etc.).","ref":"Reg 522.6.203","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a061","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"When a cable is installed through a fire-rated wall, the hole around it must be:","options":["Filled with any general-purpose expanding foam","Sealed to restore the fire resistance of the wall","Sealed only where the cable exceeds 10 mm²","Left open to allow the cable to dissipate heat"],"answer":1,"explain":"527.2 requires openings through elements of building construction to be sealed according to the fire resistance of the element, so the wiring system does not reduce the building's fire compartmentation.","ref":"Reg 527.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a062","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"A trunking passes through a fire-rated floor. Non-flame-propagating conduit or trunking need not be internally sealed if its internal cross-sectional area does not exceed:","options":["710 mm²","2500 mm²","1000 mm²","100 mm²"],"answer":0,"explain":"Wiring systems penetrating fire-rated elements must generally be internally sealed as well as externally. Non-flame-propagating systems with an internal csa not exceeding 710 mm² are exempt from internal sealing.","ref":"Reg 527.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a063","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"Why does 521.10.202 effectively rule out plastic clips or plastic trunking as the sole means of support for cables?","options":["Plastic supports cannot achieve the required IP rating","Plastic clips raise the operating temperature of the cable","Plastic trunking causes interference with data cables","Cables must not collapse early in a fire and entangle people"],"answer":3,"explain":"In a fire, plastic supports melt and cables can drop across escape routes and entangle people. 521.10.202 requires supports that resist premature collapse, e.g. metal clips or fixings.","ref":"Reg 521.10.202","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a064","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"Band I (e.g. data or bell) and Band II (230 V) circuits are to be installed in the same trunking. Which is acceptable?","options":["Run them together if the Band II circuits are RCD protected","Segregate them by a partition, or insulate all for the highest voltage","Run them together provided the Band I cables have a screen","Band I cables may never share any route with Band II cables"],"answer":1,"explain":"528.1 requires Band I and Band II circuits not to share a wiring system unless segregated (e.g. by a partition in the trunking) or every cable is insulated for the highest voltage present.","ref":"Reg 528.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a065","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"In an IP code, what does the FIRST characteristic numeral indicate?","options":["Protection against solid foreign objects","Protection against ingress of water","The maximum ambient temperature","Protection against mechanical impact"],"answer":0,"explain":"The first numeral (0–6) covers solid objects and access to hazardous parts; the second (0–8) covers water. Mechanical impact is covered separately by IK codes.","ref":"BS EN 60529 / Reg 416.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a066","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"On a new three-phase distribution circuit, the line conductors L1, L2 and L3 should be identified as:","options":["Brown, blue and grey","Red, yellow and blue","Brown, black and blue","Brown, black and grey"],"answer":3,"explain":"Harmonised colours: L1 brown, L2 black, L3 grey, neutral blue, protective conductor green-and-yellow. Red/yellow/blue is the pre-2004 scheme.","ref":"Table 51 / Section 514","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a067","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"The green-and-yellow colour combination may be used for:","options":["Any conductor provided it is sleeved at terminations","Protective conductors and functional earth only in data cabling","Protective conductors only","Neutral conductors in three-phase circuits"],"answer":2,"explain":"514.4.2 reserves the bi-colour combination green-and-yellow exclusively for identification of protective conductors; it must not be used for any other purpose.","ref":"Reg 514.4.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a068","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"When the blue conductor of a flat twin and cpc cable is used as a switched line in a lighting circuit, you must:","options":["Leave it blue, as colour doesn't matter in switch wiring","Tape it black to show it is a switched conductor","Mark it brown (or L) at its terminations","Sleeve it green-and-yellow at each end"],"answer":2,"explain":"Section 514 requires conductors to be identified by colour or marking. A blue conductor used as a line must be marked brown (or 'L') at its terminations so it is not mistaken for a neutral.","ref":"Section 514","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a069","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"Within domestic (household) premises, consumer units and similar switchgear assemblies must:","options":["Be installed at least 2 m above finished floor level","Be located outside the dwelling in a meter cupboard","Be of all-insulated plastic construction for shock protection","Have a non-combustible enclosure, or be in a non-combustible cabinet"],"answer":3,"explain":"421.1.201 requires non-combustible enclosures (e.g. ferrous metal) for consumer units in domestic premises, following fires originating from connections in plastic consumer units.","ref":"Reg 421.1.201","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a070","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"For an installation supplied directly from a public LV supply, what is the maximum voltage drop for a lighting circuit, per the Appendix 4 guidance, at 230 V?","options":["11.5 V (5 %)","9.2 V (4 %)","6.9 V (3 %)","4.6 V (2 %)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Appendix 4 gives 3 % for lighting and 5 % for other uses from a public LV supply. 230 x 0.03 = 6.9 V; for other circuits 230 x 0.05 = 11.5 V.","ref":"Appendix 4 (Section 6.4)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a071","topic":"Special locations","q":"In a bathroom (Section 701), zone 1 extends up to a height above the finished floor of:","options":["3.0 m in all cases, measured from the floor","2.5 m in all cases, measured from the bath rim","2.25 m (or the highest fixed shower head, if higher)","2.0 m, or the ceiling height if that is lower"],"answer":2,"explain":"Zone 1 is bounded by the finished floor and the higher of 2.25 m or the highest fixed shower head or water outlet. 2.5 m is arm's reach, not a zone limit.","ref":"Section 701","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a072","topic":"Special locations","q":"Zone 2 in a bathroom extends horizontally beyond the boundary of zone 1 by:","options":["1.2 m","0.3 m","3 m","0.6 m"],"answer":3,"explain":"Zone 2 extends 0.6 m horizontally from the zone 1 boundary (up to the zone 1 height limit). 3 m is the distance within which socket-outlets are generally not permitted.","ref":"Section 701","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a073","topic":"Special locations","q":"You are installing a new electric shower circuit in a domestic bathroom. What RCD protection does Section 701 require?","options":["30 mA RCD, as for every circuit in the location","None, because the shower is fixed equipment","An RCD only if the maximum Zs cannot be met","A 100 mA time-delayed RCD at the origin only"],"answer":0,"explain":"701.411.3.3 requires all circuits of the location to be provided with additional protection by one or more RCDs of 30 mA, including circuits for showers and circuits passing through the location.","ref":"Reg 701.411.3.3","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a074","topic":"Special locations","q":"In a room containing a bath or shower, socket-outlets (other than SELV and shaver supply units) are not permitted within what horizontal distance of the zone 1 boundary?","options":["0.6 m","1.2 m","2.25 m","3 m"],"answer":3,"explain":"Section 701 prohibits socket-outlets within 3 m horizontally of the zone 1 boundary, except SELV socket-outlets and shaver supply units complying with BS EN 61558-2-5.","ref":"Section 701","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a075","topic":"Special locations","q":"What is the minimum degree of protection for fixed equipment installed in bathroom zones 1 and 2 (no cleaning water jets)?","options":["IPX4","IPX7","IPX2","IPX5"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equipment in zones 1 and 2 needs at least IPX4; IPX5 where water jets are likely for cleaning. Equipment in zone 0 needs IPX7.","ref":"Section 701","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a076","topic":"Special locations","q":"Supplementary protective bonding in a bathroom may be omitted when which conditions are met?","options":["The installation is TN-S and the bathroom has a mechanical extractor fan","All final circuits meet ADS times, have 30 mA RCDs, and extraneous parts are main-bonded","The bathroom has no socket-outlets and all circuits are protected by Type B circuit-breakers of 32 A or less","All circuits have 100 mA RCD protection and all pipework is copper"],"answer":1,"explain":"701.415.2 allows supplementary bonding to be omitted if the installation has main bonding to which all extraneous-conductive-parts of the location are connected, and all final circuits of the location meet disconnection times and have 30 mA RCD protection.","ref":"Reg 701.415.2","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a077","topic":"Special locations","q":"Around a swimming pool basin, zone 1 extends horizontally from the rim of the basin by:","options":["2 m","3.5 m","1.5 m","0.6 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"Section 702: zone 1 extends 2 m horizontally from the rim and 2.5 m vertically; zone 2 extends a further 1.5 m beyond zone 1.","ref":"Section 702","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a078","topic":"Special locations","q":"On a construction site, a single-phase reduced low voltage (RLV) system uses a 110 V centre-tapped transformer. What is the maximum voltage to earth?","options":["55 V","25 V","110 V","63.5 V"],"answer":0,"explain":"The secondary centre tap is earthed, so each line is half the secondary voltage from earth: 110 / 2 = 55 V.","ref":"Section 704 / Reg 411.8","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a079","topic":"Special locations","q":"On site, 110 V industrial plugs and socket-outlets to BS EN 60309-2 are colour-coded:","options":["Blue","Yellow","White","Red"],"answer":1,"explain":"BS EN 60309-2 colour coding: yellow 110 V, blue 230 V, red 400 V.","ref":"Section 704 / BS EN 60309-2","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a080","topic":"Special locations","q":"On a construction site, a 230 V socket-outlet circuit rated 32 A supplies hand-held tools. If RLV, SELV and electrical separation are not used, what protection is required?","options":["A 300 mA RCD","A Type D circuit-breaker","No RCD, as ADS is enough","Additional protection by a 30 mA RCD"],"answer":3,"explain":"Section 704 requires socket-outlet circuits up to 32 A to be protected by RLV, SELV, electrical separation, or ADS with 30 mA RCD additional protection.","ref":"Section 704","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a081","topic":"Special locations","q":"In agricultural and horticultural premises, for protection against fire, circuits must be protected by an RCD with a rated residual operating current not exceeding:","options":["100 mA","300 mA","500 mA","30 mA"],"answer":1,"explain":"705.422.7 requires RCDs not exceeding 300 mA for fire protection in agricultural and horticultural premises, which often contain combustible materials such as hay and straw.","ref":"Reg 705.422.7","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a082","topic":"Special locations","q":"Caravan pitch socket-outlets (Section 708) must be installed with their lowest part at a height above ground of:","options":["At least 1.8 m","0.3 m to 1.0 m","0.5 m to 1.5 m","1.0 m to 2.0 m"],"answer":2,"explain":"Section 708 requires pitch socket-outlets to be between 0.5 m and 1.5 m above ground, to BS EN 60309-2, and at least IP44.","ref":"Section 708","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a083","topic":"Special locations","q":"How must each caravan pitch socket-outlet be protected?","options":["By one 30 mA RCD shared between up to six socket-outlets","By a single 100 mA time-delayed RCD at the site origin","Individually – its own overcurrent device and own 30 mA RCD","By the fuse or MCB inside each caravan's own consumer unit"],"answer":2,"explain":"Section 708 requires each socket-outlet to have individual overcurrent protection and individual 30 mA RCD protection, so a fault in one caravan does not disconnect others.","ref":"Section 708","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a084","topic":"Special locations","q":"On a caravan park supplied by PME, what earthing arrangement is used for the pitch socket-outlets?","options":["Unearthed – the pitch socket-outlets rely on RCD protection only","PME – the earth is extended to each pitch and connected to the caravan chassis","TT – the PME earth must not be connected to the pitch protective conductors","PME, plus a supplementary bond from each caravan to the water standpipe"],"answer":2,"explain":"ESQCR 2002 prohibits connecting a combined neutral and protective conductor to metalwork in a caravan or boat, and Section 708 requires the pitch protective conductors not to be connected to a PME earthing facility.","ref":"Section 708 / ESQCR 2002","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a085","topic":"Special locations","q":"For an outdoor EV charge point on a PME supply, one option to avoid TT earthing is a device that disconnects the charge point when the voltage between the circuit protective conductor and Earth exceeds:","options":["50 V r.m.s., within 0.4 s","25 V r.m.s., within 0.2 s","110 V r.m.s., within 1 s","70 V r.m.s., within 5 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"722.411.4.1 permits PME for an outdoor charge point where a device monitors the cpc-to-Earth voltage and disconnects (live conductors and protective conductor) within 5 s if it exceeds 70 V r.m.s. Other options include a TT arrangement.","ref":"Reg 722.411.4.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a086","topic":"Special locations","q":"What RCD type is acceptable for protecting a mode 3 EV charging point that has no built-in DC fault protection?","options":["Type B, or Type A with 6 mA DC detection","Type AC with a 30 mA rating","A 100 mA time-delayed S-type RCD","Type F only, with no other device"],"answer":0,"explain":"EV chargers can produce smooth DC fault currents that blind Type AC/A devices. Section 722 requires Type B, or Type A (or F) with equipment providing disconnection for DC fault currents above 6 mA.","ref":"Section 722","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a087","topic":"Special locations","q":"Why must a PV installation be treated as live on the DC side even after the AC isolator has been switched off?","options":["The DNO back-feeds the DC cabling via the meter","The AC isolator switches only the neutral conductor","The inverter stores charge for up to 24 hours","The modules generate whenever they're illuminated"],"answer":3,"explain":"PV modules produce a DC voltage whenever light falls on them, so opening the AC isolator does not make the DC cabling dead. Section 712 requires isolation on both AC and DC sides and appropriate warning labels.","ref":"Section 712","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a088","topic":"Special locations","q":"A small single-phase domestic PV inverter rated up to 16 A per phase is connected under EREC G98. What must be done regarding the DNO?","options":["Obtain written DNO approval before installation","Nothing – DNO notification is not required","Notify the HSE instead of the DNO","Notify the DNO within 28 days of commissioning"],"answer":3,"explain":"G98 covers micro-generators up to 16 A per phase and uses a 'connect and notify' process: the installer notifies the DNO within 28 days of commissioning. Larger generation falls under G99, which needs prior approval.","ref":"ENA EREC G98","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a089","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"Under BS 7671:2018+A2:2022, Regulation 443.4.1 requires protection against transient overvoltages where the consequences of an overvoltage could:","options":["Loss of a television signal","Serious injury to, or loss of, human life","Tripping of an RCD","Minor inconvenience to a single occupant"],"answer":1,"explain":"A2:2022 443.4.1 requires SPD protection where the consequences could (i) result in serious injury to, or loss of, human life; (ii) result in failure of a safety service; or (iii) result in significant financial or data loss. For all other cases SPDs shall be fitted unless the owner declines them and accepts the risk.","ref":"Reg 443.4.1 (A2:2022)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a090","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"Under BS 7671:2018+A2:2022, for an installation (including a dwelling) that does not fall into any of the categories listed in 443.4.1, what applies regarding SPDs?","options":["No SPD is needed unless a CRL risk calculation shows one is required","SPDs shall be fitted unless the owner declines them and accepts the risk","SPDs are never required in single dwelling units","An AFDD must be fitted in place of an SPD"],"answer":1,"explain":"A2:2022 removed both the risk-assessment (CRL calculation) route and the single-dwelling exemption from 443.4. For all cases not covered by 443.4.1 (i)-(iii), SPDs shall be fitted unless the owner of the installation declines such protection and accepts the risk of damage to wiring and equipment as tolerable.","ref":"Reg 443.4.1 (A2:2022)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a091","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"A Type 1 SPD is primarily intended for:","options":["Local protection of individual socket-outlets close to sensitive equipment","Installations exposed to direct lightning current, e.g. with a lightning protection system","Replacing the RCD at the origin of a TT installation fed by an overhead line","Protecting against earth leakage currents from electronic equipment"],"answer":1,"explain":"Type 1 SPDs handle partial lightning currents (10/350 µs), typically at the origin where there is an LPS. Type 2 (8/20 µs) are installed at distribution boards; Type 3 give local protection near sensitive equipment.","ref":"Section 534","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a092","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"To be effective, the total length of the connecting conductors to an SPD (line to SPD plus SPD to earth) should preferably not exceed:","options":["1.5 m","3 m","5 m","0.5 m"],"answer":3,"explain":"Long connecting leads add inductive voltage drop that reduces SPD effectiveness. Section 534 states the total lead length should preferably not exceed 0.5 m (and in any case not exceed 1 m).","ref":"Reg 534.4.8","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a093","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"Under BS 7671:2018+A2:2022, AFDDs SHALL be installed for single-phase a.c. final circuits supplying socket-outlets up to 32 A in which of the following?","options":["Detached garages","All new owner-occupied houses","Industrial workshops","Houses in multiple occupation (HMOs)"],"answer":3,"explain":"421.1.7 (A2) makes AFDDs mandatory for such circuits in higher-risk residential buildings, HMOs, purpose-built student accommodation and care homes. For all other premises they are recommended.","ref":"Reg 421.1.7 (A2:2022)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a094","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"Where an AFDD is installed to protect a final circuit, it should be placed:","options":["Inside the appliance plug","At the origin of the circuit to be protected","At the meter position before the main switch","At the furthest socket-outlet"],"answer":1,"explain":"421.1.7 requires the AFDD to be placed at the origin of the circuit, i.e. in the consumer unit or distribution board, so the whole circuit is monitored.","ref":"Reg 421.1.7","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a095","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"Which fault can an AFDD detect that neither an MCB nor an RCD would respond to?","options":["A bolted short-circuit between line and neutral","An earth fault with a residual current of 30 mA","A series arc at a loose terminal carrying load current","An overload of 1.45 times the device rating"],"answer":2,"explain":"A series arc (e.g. a loose terminal or damaged conductor) draws no more than load current and no residual current, so MCBs and RCDs do not see it. AFDDs analyse the current waveform for arc signatures.","ref":"Reg 421.1.7","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a096","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Under Regulation 14 of the Electricity at Work Regulations 1989, live working is permitted only when:","options":["It is unreasonable to be dead, reasonable to work live, and suitable precautions are taken","The circuit is protected by a 30 mA RCD and the worker is a skilled person","The voltage is below 400 V and insulated gloves are worn at all times","The customer has insisted in writing that the supply must remain on"],"answer":0,"explain":"Regulation 14 sets three conditions, all of which must be met: it is unreasonable in all the circumstances for the equipment to be dead; it is reasonable for the person to work on or near it live; and suitable precautions (including PPE where necessary) are taken.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 14","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a097","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Which Regulation of the EAWR 1989 requires persons to have the technical knowledge or experience to prevent danger (or to be under appropriate supervision)?","options":["Regulation 12","Regulation 16","Regulation 13","Regulation 4"],"answer":1,"explain":"Regulation 16 deals with competence to prevent danger and injury. Reg 4 covers systems, work activities and protective equipment; Reg 12 means for cutting off supply and isolation; Reg 13 precautions for work on equipment made dead.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 16","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a098","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Regulation 12 of the EAWR 1989 requires suitable means to be available for:","options":["Adequate working space, access and lighting","Cutting off the supply and isolating equipment","Earthing or other precautions against charge","Protecting systems against excess current"],"answer":1,"explain":"Regulation 12 requires means for cutting off the supply and for isolation. Excess current protection is Reg 11; earthing is Reg 8; working space, access and lighting is Reg 15.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 12","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a099","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Regulation 13 of the EAWR 1989 requires that when equipment has been made dead for work:","options":["A 30 mA RCD is fitted to the circuit before work starts","The work is always carried out by a minimum of two people","It is inspected and tested again every twelve months thereafter","Precautions are taken to stop it becoming charged during the work"],"answer":3,"explain":"Regulation 13 covers precautions for work on equipment made dead, e.g. securing isolation with locks, posting warning notices and proving dead, so the equipment cannot be re-energised during the work.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 13","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a100","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Under section 7 of the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, employees have a duty to:","options":["Prepare and revise the company's written health and safety policy","Take reasonable care for themselves and others, and co-operate with their employer","Carry out and record all the risk assessments for the site themselves","Provide and maintain safe plant and safe systems of work for all staff"],"answer":1,"explain":"Section 7 places duties on employees. Providing safe plant and systems of work is an employer duty under section 2.","ref":"HSWA 1974 s.7","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a101","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"According to HSE GS38, test probes should have an exposed metal tip of not more than:","options":["6 mm, preferably 4 mm, with finger guards","1 mm in all cases, with no exceptions","4 mm (preferably 2 mm), or be spring-loaded","10 mm, to allow contact with recessed terminals"],"answer":2,"explain":"GS38 recommends a maximum 4 mm exposed tip, preferably 2 mm or less or spring-loaded retractable, to reduce the risk of accidental short circuits, together with finger barriers and suitably protected leads.","ref":"HSE GS38","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a102","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Why does GS38 advise against using a multimeter to prove a circuit dead?","options":["Multimeters are unable to measure a.c. voltages above 50 V","Selecting the wrong range or function can give a false 'dead' reading","Multimeters may only be used for insulation resistance testing","GS38 prohibits multimeters from being taken onto building sites"],"answer":1,"explain":"A multimeter can easily be left on the wrong setting (e.g. current or resistance), giving a false reading or causing a fault. GS38 recommends a proprietary two-pole voltage indicator for proving dead.","ref":"HSE GS38","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a103","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"In the safe isolation procedure, after testing the circuit and finding it dead, what must you do next?","options":["Switch the circuit back on briefly to confirm the tester works","Re-prove the voltage indicator on a known live source or proving unit","Remove your lock so others can use the circuit when needed","Start work immediately, as the circuit has been proved dead"],"answer":1,"explain":"The indicator is proved before and after the test. If it failed during the test, a 'dead' reading would be false; re-proving confirms the dead result was genuine.","ref":"HSE GS38 / safe isolation procedure","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a104","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Which sequence is correct for safe isolation?","options":["Isolate, test dead, identify the circuit, lock off, then prove the tester","Identify, isolate, secure (lock and label), prove tester, test dead, re-prove tester","Identify, test dead, isolate, remove the fuse and keep it, then start work","Prove tester, test dead, isolate, lock off and label, then re-prove the tester"],"answer":1,"explain":"The circuit must be identified, isolated and secured before testing so the test confirms the isolation. The voltage indicator is proved on a known source before and after testing dead.","ref":"Safe isolation procedure","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a105","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"When proving dead at a single-phase isolator, which combinations should be tested?","options":["Line–neutral, line–earth and neutral–earth","Neutral–earth only, at the isolator terminals","Line–neutral only, using a two-pole tester","Line–earth only, as neutral is at earth potential"],"answer":0,"explain":"Testing all three combinations detects a live conductor however it is connected, including a borrowed neutral or a line connected to the wrong terminal.","ref":"Safe isolation procedure","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a106","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"On a three-phase and neutral isolator, how many tests are needed to prove dead between all conductors?","options":["10","6","4","7"],"answer":0,"explain":"Line–line: L1–L2, L1–L3, L2–L3 = 3. Line–neutral: 3. Line–earth: 3. Neutral–earth: 1. Total 3 + 3 + 3 + 1 = 10.","ref":"Safe isolation procedure","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a107","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"You have isolated a circuit at a consumer unit in an occupied house. Which is the best way to secure the isolation?","options":["Remove the consumer unit cover so it's obvious work is going on","Lock it off with your own padlock and key, and post a warning notice","Switch off the MCB and put insulating tape over the toggle","Switch off and ask the homeowner not to touch the consumer unit at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tape or verbal instructions do not prevent re-energisation. A lock-off device with a padlock under the worker's control, plus a warning notice, meets the intent of EAWR Reg 13.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 13 / safe isolation","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a108","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"The requirement of Part P of the Building Regulations is that reasonable provision shall be made in the design and installation of electrical installations to protect persons:","options":["From excessive energy use and high electricity bills","Operating, maintaining or altering them, from fire or injury","From electromagnetic interference caused by the wiring","Against lightning strikes to the building structure"],"answer":1,"explain":"Requirement P1 is about protecting people operating, maintaining or altering the installation from fire or injury. It applies to electrical installations in and around dwellings.","ref":"Building Regulations Part P","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a109","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"Which of the following electrical work in a dwelling is notifiable under Part P in both England and Wales?","options":["Changing a lamp","Installing a new circuit","Replacing a damaged socket-outlet faceplate like-for-like in a hallway","Replacing a flexible cord to a pendant"],"answer":1,"explain":"In both England and Wales, installing a new circuit and replacing a consumer unit are notifiable, as is certain work in special locations. Like-for-like replacement of accessories outside special locations is not notifiable.","ref":"Building Regulations Part P (England and Wales)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a110","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"A homeowner asks you to replace their consumer unit. Under Part P, this work is:","options":["Non-notifiable if the new unit has the same number of ways","Exempt if an MWC is issued","Notifiable only if the house is rented","Notifiable, via a competent person scheme or Building Control"],"answer":3,"explain":"Consumer unit replacement is notifiable in England and Wales. It must be self-certified through a registered competent person scheme, or notified to Building Control before work starts. An EIC, not an MWC, is issued.","ref":"Building Regulations Part P","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a111","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"An electrician who is NOT registered with a competent person scheme wants to carry out notifiable work in a dwelling. What must they do?","options":["Have the homeowner sign a disclaimer accepting responsibility","Notify Building Control before starting, so the work can be inspected","Nothing further, provided they hold the 2391 qualification","Carry out the work and then issue an EICR on completion"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without competent person scheme registration, notifiable work must go through Building Control (e.g. a building notice) before work begins. Qualifications alone do not allow self-certification.","ref":"Building Regulations Part P","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a112","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"When a registered competent person completes notifiable work, how is Building Control informed?","options":["The homeowner must apply to Building Control for an inspection","The DNO notifies Building Control when the meter is next read","The scheme notifies Building Control; the occupier gets a compliance certificate","The electrician posts a copy of the EIC to the local council"],"answer":2,"explain":"Competent person scheme members self-certify: they notify via their scheme, which informs the local authority, and the occupier receives a compliance certificate as well as the EIC.","ref":"Building Regulations Part P","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a113","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"You add a new radial circuit for a kitchen appliance. Which certificate should be issued?","options":["Electrical Installation Certificate (EIC)","Minor Electrical Installation Works Certificate (MWC)","No certificate for a single circuit","Electrical Installation Condition Report (EICR)"],"answer":0,"explain":"A new circuit requires an EIC with a schedule of inspections and schedule of test results. An MWC is only for additions or alterations to an existing circuit that do not include a new circuit.","ref":"Chapter 64 / Appendix 6","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a114","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"You add one socket-outlet to an existing ring final circuit (not a new circuit). Which certificate is appropriate?","options":["Minor Electrical Installation Works Certificate (MWC)","Electrical Installation Condition Report (EICR)","Electrical Danger Notification only","Electrical Installation Certificate (EIC)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The MWC covers additions and alterations to an existing circuit that do not include the provision of a new circuit, such as adding a socket-outlet or lighting point.","ref":"Chapter 64 / Appendix 6","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a115","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"On a three-signature EIC, the three signatories are responsible for:","options":["Design, DNO approval and Building Control","Inspection, testing and client acceptance","Design, construction, and inspection and testing","Construction, testing and scheme assessment"],"answer":2,"explain":"The EIC has separate declarations for design, construction and inspection and testing. Where one person is responsible for all three, a single-signature form may be used.","ref":"Chapter 64 / Appendix 6","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a116","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"During an EICR you find a socket-outlet with a broken faceplate exposing live terminals. What classification code applies?","options":["C3 – Improvement recommended","C2 – Potentially dangerous, urgent remedial action required","FI – Further investigation required","C1 – Danger present, immediate remedial action required"],"answer":3,"explain":"Accessible live parts present immediate danger, so C1. The inspector should take immediate action (e.g. make safe or isolate with the duty holder's agreement) as well as recording it.","ref":"GN3 / EICR classification codes","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a117","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"On an EICR, what does code C2 mean?","options":["Further investigation required without delay","Improvement recommended","Danger present – immediate remedial action required","Potentially dangerous – urgent remedial action required"],"answer":3,"explain":"C1 = danger present; C2 = potentially dangerous, urgent remedial action required; C3 = improvement recommended; FI = further investigation required without delay.","ref":"GN3 / EICR classification codes","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a118","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"An EICR records several C3 observations and one FI. What should the overall assessment be?","options":["Satisfactory with recommendations","Satisfactory","Unsatisfactory","Not applicable until the FI is resolved"],"answer":2,"explain":"Any C1, C2 or FI makes the overall assessment unsatisfactory. C3s alone do not make an installation unsatisfactory.","ref":"GN3 / EICR","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a119","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"What is the typical recommended maximum interval between periodic inspections for an owner-occupied domestic installation?","options":["5 years or on change of occupancy","3 years","1 year","10 years or on change of occupancy"],"answer":3,"explain":"GN3 suggests up to 10 years (or change of occupancy) for owner-occupied domestic premises; rented domestic, commercial and many other premises are typically 5 years.","ref":"GN3 (recommended maximum intervals)","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a120","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"What is the typical recommended maximum interval between periodic inspections for an industrial installation?","options":["10 years","5 years","3 years","1 year"],"answer":2,"explain":"GN3 guidance: industrial 3 years, commercial 5 years, domestic 10 years (rented domestic 5 years).","ref":"GN3 (recommended maximum intervals)","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a121","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"Under the Electrical Safety Standards in the Private Rented Sector (England) Regulations 2020, landlords must have the installation inspected and tested at intervals of no more than:","options":["10 years","5 years","1 year","3 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"The 2020 regulations require private landlords in England to ensure inspection and testing by a qualified person at intervals of no more than 5 years, and to give tenants a copy of the report.","ref":"Electrical Safety Standards in the Private Rented Sector (England) Regulations 2020","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"b001","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A 3 kW immersion heater is connected to a 230 V supply. What current does it draw?","options":["0.08 A","17.63 A","13.04 A","6.52 A"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Formula: I = P / V\n2. Substitute: I = 3000 / 230\n3. Result: I = 13.04 A\n4. Check: P = V x I = 230 x 13.04 = 2999 W (approx 3 kW). Dividing V by P (0.08 A) is the classic upside-down mistake.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b002","topic":"Electrical science","q":"What is the hot resistance of the element in a 3 kW, 230 V immersion heater?","options":["13.04 Ω","17.63 Ω","0.06 Ω","690 Ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Formula: P = V²/R, so R = V² / P\n2. Substitute: R = 230² / 3000 = 52 900 / 3000\n3. Result: R = 17.63 Ω\n4. Check with Ohm's law: I = 230 / 17.63 = 13.04 A, and 230 x 13.04 = 3000 W.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b003","topic":"Electrical science","q":"Three resistors of 12 Ω, 18 Ω and 30 Ω are connected in SERIES across 230 V. What current flows?","options":["39.61 A","0.26 A","19.17 A","3.83 A"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Series: RT = R1 + R2 + R3 = 12 + 18 + 30 = 60 Ω\n2. I = V / RT = 230 / 60\n3. Result: I = 3.83 A\n4. 39.61 A would be the answer if you had wrongly treated them as parallel (5.81 Ω).","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b004","topic":"Electrical science","q":"Two heating elements of 20 Ω and 30 Ω are connected in PARALLEL. What is the total resistance?","options":["12.00 Ω","50.00 Ω","25.00 Ω","0.083 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Parallel: 1/RT = 1/R1 + 1/R2\n2. 1/RT = 1/20 + 1/30 = 0.05 + 0.0333 = 0.0833\n3. RT = 1 / 0.0833 = 12.00 Ω (or product over sum: (20 x 30)/(20 + 30) = 600/50 = 12 Ω)\n4. Leaving the answer as 0.083 means you forgot to take the reciprocal; total parallel resistance is always less than the smallest branch.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b005","topic":"Electrical science","q":"Resistors of 10 Ω, 20 Ω and 20 Ω are connected in parallel across 230 V. What is the total current drawn?","options":["46.00 A","4.60 A","23.00 A","13.80 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. 1/RT = 1/10 + 1/20 + 1/20 = 0.10 + 0.05 + 0.05 = 0.20\n2. RT = 1 / 0.20 = 5.00 Ω\n3. I = V / RT = 230 / 5.00 = 46.00 A\n4. Check: branch currents 23 + 11.5 + 11.5 = 46 A. 4.60 A is the series answer.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b006","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A cable run has a total loop resistance of 0.25 Ω and carries 32 A. What power is lost as heat in the cable?","options":["256 W","8 W","64 W","4096 W"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: P = I²R\n2. Substitute: P = 32² x 0.25 = 1024 x 0.25\n3. Result: P = 256 W\n4. 8 W is just I x R (that is the volt drop in volts, not watts); 4096 W comes from I²/R.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b007","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A single-phase motor draws 8 A at 230 V with a power factor of 0.8. What is its true (active) power?","options":["1840 W","2300 W","1472 W","1178 W"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Apparent power S = V x I = 230 x 8 = 1840 VA\n2. True power P = V x I x pf = 1840 x 0.8\n3. Result: P = 1472 W\n4. 1840 W ignores the power factor; 2300 W divides by pf instead of multiplying.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b008","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A 4.6 kW single-phase load operates at 230 V with a power factor of 0.85. What is the design current?","options":["23.53 A","20.00 A","17.00 A","13.58 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: I = P / (V x pf)\n2. Substitute: I = 4600 / (230 x 0.85) = 4600 / 195.5\n3. Result: I = 23.53 A\n4. 20.00 A ignores pf; 17.00 A multiplies by pf; 13.58 A wrongly brings in √3 for a single-phase load. A poor pf always INCREASES current.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b009","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A 22 kW three-phase motor runs on a 400 V supply at a power factor of 0.9. What is the line current?","options":["61.11 A","35.28 A","31.75 A","20.37 A"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Formula: IL = P / (√3 x VL x pf)\n2. √3 x 400 x 0.9 = 1.732 x 400 x 0.9 = 623.5\n3. IL = 22 000 / 623.5 = 35.28 A\n4. 61.11 A is the single-phase formula (no √3); 31.75 A forgets the pf; 20.37 A uses 3 instead of √3.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b010","topic":"Electrical science","q":"An 18 kW balanced three-phase heater bank (unity power factor) is supplied at 400 V. What is the line current?","options":["25.98 A","45.00 A","78.26 A","15.00 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: IL = P / (√3 x VL)  (pf = 1)\n2. √3 x 400 = 692.8\n3. IL = 18 000 / 692.8 = 25.98 A\n4. Cross-check per phase: 6 kW per phase / 230 V = 26.09 A - near enough the same (the exact phase voltage is 400/√3 = 230.9 V).","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b011","topic":"Electrical science","q":"In a star-connected supply the line voltage is 400 V. What is the phase (line-to-neutral) voltage?","options":["230.9 V","692.8 V","133.3 V","200.0 V"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. In star: VL = √3 x VP, so VP = VL / √3\n2. VP = 400 / 1.732\n3. Result: VP = 230.9 V (nominally 230 V)\n4. Multiplying by √3 (692.8 V) is the wrong direction.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b012","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A 50 m length of 2.5 mm² copper conductor measures 0.37 Ω. Using proportion only, what would be the resistance of 100 m of 1.5 mm² copper conductor?","options":["0.44 Ω","0.74 Ω","0.22 Ω","1.23 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. R is proportional to length and inversely proportional to csa: R2 = R1 x (L2/L1) x (A1/A2)\n2. Length factor = 100/50 = 2; csa factor = 2.5/1.5 = 1.667\n3. R2 = 0.37 x 2 x 1.667 = 1.23 Ω\n4. 0.44 Ω inverts the csa ratio - a smaller conductor must have HIGHER resistance.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b013","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A copper conductor measures 0.50 Ω at 20 °C. Taking the temperature coefficient as 0.004 per °C, what is its resistance at its 70 °C operating temperature?","options":["0.64 Ω","0.52 Ω","0.42 Ω","0.60 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Formula: Rt = R20 x (1 + α x Δt)\n2. Temperature rise Δt = 70 - 20 = 50 °C\n3. Rt = 0.50 x (1 + 0.004 x 50) = 0.50 x 1.20 = 0.60 Ω\n4. This is where the 1.2 multiplier used for 70 °C PVC cables in Zs calculations comes from. 0.64 Ω wrongly uses 70 °C as the rise.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b014","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A 3 kW heater runs for 2.5 hours. Electricity costs 28p per kWh. What is the running cost?","options":["£2.10","£0.84","£21.00","£0.34"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Energy = P (kW) x t (h) = 3 x 2.5 = 7.5 kWh\n2. Cost = 7.5 x 28p = 210p\n3. Result: £2.10\n4. £0.84 forgets the time (3 x 28p).","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b015","topic":"Electrical science","q":"In a perfectly balanced three-phase, four-wire system with purely resistive loads, what current flows in the neutral?","options":["The same as one line current","Zero","√3 times one line current","Three times one line current"],"answer":1,"explain":"With equal currents 120° apart, the phasor sum of the three line currents is zero, so no neutral current flows. Neutral current appears when the phases are unbalanced (or with triplen harmonics).","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b016","topic":"Electrical science","q":"Power factor is defined as the ratio of:","options":["True power (W) to apparent power (VA)","Apparent power (VA) to true power (W)","Reactive power (var) to true power (W)","Voltage to current"],"answer":0,"explain":"pf = P / S = kW / kVA. It is 1 for purely resistive loads and less than 1 for inductive loads such as motors, which draw more current for the same useful power.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b017","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A 9.2 kW, 230 V electric shower is to be installed. What is the design current Ib?","options":["23.00 A","13.28 A","50.00 A","40.00 A"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Formula: Ib = P / V\n2. Substitute: Ib = 9200 / 230\n3. Result: Ib = 40.00 A\n4. Then choose In ≥ Ib (e.g. a 40 A device). 23 A wrongly uses 400 V; the √3 answer wrongly treats it as three-phase.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b018","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A circuit protected by a 32 A MCB runs in an ambient of 35 °C (Ca = 0.94) and is grouped with another circuit (Cg = 0.80). What minimum tabulated current-carrying capacity (It) is required?","options":["42.55 A","24.06 A","34.04 A","40.00 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: It ≥ In / (Ca x Cg)\n2. Ca x Cg = 0.94 x 0.80 = 0.752\n3. It ≥ 32 / 0.752 = 42.55 A\n4. Correction factors are DIVIDED into In, so It always comes out bigger than In. 24.06 A multiplies instead.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b019","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A radial circuit protected by a 20 A MCB passes through a loft where the cable is totally surrounded by thermal insulation for 1.2 m (Ci = 0.5). No other factors apply. What minimum It is required?","options":["40.00 A","10.00 A","20.00 A","27.59 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: It ≥ In / Ci\n2. Substitute: It ≥ 20 / 0.5\n3. Result: It ≥ 40.00 A\n4. Ci of 0.5 applies because the cable is totally surrounded by insulation for more than 0.5 m. 10 A multiplies; 27.59 A wrongly uses the BS 3036 factor.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b020","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A circuit is protected by a 30 A BS 3036 semi-enclosed (rewireable) fuse. No other correction factors apply. What minimum It is required?","options":["21.75 A","41.38 A","30.00 A","37.50 A"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Formula: It ≥ In / Cc, with Cc = 0.725 for BS 3036 fuses\n2. Substitute: It ≥ 30 / 0.725\n3. Result: It ≥ 41.38 A\n4. 21.75 A multiplies instead of divides - that would undersize the cable.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b021","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A 20 A BS 3036 fuse protects a cable in a 40 °C ambient (Ca = 0.87). What minimum It is required? (Cc for BS 3036 = 0.725)","options":["31.71 A","12.61 A","22.99 A","27.59 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: It ≥ In / (Ca x Cc)\n2. Ca x Cc = 0.87 x 0.725 = 0.6308\n3. It ≥ 20 / 0.6308 = 31.71 A\n4. The other answers either multiply (12.61 A) or apply only one of the two factors.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b022","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A 32 A MCB protects a cable where Ca = 0.94 and Cg = 0.80. For the installation method used, the tabulated ratings are: 2.5 mm² 27 A, 4 mm² 37 A, 6 mm² 47 A, 10 mm² 64 A. What is the smallest suitable cable?","options":["4 mm²","2.5 mm²","10 mm²","6 mm²"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. It ≥ In / (Ca x Cg) = 32 / (0.94 x 0.80) = 32 / 0.752\n2. It ≥ 42.55 A\n3. From the values given: 4 mm² (37 A) is too small; 6 mm² (47 A) is the first that is ≥ 42.55 A\n4. Choosing 4 mm² is the mistake of comparing In (32 A) directly with the table and ignoring the factors.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b023","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A 6 mm² cable has a tabulated rating It of 47 A. It is installed with Ca = 0.94 and Cg = 0.80 and protected by a 32 A MCB. What is its effective current-carrying capacity Iz, and is the device suitable?","options":["35.3 A - suitable, as Iz ≥ In","62.5 A - suitable, as Iz ≥ In","47.0 A - suitable, as Iz ≥ In","35.3 A - not suitable, as Iz < In"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: Iz = It x Ca x Cg (factors multiply when going from table to real capacity)\n2. Iz = 47 x 0.94 x 0.80 = 35.34 A\n3. Compare: 35.3 A ≥ 32 A, so In ≤ Iz is satisfied\n4. Dividing (62.5 A) is the reverse of the correct direction.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b024","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A 16 A MCB protects a circuit with Ca = 0.87, Cg = 0.70, and a section totally surrounded by thermal insulation for over 0.5 m (Ci = 0.5). What minimum It is required?","options":["4.87 A","52.55 A","26.27 A","36.78 A"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Formula: It ≥ In / (Ca x Cg x Ci)\n2. Ca x Cg x Ci = 0.87 x 0.70 x 0.5 = 0.3045\n3. It ≥ 16 / 0.3045 = 52.55 A\n4. 26.27 A forgets Ci; 36.78 A forgets Cg. Thermal insulation alone can double the cable size needed.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b025","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"Which relationship must be satisfied when selecting a protective device for overload protection of a cable?","options":["Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz","Iz ≤ In ≤ Ib","In ≤ Ib ≤ Iz","Ib ≤ Iz ≤ In"],"answer":0,"explain":"The device rating must be at least the design current (In ≥ Ib) and no greater than the cable's effective current-carrying capacity (In ≤ Iz).","ref":"Chapter 43","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b026","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"Why is a correction factor of 0.725 applied when a cable is protected by a BS 3036 semi-enclosed fuse?","options":["Because BS 3036 fuses run hotter than MCBs","Because the fuse needs about twice its rating to operate, and 1.45 ÷ 2 = 0.725 keeps the cable within its overload limit","Because the fuse element is 72.5% efficient","Because BS 3036 fuses have a lower breaking capacity"],"answer":1,"explain":"Overload protection requires the conventional operating current I2 ≤ 1.45 Iz. A BS 3036 fuse has a fusing factor of about 2, so the cable must be derated by 1.45/2 = 0.725.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b027","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"When does the correction factor Ci of 0.5 apply to a cable in thermal insulation?","options":["Whenever the cable touches thermal insulation on one side","Only when the cable passes through insulation for less than 0.1 m","When the cable is totally surrounded by thermal insulation for a length of more than 0.5 m","Only for cables in conduit in insulated walls"],"answer":2,"explain":"0.5 is the factor for a cable totally surrounded over more than 0.5 m. Shorter lengths totally surrounded use a less severe factor (Table 52.2); a cable touching insulation on one side uses the relevant reference installation method instead.","ref":"Reg 523.9","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b028","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A 30 m radial power circuit in 2.5 mm² cable (18 mV/A/m) has a design current of 20 A. What is the voltage drop, and does it comply with the 5% limit for a public supply?","options":["10.8 V - exceeds the 6.9 V limit","1.08 V - within the limit","108 V - exceeds the limit","10.8 V - within the 11.5 V limit"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Formula: VD = (mV/A/m x Ib x L) / 1000\n2. Substitute: VD = (18 x 20 x 30) / 1000 = 10800 / 1000\n3. Result: VD = 10.8 V (4.7%)\n4. Limit for 'other' circuits = 5% of 230 = 11.5 V, so it complies. 6.9 V is the lighting limit.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b029","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A lighting circuit is 35 m long in 1.5 mm² cable (29 mV/A/m), protected by a 10 A MCB, with a design current of 6 A. What is the voltage drop and does it comply?","options":["10.15 V - fails the 6.9 V limit","6.09 V - complies with the 6.9 V limit","6.09 V - fails the 3% limit","0.61 V - complies"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Formula: VD = (mV/A/m x Ib x L) / 1000 - use Ib, not In\n2. Substitute: VD = (29 x 6 x 35) / 1000 = 6090 / 1000\n3. Result: VD = 6.09 V (2.65%)\n4. Lighting limit is 3% of 230 = 6.9 V, so it complies. 10.15 V comes from wrongly using the 10 A device rating.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b030","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A 40 A shower circuit is wired in 6 mm² cable (7.3 mV/A/m). What is the maximum length that keeps voltage drop within the 5% limit (230 V supply)?","options":["39.4 m","23.6 m","68.5 m","3.94 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Allowed VD = 5% of 230 = 11.5 V\n2. Rearrange VD = (mV/A/m x Ib x L)/1000 → L = (VD x 1000) / (mV/A/m x Ib)\n3. L = (11.5 x 1000) / (7.3 x 40) = 11 500 / 292\n4. Result: L = 39.4 m. 23.6 m uses the 3% lighting limit; 68.5 m uses 5% of 400 V.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b031","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A three-phase 400 V circuit is 60 m long in 4 mm² cable with a three-phase voltage drop figure of 9.5 mV/A/m. Ib = 25 A. What is the voltage drop and does it meet the 5% limit?","options":["24.68 V - fails (limit 20 V)","14.25 V - fails (limit 11.5 V)","14.25 V - complies (limit 20 V)","8.23 V - complies (limit 20 V)"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. VD = (mV/A/m x Ib x L) / 1000 using the three-phase mV/A/m value\n2. VD = (9.5 x 25 x 60) / 1000 = 14.25 V\n3. Limit = 5% of 400 V = 20 V\n4. 14.25 V < 20 V so it complies. The three-phase mV/A/m value already includes √3 - do not multiply again, and do not judge against the 230 V limit.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b032","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A ring final circuit in 2.5 mm² cable (18 mV/A/m) has a total ring length of 60 m. A 26 A load is connected at the midpoint of the ring. What is the approximate voltage drop?","options":["28.08 V","7.02 V","14.04 V","3.51 V"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. At the midpoint the load is fed by two legs of 30 m in parallel, which is equivalent to one conductor of 60 / 4 = 15 m\n2. VD = (mV/A/m x Ib x L) / 1000 / 4 = (18 x 26 x 60) / 1000 / 4\n3. = 28.08 / 4\n4. Result: VD = 7.02 V. Forgetting to divide by 4 gives 28.08 V.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b033","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A 32 m radial in 4 mm² cable (11 mV/A/m) carries a design current of 28 A on a 230 V supply. What percentage voltage drop is this?","options":["4.29%","2.46%","9.86%","0.43%"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. VD = (11 x 28 x 32) / 1000 = 9.856 V\n2. Percentage = (VD / 230) x 100 = (9.856 / 230) x 100\n3. Result: 4.29%\n4. Within the 5% limit for power circuits. Dividing by 400 V is wrong for a single-phase circuit.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b034","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"For an installation supplied from a public low-voltage distribution system at 230 V, what are the usual voltage drop limits?","options":["Lighting 5% (11.5 V); other uses 3% (6.9 V)","Lighting 3% (6.9 V); other uses 5% (11.5 V)","Lighting 4% (9.2 V); other uses 4% (9.2 V)","Lighting 6% (13.8 V); other uses 8% (18.4 V)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Appendix 4 gives 3% for lighting and 5% for other uses where the supply is from a public LV network. At 230 V that is 6.9 V and 11.5 V.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b035","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"When calculating voltage drop for a radial circuit, which current should be used?","options":["The protective device rating In","The tabulated rating It","The design current Ib","The prospective fault current"],"answer":2,"explain":"Voltage drop depends on the current the circuit will actually carry, so it is calculated using Ib. Using In gives a pessimistic answer and can lead to oversizing.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b036","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"A fault current of 1000 A is cleared in 0.1 s. Using the adiabatic equation with k = 115 (70 °C PVC copper), what is the minimum csa of protective conductor?","options":["2.75 mm²","0.87 mm²","2.21 mm²","869.57 mm²"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: S = √(I²t) / k\n2. I²t = 1000² x 0.1 = 100 000 A²s\n3. √100 000 = 316.2\n4. S = 316.2 / 115 = 2.75 mm²\n5. 0.87 mm² comes from using I x t instead of √(I²t); forgetting the square root gives 869.57.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b037","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"A cpc incorporated in a 70 °C PVC cable (k = 115) must withstand a fault of 2000 A for 0.04 s. What is the minimum calculated csa and the smallest standard size?","options":["3.48 mm², so 4 mm² is needed","3.48 mm², so 2.5 mm² is adequate","0.70 mm², so 1.0 mm² is adequate","12.10 mm², so 16 mm² is needed"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. S = √(I²t) / k\n2. I²t = 2000² x 0.04 = 160 000 A²s; √160 000 = 400\n3. S = 400 / 115 = 3.48 mm²\n4. Always round UP to the next standard size: 2.5 mm² is below 3.48 mm², so 4 mm² is the minimum.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b038","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"A separate 70 °C thermoplastic-insulated copper cpc, not bunched with cables (k = 143), must carry 1500 A for 0.4 s. What minimum csa is required?","options":["6.63 mm² - use 10 mm²","6.63 mm² - use 6 mm²","8.25 mm² - use 10 mm²","4.20 mm² - use 6 mm²"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. S = √(I²t) / k\n2. I²t = 1500² x 0.4 = 900 000 A²s; √900 000 = 948.7\n3. S = 948.7 / 143 = 6.63 mm²\n4. 6 mm² is less than 6.63 mm², so round up to 10 mm². 8.25 mm² uses the wrong k (115).","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b039","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"What is the maximum energy let-through (I²t) that a 1.5 mm² copper cpc with k = 115 can withstand?","options":["172.5 A²s","29,756 A²s","19,838 A²s","46,010 A²s"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Rearrange S = √(I²t)/k → I²t = k²S²\n2. k²S² = 115² x 1.5² = 13 225 x 2.25\n3. Result: 29,756 A²s\n4. 172.5 is just k x S (no squaring); 46,010 uses k = 143.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b040","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"A circuit has Zs = 0.80 Ω at 230 V. The device curve shows it disconnects that fault current in 0.1 s. With k = 115, what is the minimum cpc csa?","options":["0.25 mm²","2.50 mm²","0.64 mm²","0.79 mm²"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Fault current If = Uo / Zs = 230 / 0.80 = 287.5 A\n2. I²t = 287.5² x 0.1 = 8266 A²s; √ = 90.92\n3. S = 90.92 / 115 = 0.79 mm²\n4. So a 1.0 mm² or larger cpc satisfies the adiabatic check.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b041","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"A 2.5 mm² copper conductor (k = 115) carries a fault current of 500 A. What is the maximum time it can carry this before exceeding its limiting temperature?","options":["0.57 s","0.51 s","0.17 s","0.33 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Rearrange S = √(I²t)/k → t = k²S² / I²\n2. k²S² = (115 x 2.5)² = 287.5² = 82656.25\n3. I² = 500² = 250 000\n4. t = 82656.25 / 250 000 = 0.33 s. The protective device must clear the fault faster than this.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b042","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"The value of k in the adiabatic equation depends on:","options":["The length of the circuit only","The protective device type only","The conductor material, its insulation, and the initial and final temperatures","The supply voltage and frequency"],"answer":2,"explain":"k is a factor that takes account of resistivity, heat capacity and the temperature limits of the conductor and its insulation. That is why a cpc inside a PVC cable (115) and a separate PVC cpc (143) have different values.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b043","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"Where is the disconnection time t in the adiabatic equation normally obtained from?","options":["Table 41.1 maximum disconnection times","It is always 0.4 s","It is always 5 s","The time/current characteristic of the protective device at the calculated fault current"],"answer":3,"explain":"You calculate the fault current (Uo / Zs) and read the actual operating time from the device's time/current curve (Appendix 3). Table 41.1 times are maximums for shock protection, not the actual clearance time.","ref":"Appendix 3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b044","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Using Cmin = 0.95 and Uo = 230 V, what is the maximum Zs for a Type B 32 A MCB (Ia = 5 x In)?","options":["1.44 Ω","0.68 Ω","6.83 Ω","1.37 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Ia = 5 x 32 = 160 A\n2. Formula: Zs = (Cmin x Uo) / Ia\n3. Zs = (0.95 x 230) / 160 = 218.5 / 160\n4. Result: Zs = 1.37 Ω. Leaving out Cmin gives 1.44 Ω; using 10In (Type C) gives 0.68 Ω.","ref":"Table 41.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b045","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"A radial in 2.5/1.5 mm² cable (R1+R2 = 19.51 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is 25 m long. Ze = 0.35 Ω. Using the 1.2 multiplier for 70 °C, what is the expected Zs?","options":["0.84 Ω","0.59 Ω","0.94 Ω","1.01 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. R1+R2 at 20 °C = (19.51 x 25) / 1000 = 0.488 Ω\n2. At 70 °C: 0.488 x 1.2 = 0.585 Ω\n3. Zs = Ze + (R1+R2) = 0.35 + 0.585 = 0.935 Ω\n4. Result: 0.94 Ω. Forgetting the 1.2 gives 0.84 Ω; the 1.2 applies to the cable only, NOT to Ze.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b046","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"The tabulated maximum Zs for a B32 MCB is 1.37 Ω. A reading of 1.15 Ω is measured at ambient temperature. Applying the 80% rule of thumb, what is the limit and the verdict?","options":["1.71 Ω - the reading is acceptable","1.37 Ω - the reading is acceptable","1.10 Ω - the reading is too high and should be investigated","1.10 Ω - the reading is acceptable"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Rule of thumb: measured Zs should not exceed 0.8 x tabulated max (allows for conductor temperature rise under load)\n2. 0.8 x 1.37 = 1.096 Ω\n3. Compare: 1.15 Ω > 1.10 Ω\n4. The reading fails the rule of thumb, so it needs investigating even though it is below 1.37 Ω.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b047","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"A B20 radial (max Zs 2.19 Ω) in 2.5/1.5 mm² cable (19.51 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is on a TN-S supply with Ze = 0.80 Ω. Using the 1.2 multiplier, what is the maximum length for shock protection?","options":["71.2 m","59.4 m","93.5 m","85.5 m"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Allowance for cable = Max Zs - Ze = 2.19 - 0.80 = 1.39 Ω (at 70 °C)\n2. Convert to 20 °C: 1.39 / 1.2 = 1.1583 Ω\n3. L = 1.1583 / 0.01951 Ω/m = 59.4 m\n4. Forgetting the 1.2 gives 71.2 m; ignoring Ze gives 93.5 m.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b048","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"A B6 lighting circuit in 1.0/1.0 mm² cable (36.20 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is 30 m long. Ze = 0.80 Ω. Using the 1.2 multiplier, what is the expected Zs?","options":["1.89 Ω","2.10 Ω","1.30 Ω","2.26 Ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. R1+R2 at 20 °C = (36.20 x 30) / 1000 = 1.086 Ω\n2. At 70 °C: 1.086 x 1.2 = 1.303 Ω\n3. Zs = 0.80 + 1.303 = 2.103 Ω\n4. Result: 2.10 Ω - well within the B6 limit of 7.28 Ω.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b049","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"A 40 A shower circuit in 6/2.5 mm² cable (R1+R2 = 10.49 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is 22 m long. Ze = 0.35 Ω. Using the 1.2 multiplier, what is the expected Zs?","options":["0.58 Ω","0.63 Ω","0.28 Ω","0.70 Ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. R1+R2 at 20 °C = (10.49 x 22) / 1000 = 0.2308 Ω\n2. At 70 °C: 0.2308 x 1.2 = 0.2769 Ω\n3. Zs = 0.35 + 0.2769 = 0.627 Ω\n4. Result: 0.63 Ω. Max for B40 = 0.95 x 230 / 200 = 1.09 Ω, so it complies.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b050","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Using Cmin = 0.95 and Uo = 230 V, what is the maximum Zs for a Type C 20 A MCB (Ia = 10 x In)?","options":["1.09 Ω","2.19 Ω","0.55 Ω","1.15 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Ia = 10 x 20 = 200 A\n2. Zs = (0.95 x 230) / 200 = 218.5 / 200\n3. Result: 1.09 Ω\n4. 2.19 Ω is the Type B value (5In); 0.55 Ω is Type D (20In).","ref":"Table 41.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b051","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Using Cmin = 0.95 and Uo = 230 V, what is the maximum Zs for a Type D 16 A MCB (Ia = 20 x In)?","options":["1.37 Ω","2.73 Ω","0.68 Ω","0.72 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Ia = 20 x 16 = 320 A\n2. Zs = (0.95 x 230) / 320 = 218.5 / 320\n3. Result: 0.68 Ω\n4. Type D needs a much lower Zs because it needs far more current to trip instantaneously.","ref":"Table 41.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b052","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"A B32 circuit has a measured Zs of 1.15 Ω. What earth fault current will flow (Uo = 230 V), and is it enough for instantaneous (magnetic) operation?","options":["200 A - no, it is below 320 A","264.5 A - yes, it exceeds 160 A","5.0 A - no, it is below 160 A","200 A - yes, it exceeds 160 A"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. If = Uo / Zs = 230 / 1.15 = 200 A\n2. B32 instantaneous trip current = 5 x 32 = 160 A\n3. 200 A > 160 A, so it will disconnect instantaneously\n4. 320 A would only apply to a Type C (10In).","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b053","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Supplementary bonding is being checked where the circuit is protected by a B16 MCB. Using R ≤ 50 V / Ia, with Ia taken as 5 x In, what is the maximum resistance allowed?","options":["3.125 Ω","0.313 Ω","0.625 Ω","1.600 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Ia = 5 x 16 = 80 A\n2. R ≤ 50 / Ia = 50 / 80\n3. Result: R ≤ 0.625 Ω\n4. 3.125 Ω wrongly uses In (16 A) instead of Ia; 0.313 Ω uses 10In (Type C); 1.600 Ω is the formula upside down.","ref":"Reg 415.2.2","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b054","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"How should Ze be measured at the origin of an installation?","options":["With all circuits switched on and bonding in place","Between line and neutral at the furthest socket","With the main switch on and the earthing conductor connected","With the installation isolated and the main earthing conductor disconnected from the MET"],"answer":3,"explain":"Disconnecting the earthing conductor removes parallel earth paths through the bonding, so you measure only the external loop. The installation must be isolated while the earthing conductor is off, and it must be reconnected before re-energising.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b055","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Which pair of maximum Ze values are commonly declared by DNOs for TN-C-S and TN-S supplies?","options":["TN-C-S 0.80 Ω; TN-S 0.35 Ω","TN-C-S 21 Ω; TN-S 0.80 Ω","TN-C-S 0.35 Ω; TN-S 0.80 Ω","TN-C-S 1.37 Ω; TN-S 2.19 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"0.35 Ω (TN-C-S) and 0.8 Ω (TN-S) are the typical maximum declared values. 21 Ω is the typical figure quoted for the DNO's contribution on a TT supply.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"EWA","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b056","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Why is the factor Cmin (0.95) included in the maximum Zs calculation?","options":["To allow for the supply voltage being lower than nominal, which reduces fault current","To allow for the cable heating up to 70 °C","To convert AC to DC values","To allow for instrument inaccuracy only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cmin accounts for the minimum supply voltage. Lower voltage means lower fault current, so the maximum Zs is reduced to make sure the device still disconnects in time. Conductor temperature is dealt with separately (the 1.2 multiplier / 80% rule).","ref":"Table 41.3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b057","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"In a TN system, what are the maximum disconnection times for a 230 V final circuit rated up to 32 A and for a distribution circuit?","options":["0.4 s for the final circuit; 5 s for the distribution circuit","0.2 s and 1 s","5 s and 0.4 s","0.4 s and 1 s"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 41.1 gives 0.4 s at 230 V for TN final circuits within its scope (up to 63 A with socket-outlets, or up to 32 A supplying only fixed equipment). Distribution circuits in a TN system are allowed 5 s. 0.2 s and 1 s are the corresponding TT values.","ref":"Table 41.1","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b058","topic":"Fault current","q":"Ze at the origin is measured as 0.25 Ω. What is the prospective earth fault current (Uo = 230 V)?","options":["0.0575 kA","1.60 kA","0.87 kA","0.92 kA"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Formula: PEFC = Uo / Ze\n2. PEFC = 230 / 0.25 = 920 A\n3. Result: 0.92 kA\n4. Using 400 V is for a line-line fault, not line-earth.","ref":"Fault current","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b059","topic":"Fault current","q":"The single-phase prospective short-circuit current at a three-phase board is 2.4 kA. Using the standard approximation, what is the three-phase PSCC?","options":["4.16 kA","4.8 kA","1.2 kA","7.2 kA"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Approximation: 3-phase PSCC ≈ 2 x single-phase PSCC\n2. 2 x 2.4 kA\n3. Result: 4.8 kA\n4. This simple doubling is the accepted rule of thumb; multiplying by √3 or 3 is incorrect for this method.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b060","topic":"Fault current","q":"The line-neutral loop impedance at the origin measures 0.16 Ω. What is the PSCC (Uo = 230 V)?","options":["2.50 kA","0.037 kA","1.44 kA","1.37 kA"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Formula: PSCC = Uo / Z(L-N)\n2. PSCC = 230 / 0.16 = 1437.5 A\n3. Result: 1.44 kA\n4. Record this on the schedule; the higher of PSCC and PEFC is the PFC.","ref":"Fault current","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b061","topic":"Fault current","q":"Supply line-neutral impedance is 0.25 Ω. A radial in 2.5/2.5 mm² cable (R1+Rn = 14.82 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is 20 m long. What is the maximum prospective short-circuit current at the end of the circuit? (Use 20 °C values for maximum fault current.)","options":["920 A","421 A","380 A","776 A"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. R1+Rn = (14.82 x 20) / 1000 = 0.2964 Ω\n2. Total Z = 0.25 + 0.2964 = 0.5464 Ω\n3. I = 230 / 0.5464 = 421 A\n4. For MAXIMUM fault current the conductors are taken cold (no 1.2 multiplier). Applying 1.2 gives the lower figure of 380 A.","ref":"Fault current","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b062","topic":"Fault current","q":"What value of prospective fault current should be recorded at the origin on test certificates?","options":["The lower of PSCC and PEFC","The average of PSCC and PEFC","Always the earth fault current","The greater of the prospective short-circuit current and the prospective earth fault current"],"answer":3,"explain":"The PFC recorded is the higher of the two, because the protective devices must have a breaking capacity that can cope with the worst-case fault.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b063","topic":"Fault current","q":"A domestic consumer unit to BS EN 61439-3 has a conditional short-circuit rating of 16 kA, but the MCBs fitted are marked 6000. The PFC is 3.2 kA. What is correct?","options":["The MCBs must be replaced with 10 kA devices","The PFC must be below 6 A","The consumer unit is only suitable up to 1.6 kA","The arrangement is acceptable - PFC is below both values"],"answer":3,"explain":"The '6000' marking is the MCB's short-circuit capacity (6 kA). 3.2 kA is within that and within the assembly's conditional rating, so the devices are adequate.","ref":"Fault current","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b064","topic":"TT systems","q":"For a TT installation protected by a 30 mA RCD, what is the maximum earth electrode resistance RA allowed by RA x IΔn ≤ 50 V?","options":["1667 Ω","1.67 Ω","200 Ω","150 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: RA ≤ 50 / IΔn\n2. IΔn = 30 mA = 0.03 A\n3. RA ≤ 50 / 0.03 = 1666.7 Ω\n4. Forgetting to convert mA to A gives 1.67 Ω. In practice aim much lower - values over 200 Ω may not be stable.","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b065","topic":"TT systems","q":"For a TT installation using a 100 mA RCD, what is the maximum RA from RA x IΔn ≤ 50 V?","options":["500 Ω","5000 Ω","0.5 Ω","1667 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. RA ≤ 50 / IΔn\n2. IΔn = 100 mA = 0.1 A\n3. RA ≤ 50 / 0.1 = 500 Ω\n4. 1667 Ω is the 30 mA figure.","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b066","topic":"TT systems","q":"An upfront time-delayed 300 mA RCD protects a TT installation. What is the maximum RA from RA x IΔn ≤ 50 V?","options":["15 Ω","500 Ω","167 Ω","1667 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. RA ≤ 50 / IΔn\n2. IΔn = 300 mA = 0.3 A\n3. RA ≤ 50 / 0.3 = 166.7 Ω (≈ 167 Ω)\n4. The larger the RCD rating, the lower the electrode resistance must be.","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b067","topic":"TT systems","q":"A TT electrode measures RA = 145 Ω and the installation is protected by a 100 mA RCD. What is the touch voltage figure RA x IΔn, and does it comply?","options":["14.5 V - does not comply","1450 V - does not comply","14.5 V - complies","145 V - does not comply"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. RA x IΔn = 145 x 0.1\n2. Result: 14.5 V\n3. 14.5 V ≤ 50 V, so it complies\n4. 145 Ω is also below 200 Ω, so the reading is likely to be stable.","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b068","topic":"TT systems","q":"An installation with a 300 mA S-type RCD has RA = 88 Ω. What is RA x IΔn and does it comply?","options":["26.4 V - does not comply","26400 V - does not comply","26.4 V - complies","293.3 V - does not comply"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. RA x IΔn = 88 x 0.3\n2. Result: 26.4 V\n3. 26.4 V ≤ 50 V, so it complies\n4. Dividing by IΔn (293.3) is the wrong way round.","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b069","topic":"TT systems","q":"A TT installation has RA = 60 Ω (ignore all other impedances). If a B32 MCB alone were relied on for fault protection, what earth fault current would flow and would it trip instantaneously?","options":["3.83 A - yes, above 32 A","13800 A - yes, above 160 A","3.83 A - yes, above 0.03 A","3.83 A - no, far below the 160 A needed"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. If = Uo / RA = 230 / 60 = 3.83 A\n2. Instantaneous trip for B32 = 5 x 32 = 160 A\n3. 3.83 A is far too small - the MCB would not operate in time (it may not operate at all)\n4. This is why TT installations rely on RCDs for fault protection.","ref":"TT systems","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b070","topic":"TT systems","q":"According to GN3, what should be borne in mind if a TT earth electrode resistance exceeds 200 Ω?","options":["It is automatically non-compliant with a 30 mA RCD","It means the electrode is too deep","It must be recorded as C1","The value may not be stable (e.g. soil drying or freezing) and should be improved"],"answer":3,"explain":"Although RA x IΔn ≤ 50 V would allow up to 1667 Ω with a 30 mA RCD, GN3 notes that readings above 200 Ω may not be stable over time due to changing ground conditions.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b071","topic":"TT systems","q":"In a TT system, what is the maximum disconnection time for a 230 V final circuit rated up to 32 A?","options":["0.2 s","0.4 s","1 s","5 s"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 41.1 gives 0.2 s at 230 V for TT final circuits within its scope (0.4 s is the TN value). Distribution circuits in TT systems are allowed 1 s.","ref":"Table 41.1","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b072","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"Ring final end-to-end readings are r1 = 0.60 Ω, rn = 0.60 Ω, r2 = 1.00 Ω. After cross-connecting line and neutral, what reading would you expect at each socket (L-N)?","options":["0.30 Ω","1.20 Ω","0.60 Ω","0.15 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: L-N reading ≈ (r1 + rn) / 4\n2. (0.60 + 0.60) / 4 = 1.20 / 4\n3. Result: 0.30 Ω at every socket (roughly the same all round)\n4. 1.20 Ω forgets to divide by 4.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b073","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"Ring end-to-end readings are r1 = 0.60 Ω and r2 = 1.00 Ω. With line and cpc cross-connected, what R1+R2 would you expect at each socket?","options":["1.60 Ω","0.80 Ω","0.40 Ω","0.30 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Formula: R1+R2 ≈ (r1 + r2) / 4\n2. (0.60 + 1.00) / 4 = 1.60 / 4\n3. Result: 0.40 Ω\n4. This value is the R1+R2 for each socket and is used with Ze to find Zs. The highest reading is recorded.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b074","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"A ring final in 2.5/1.5 mm² cable has a total length of 70 m. Given 2.5 mm² = 7.41 mΩ/m and 1.5 mm² = 12.10 mΩ/m (20 °C), what R1+R2 would you expect at each socket after cross-connection?","options":["1.37 Ω","0.68 Ω","0.41 Ω","0.34 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. r1 = 7.41 x 70 / 1000 = 0.519 Ω\n2. r2 = 12.10 x 70 / 1000 = 0.847 Ω\n3. R1+R2 = (r1 + r2) / 4 = (0.519 + 0.847) / 4 = 1.366 / 4\n4. Result: 0.34 Ω (for a test at about 20 °C, so no 1.2 multiplier).","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b075","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"A ring's end-to-end line reading r1 = 0.64 Ω. After cross-connection, the R1+R2 readings at the sockets are all about 0.42 Ω. What is the implied end-to-end cpc reading r2?","options":["1.04 Ω","1.68 Ω","0.26 Ω","0.20 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. R1+R2 = (r1 + r2) / 4 → r2 = 4 x (R1+R2) - r1\n2. r2 = 4 x 0.42 - 0.64 = 1.68 - 0.64\n3. Result: r2 = 1.04 Ω\n4. That is about 1.6 x r1, which is consistent with a 2.5/1.5 cable.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b076","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"End-to-end values for a ring, calculated at 20 °C, are r1 = 0.50 Ω and r2 = 0.84 Ω. Ze = 0.35 Ω. Using the 1.2 multiplier, what is the expected Zs at the sockets?","options":["0.75 Ω","0.68 Ω","1.96 Ω","1.15 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. R1+R2 = (0.50 + 0.84) / 4 = 0.335 Ω\n2. At 70 °C: 0.335 x 1.2 = 0.402 Ω\n3. Zs = 0.35 + 0.402 = 0.752 Ω\n4. Result: 0.75 Ω, well inside 1.37 Ω for a B32. Forgetting the ÷4 gives a wildly high answer.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b077","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"After cross-connecting a ring, every socket reads R1+R2 ≈ 0.30 Ω except one, which reads 0.38 Ω. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The ring has an interconnection","The ring is broken in the neutral","That socket is on an unfused spur adding about 0.08 Ω","The ends were not cross-connected correctly"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Readings on a correctly cross-connected ring should be substantially the same\n2. Extra = 0.38 - 0.30 = 0.08 Ω at one point only\n3. A single higher reading points to a spur (the extra is the spur's R1+R2) or a poor termination at that socket\n4. Incorrect cross-connection shows a steady rise towards the middle, not one outlier.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b078","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"Ring end-to-end readings: r1 = 0.60 Ω, rn = 0.85 Ω, r2 = 1.00 Ω. What does this indicate?","options":["Everything is normal","An interconnection in the cpc","The cable is 2.5/1.5 so this is expected","A likely high-resistance joint or loose connection in the neutral conductor"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. r1 and rn are the same csa so should be within 0.05 Ω of each other\n2. Difference = 0.85 - 0.60 = 0.25 Ω, far above 0.05 Ω\n3. That points to a loose/high-resistance neutral termination somewhere on the ring\n4. r2 being higher than r1 is expected because the cpc is smaller.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b079","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"When testing a ring, the L-N readings at the sockets rise steadily towards the middle of the ring and then fall again. What is the most likely cause?","options":["There is a spur on the midpoint socket","The cpc is broken","The ends of the ring were not cross-connected correctly (same ends joined)","The ring is healthy"],"answer":2,"explain":"When correctly cross-connected (line of one end to neutral of the other), every socket reads about the same. A reading that peaks mid-ring shows the ends have been joined like-to-like rather than crossed.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b080","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"What is the maximum difference GN3 expects between the end-to-end readings of r1 and rn on a ring of the same csa?","options":["0.5 Ω","0.005 Ω","0.2 Ω","0.05 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"Line and neutral are the same size and length, so their end-to-end resistances should be within 0.05 Ω of each other. A bigger difference suggests a poor connection.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b081","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"During step 1 of a ring test, the end-to-end reading of r1 is open circuit (OL) but rn and r2 read normally. What does this indicate?","options":["An interconnection in the ring","A short circuit L-N","A break in the line conductor somewhere in the ring","The ring is actually a radial with a spur"],"answer":2,"explain":"An open-circuit end-to-end reading means the conductor path is not continuous. Rn and r2 reading normally narrows it to the line conductor - check each socket's line terminations.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b082","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"The end-to-end readings on a 90 m ring are much LOWER than expected for its length, and the socket readings after cross-connection vary. What is the most likely fault?","options":["An interconnection (bridge) forming a figure-of-eight in the ring","A break in the cpc","A high-resistance joint","Incorrect polarity at one socket"],"answer":0,"explain":"A bridge between two points of a ring shortens the conductor path, so end-to-end readings are lower than the cable length suggests and socket readings are uneven. A break or high-resistance joint would give higher or open readings.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b083","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"Four circuits are tested separately at a board with IR values of 200 MΩ, 100 MΩ, 50 MΩ and 25 MΩ. What would the combined reading be if tested together?","options":["375.00 MΩ","93.75 MΩ","25.00 MΩ","13.33 MΩ"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. IR paths are in parallel: 1/RT = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3 + 1/R4\n2. 1/RT = 0.005 + 0.010 + 0.020 + 0.040 = 0.075\n3. RT = 1 / 0.075 = 13.33 MΩ\n4. The combined value is always LOWER than the lowest individual reading. 375 MΩ adds them as if in series.","ref":"Reg 643.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b084","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"Two circuits have insulation resistances of 3 MΩ and 6 MΩ. What is their combined insulation resistance?","options":["9.00 MΩ","4.50 MΩ","2.00 MΩ","0.50 MΩ"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Product over sum: RT = (3 x 6) / (3 + 6) = 18 / 9\n2. Result: 2.00 MΩ\n3. Check: 1/3 + 1/6 = 0.5; 1/0.5 = 2 MΩ\n4. 0.5 is 1/RT - you must take the reciprocal.","ref":"Reg 643.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b085","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"Ten identical circuits each have an insulation resistance of 20 MΩ. What is the combined value when tested together at the main switch?","options":["200.00 MΩ","2.00 MΩ","20.00 MΩ","0.50 MΩ"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. For n identical parallel paths: RT = R / n\n2. RT = 20 / 10\n3. Result: 2.00 MΩ\n4. This is why a whole-installation reading can look low even when every circuit is healthy.","ref":"Reg 643.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b086","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"A distribution board's overall IR reading is 1.2 MΩ. One circuit on its own reads 2 MΩ. What is the combined IR of all the other circuits?","options":["3.00 MΩ","0.80 MΩ","0.75 MΩ","3.20 MΩ"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. 1/RT = 1/R1 + 1/Rrest → 1/Rrest = 1/RT - 1/R1\n2. 1/Rrest = 1/1.2 - 1/2 = 0.8333 - 0.5 = 0.3333\n3. Rrest = 1 / 0.3333 = 3.00 MΩ\n4. Simple subtraction (2 - 1.2 = 0.8) does not work with parallel resistances.","ref":"Reg 643.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b087","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"What test voltage and minimum insulation resistance apply to a 230 V final circuit (Table 64)?","options":["250 V d.c.; 0.5 MΩ","1000 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ","500 V d.c.; 0.5 MΩ","500 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ"],"answer":3,"explain":"For circuits up to and including 500 V (other than SELV/PELV) the test is at 500 V d.c. and the minimum is 1.0 MΩ.","ref":"Table 64","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b088","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"What test voltage and minimum insulation resistance apply to a SELV or PELV circuit?","options":["250 V d.c.; 0.5 MΩ","500 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ","250 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ","100 V d.c.; 0.25 MΩ"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 64 requires 250 V d.c. with a minimum of 0.5 MΩ for SELV and PELV circuits.","ref":"Table 64","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b089","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"What test voltage and minimum insulation resistance apply to a circuit with a nominal voltage above 500 V (e.g. 690 V)?","options":["500 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ","1000 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ","1000 V d.c.; 2.0 MΩ","2500 V d.c.; 0.5 MΩ"],"answer":1,"explain":"Table 64: circuits above 500 V are tested at 1000 V d.c. with a minimum of 1.0 MΩ.","ref":"Table 64","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b090","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"An SPD in a circuit cannot practicably be disconnected for the insulation resistance test. What does BS 7671 allow?","options":["Omit the test on that circuit","Test at 1000 V d.c. to overcome the SPD","Test that circuit at 250 V d.c., with a minimum of 1 MΩ","Test at 250 V d.c. with a minimum of 0.5 MΩ"],"answer":2,"explain":"Where SPDs or other equipment likely to influence the test cannot be disconnected, the test voltage may be reduced to 250 V d.c., but the insulation resistance must still be at least 1 MΩ.","ref":"Reg 643.3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b091","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"An individual circuit on an EIC gives an IR reading of 1.6 MΩ at 500 V. It meets the Table 64 minimum. What does GN3 advise?","options":["Nothing - it passes","It should be investigated, as readings under 2 MΩ may indicate a latent defect","It must be retested at 1000 V","It must be coded C1"],"answer":1,"explain":"Although 1.6 MΩ exceeds 1 MΩ, GN3 notes that a reading below 2 MΩ on an individual circuit can indicate a latent defect, and each circuit should be tested separately to find it.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b092","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"Before an insulation resistance test on a circuit, why should lamps and electronic equipment be removed or disconnected?","options":["They increase the IR reading to infinity","They may be damaged by the test voltage and can give false low readings","The test is only valid with loads connected","It is only needed for SELV circuits"],"answer":1,"explain":"The d.c. test voltage can damage electronics, and connected loads give a path between L and N that produces a misleadingly low reading.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b093","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"A general-purpose 30 mA RCD to BS EN 61008 is tested at its rated residual current (IΔn). What is the maximum permitted operating time?","options":["40 ms","200 ms","500 ms","300 ms"],"answer":3,"explain":"At 1 x IΔn a general (non-delay) RCD to BS EN 61008/61009 must trip within 300 ms.","ref":"RCD & polarity testing","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b094","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"A 30 mA RCD provides additional protection. What test current is used for the 5 x IΔn test and what is the maximum trip time?","options":["150 mA; 300 ms","60 mA; 40 ms","30 mA; 40 ms","150 mA; 40 ms"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Test current = 5 x IΔn = 5 x 30 mA = 150 mA\n2. A general-type 30 mA RCD to BS EN 61008/61009 should trip within 40 ms at this current\n3. 300 ms is the limit at 1 x IΔn.\nNote: BS 7671:2018 no longer requires the 5 x IΔn test, but if you do it (many testers and GN3 still show it) 40 ms is the figure to compare against.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b095","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"A 30 mA RCD providing additional protection gives: 1 x IΔn = 280 ms; 5 x IΔn = 46 ms. What is the verdict?","options":["Pass - both are under 300 ms","Fail - the 1 x IΔn time exceeds 200 ms","Pass - 46 ms is under 50 ms","Fail - the 5 x IΔn time exceeds 40 ms"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. 1 x IΔn: 280 ms ≤ 300 ms - OK\n2. 5 x IΔn: 46 ms > 40 ms - not performing to its product standard (BS EN 61008/61009)\n3. BS 7671:2018 doesn't require the 5 x IΔn test, but a result like this shows the device is slow - investigate or replace it rather than rely on it for additional protection.","ref":"RCD & polarity testing","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b096","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"A time-delayed (S-type) RCD is tested at 1 x IΔn. What operating time range is acceptable?","options":["130 ms to 500 ms","0 to 40 ms","0 to 300 ms","300 ms to 1 s"],"answer":0,"explain":"S-type RCDs are deliberately delayed for discrimination with downstream RCDs; at IΔn they must operate no sooner than 130 ms and no later than 500 ms.","ref":"RCD & polarity testing","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b097","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"Why are RCD trip-time tests carried out at both 0° and 180°?","options":["To test both line and neutral poles separately","Operating time can differ depending on the point on the waveform; the longer time is recorded","To check the test button","Because the 180° test is for three-phase RCDs only"],"answer":1,"explain":"An RCD may respond differently depending on whether the test starts on the positive or negative half-cycle. Test at both and record the longer (worst) time.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b098","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"What does pressing an RCD's integral test button prove?","options":["That the trip time is within 40 ms","That the earth fault loop impedance is acceptable","That polarity is correct","That the mechanical tripping mechanism works - not the trip time or sensitivity"],"answer":3,"explain":"The test button creates an imbalance to check the mechanism operates. It does not verify operating times, which need an RCD tester.","ref":"RCD & polarity testing","level":"EWA","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b099","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"Which requirement is checked by the polarity test for single-pole devices?","options":["All switches are connected in the neutral","The cpc is connected to the neutral terminal","RCDs trip within 40 ms","Every fuse and single-pole control/protective device is connected in the line conductor only"],"answer":3,"explain":"Polarity testing confirms single-pole devices are in the line conductor so that switching off isolates the line from the equipment.","ref":"Reg 643.6","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b100","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"For an Edison-screw lampholder (other than E14 and E27 types to BS EN 60238), which conductor must connect to the centre contact?","options":["The neutral conductor","The protective conductor","The line conductor","Either, as it is a.c."],"answer":2,"explain":"The centre contact must be connected to line so the screw shell, which may be touched, is at neutral potential.","ref":"Reg 643.6","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b101","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"A common dead-test method for polarity on a radial circuit is to:","options":["Measure IR between L and N with lamps in","Link line to cpc at the board and measure between line and cpc at each point with a low-resistance ohmmeter","Use an RCD tester at each socket","Measure voltage between L and N at each socket"],"answer":1,"explain":"With L linked to cpc at the origin, a low reading between the line terminal and cpc at each point confirms polarity and gives R1+R2 at the same time. Remove the link after testing.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b102","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"For a new installation, which test is normally carried out FIRST in the GN3 sequence?","options":["Earth fault loop impedance","RCD operation","Insulation resistance","Continuity of protective conductors (including bonding)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Continuity of protective conductors comes first so that a sound earth path is proven before any other test (especially before energising).","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b103","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"Which is the correct order for these tests on a new installation?","options":["Insulation resistance → continuity → RCD → polarity → Zs","Zs → RCD → continuity → IR → polarity","Continuity → insulation resistance → polarity → earth fault loop impedance → RCD","Polarity → RCD → Zs → IR → continuity"],"answer":2,"explain":"Dead tests (continuity, insulation resistance, polarity) come before live tests (Zs, PFC, RCD operation), so faults are found before the installation is energised.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b104","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"Why must dead tests be completed before live tests?","options":["Because live tests are quicker","Because instruments can only test live after calibration","It does not matter which order","To prove the installation is safe to energise (no shorts, sound cpc, correct polarity) before connecting the supply"],"answer":3,"explain":"Energising an installation with a broken cpc, a short circuit or reversed polarity could be dangerous. The dead tests find those faults first.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b105","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"A low-resistance ohmmeter used for continuity testing should have which characteristics (BS EN 61557-4)?","options":["Test current of 1 mA at 500 V","Test current of 30 mA at 230 V","Test current of at least 200 mA and open-circuit voltage of 4 V to 24 V","Test current of 10 A at 400 V"],"answer":2,"explain":"Continuity testers must deliver at least 200 mA with an open-circuit voltage between 4 V and 24 V. 1 mA at the test voltage is the requirement for insulation testers.","ref":"BS EN 61557-4","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b106","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"An electrician forgets to null the test leads. The leads measure 0.12 Ω and the R1+R2 reading taken with them is 0.61 Ω. What is the true R1+R2?","options":["0.73 Ω","0.61 Ω","0.12 Ω","0.49 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. True value = instrument reading - lead resistance\n2. 0.61 - 0.12\n3. Result: 0.49 Ω\n4. Always null the leads (or subtract them) before recording continuity values.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b107","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"Using the fall-of-potential (three-terminal) method, where is the potential spike normally placed relative to the current spike?","options":["At the electrode under test","About 62% of the distance from the electrode under test to the current spike","At 10% of the distance","Beyond the current spike"],"answer":1,"explain":"The potential spike is placed at about 62% of the way to the current spike, and readings are repeated with it moved either side to confirm they are consistent.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b108","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"What does the 'wander lead' (R2) method measure?","options":["R1+R2 at each point","The resistance of the protective conductor (R2) only, from the MET to the point being tested","The earth electrode resistance","Line to neutral loop impedance"],"answer":1,"explain":"One end of a long lead goes to the MET, the other to the tester; the other test lead touches the cpc at each point. It is also used for bonding conductors. R1+R2 needs the line linked to cpc instead.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b109","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"Which standard covers insulation resistance testers?","options":["BS EN 61557-2","BS EN 61557-4","BS EN 61557-6","BS EN 61008"],"answer":0,"explain":"BS EN 61557-2 covers insulation resistance testers, -3 loop impedance, -4 continuity and -6 RCD testers.","ref":"Test sequence & instruments","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b110","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"Before starting dead testing, what is the correct way to confirm a circuit is dead?","options":["Use a neon screwdriver","Use an approved voltage indicator (to GS38), proved on a known source before and after the test","Switch off and trust the MCB","Use the insulation tester on its voltage range without proving it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Safe isolation requires an approved voltage indicator proved on a proving unit or known live source before and after checking the circuit is dead.","ref":"GS38","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b111","topic":"EICR coding","q":"On an EICR, what does classification code C1 mean?","options":["Potentially dangerous - urgent remedial action required","Danger present - risk of injury, immediate remedial action required","Improvement recommended","Further investigation required without delay"],"answer":1,"explain":"C1 is for danger that exists now; the person ordering the work should be told immediately and the danger made safe where possible.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b112","topic":"EICR coding","q":"What does classification code C2 mean?","options":["Danger present - immediate action required","Potentially dangerous - urgent remedial action required","Improvement recommended","No action required"],"answer":1,"explain":"C2 is for a defect that is not immediately dangerous but would become a danger if a further fault or event occurred.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b113","topic":"EICR coding","q":"An EICR contains only C3 observations. What is the overall assessment?","options":["Unsatisfactory","Satisfactory","Satisfactory only if the C3s are fixed within 28 days","It cannot be assessed"],"answer":1,"explain":"C3 means improvement recommended; it does not by itself make the installation unsatisfactory.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b114","topic":"EICR coding","q":"Which observations make the overall condition of an installation UNSATISFACTORY?","options":["Any C1, C2 or FI","Any C3","Only C1","Three or more C3s"],"answer":0,"explain":"The presence of any C1, C2 or FI code means the overall assessment must be unsatisfactory.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b115","topic":"EICR coding","q":"When should code FI be used?","options":["For any minor departure from the current edition","When the client refuses access to one room","Whenever a test result is borderline","When an observation needs further investigation that cannot be done during the inspection, but may reveal a dangerous or potentially dangerous condition"],"answer":3,"explain":"FI means further investigation required without delay. It flags a possible C1/C2 issue whose cause could not be determined within the extent of the inspection.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b116","topic":"EICR coding","q":"Blanks are missing from a consumer unit, leaving live busbars accessible to touch. What code is appropriate?","options":["C2","C1","C3","FI"],"answer":1,"explain":"Accessible live parts are an immediate danger. Code C1 and make safe (e.g. fit blanks) with the client's agreement before leaving.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b117","topic":"EICR coding","q":"There is no main protective bonding to the incoming metallic water service. What code is appropriate?","options":["C1","C3","C2","No code"],"answer":2,"explain":"Missing main bonding is potentially dangerous (it would become dangerous in the event of a fault), so C2 applies.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b118","topic":"EICR coding","q":"A circuit's measured Zs exceeds the maximum for its overcurrent device and there is no RCD protection. What code is appropriate?","options":["C3","No code","C2","FI"],"answer":2,"explain":"Fault protection is not assured - the device may not disconnect in the required time under fault conditions. This is potentially dangerous, so C2.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b119","topic":"EICR coding","q":"An installation was installed to an earlier edition of BS 7671 and does not comply with some current requirements, but is otherwise safe. How should this be treated?","options":["Non-compliance with the current edition alone does not mean it is unsafe; code only on the basis of safety","Every non-compliance must be coded C2","The installation must be marked unsatisfactory","Every non-compliance must be coded FI"],"answer":0,"explain":"An EICR assesses safety for continued use. A departure from the current edition is not automatically dangerous; use judgement and code according to the actual risk.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b120","topic":"EICR coding","q":"How should limitations on an EICR be dealt with?","options":["They do not need to be recorded","Agreed with the client before starting where possible, and recorded on the report with reasons","They are only recorded if the result is unsatisfactory","They must be coded C3"],"answer":1,"explain":"The extent of the inspection and any agreed or operational limitations (e.g. no access, sampling percentage) must be recorded so the reader knows what was and was not checked.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b121","topic":"EICR coding","q":"Which documents must accompany an EICR for it to be complete?","options":["An EIC and a minor works certificate","Only a photograph of the consumer unit","The original installation's EIC","A schedule of inspections and a schedule of test results"],"answer":3,"explain":"An EICR without its schedule of inspections and schedule of test results is incomplete.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b122","topic":"Fault finding","q":"A loose terminal has a resistance of 0.5 Ω and carries 20 A. How much heat (power) is produced at the joint?","options":["10 W","40 W","200 W","800 W"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Formula: P = I²R\n2. P = 20² x 0.5 = 400 x 0.5\n3. Result: 200 W - enough to scorch accessories and cause a fire\n4. 10 W is I x R (the volt drop).","ref":"Fault finding","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b123","topic":"Fault finding","q":"On a radial socket circuit, the expected R1+R2 at the last socket is 0.49 Ω, but the reading at the second socket is already 0.95 Ω while the first reads normally. What does this suggest?","options":["The circuit is healthy","An interconnection between two circuits","A high-resistance joint (at least 0.46 Ω) between the first and second sockets","A short circuit between line and cpc"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. On a radial, R1+R2 rises steadily along the run, so the second socket should read LESS than the 0.49 Ω expected at the last socket\n2. It reads 0.95 Ω, so there is at least 0.95 - 0.49 = 0.46 Ω of extra resistance (the true excess is a little more, as socket 2's normal value is below 0.49 Ω)\n3. Because the first socket reads normally, the added resistance lies between socket 1 and socket 2 - check those terminations and any joints.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b124","topic":"Fault finding","q":"Four computers each have 2.5 mA of protective conductor current and share one 30 mA RCD. Using the guideline that accumulated protective conductor current should not exceed 30% of IΔn, is this acceptable?","options":["Yes - 10 mA is below 30 mA","No - 10 mA exceeds the 9 mA limit","Yes - 2.5 mA is below 9 mA","No - 10 mA exceeds the 3 mA limit"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Total leakage = 4 x 2.5 = 10 mA\n2. Limit = 30% x 30 mA = 9 mA\n3. 10 mA > 9 mA, so nuisance tripping is likely - split the loads across RCDs/RCBOs.","ref":"Reg 531.3.2","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b125","topic":"Fault finding","q":"In a split-load board, an RCD trips whenever a lighting circuit on the OTHER RCD is switched on. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A borrowed neutral - the lighting circuit's neutral returns via a circuit on the other RCD","A high Ze","A faulty MCB on the lighting circuit","A broken cpc"],"answer":0,"explain":"Current goes out through one RCD and returns through another, so both see an imbalance and trip. Borrowed neutrals also leave 'isolated' circuits with a live neutral.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b126","topic":"Fault finding","q":"How could you confirm a suspected borrowed neutral between two circuits with the installation isolated?","options":["Test IR between line and cpc","Carry out an RCD test at 5 x IΔn","Measure Ze at the origin","Disconnect both neutrals from the neutral bar and test continuity between them"],"answer":3,"explain":"Once removed from the bar, the two neutrals should be separate. Continuity between them shows they are connected somewhere in the installation.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b127","topic":"Fault finding","q":"An RCD protecting several circuits trips as soon as a particular load is switched on, but MCBs do not operate. IR between neutral and earth on that circuit is 0.02 MΩ. What is the fault?","options":["A line-to-neutral short circuit","An open-circuit neutral","An overload","A neutral-to-earth fault"],"answer":3,"explain":"A low N-E insulation resistance lets load current return through the cpc instead of the neutral. The RCD sees the imbalance and trips, while the MCB sees no overcurrent.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b128","topic":"Fault finding","q":"An insulation resistance test between line and neutral on a radial reads 0.00 MΩ with all loads disconnected. What does this indicate?","options":["An open circuit in the line","A healthy circuit","A broken cpc","A short circuit between line and neutral"],"answer":3,"explain":"A near-zero IR between live conductors with loads removed means the conductors are in contact - e.g. a damaged cable or a trapped conductor in a box.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b129","topic":"Fault finding","q":"A continuity test of R1+R2 at a lighting point reads open circuit (OL), but the light works normally. What is the most likely fault?","options":["A short circuit L-N","Incorrect polarity","A high Ze","An open circuit in the cpc (e.g. a disconnected earth at a switch or ceiling rose)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The line conductor is clearly continuous because the light works, so an OL R1+R2 reading points to a broken or disconnected cpc - a dangerous fault.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b130","topic":"Fault finding","q":"A low IR reading is found on a long radial with several junction boxes. What is the efficient way to find the fault?","options":["Replace the whole circuit","Test at 1000 V to burn out the fault","Carry out an RCD test at each outlet","Split the circuit at a midpoint and test each half, then repeat on the faulty half"],"answer":3,"explain":"The half-split method quickly narrows down the faulty section, reducing the number of accessories that need to be opened up.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b131","topic":"Fault finding","q":"On a TN-C-S (PME) supply, what is the main danger if the supply PEN conductor becomes open circuit?","options":["Exposed and extraneous-conductive-parts connected to the MET can rise to a dangerous voltage","The RCD will trip and make everything safe","Only the lights will dim","The MCBs will trip on overload"],"answer":0,"explain":"The PEN carries both neutral and protective functions. If it breaks, load current can drive the earthed metalwork towards line voltage - an RCD will not necessarily detect this.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"p001","topic":"AM2: Safe isolation & risk assessment","q":"How many individual voltage tests make up the full test on an isolated three-phase and neutral distribution board?","options":["3","6","10","4"],"answer":2,"explain":"Three line-to-line, three line-to-neutral, three line-to-earth, plus neutral-to-earth = 10.","ref":"NET AM2/AM2E safe isolation procedure; HSE GS38","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p002","topic":"AM2: Safe isolation & risk assessment","q":"When must the voltage indicator be proved on a proving unit during safe isolation?","options":["Only before testing","Before and after testing for dead","Only after testing","Once at the start of the day"],"answer":1,"explain":"Proving after as well as before shows the tester was working throughout the test.","ref":"HSE GS38","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p003","topic":"AM2: Safe isolation & risk assessment","q":"Which instrument is appropriate for proving an isolated circuit dead?","options":["A two-pole voltage indicator with GS38-compliant shrouded probes and fused or current-limited leads","A non-contact voltage pen","A multimeter on its resistance range","An insulation resistance tester on 500 V"],"answer":0,"explain":"GS38 steers you to a purpose-made two-pole voltage indicator; non-contact pens and multimeters on the wrong range are not reliable for proving dead.","ref":"HSE GS38","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p004","topic":"AM2: Safe isolation & risk assessment","q":"After locking off the isolator for your work, where should the key be?","options":["Left in the lock so the assessor can find it","On top of the board","Handed to the next candidate","Kept on your person"],"answer":3,"explain":"Keeping the key on you prevents anyone else restoring the supply while you are working.","ref":"HSE HSG85","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p005","topic":"AM2: Safe isolation & risk assessment","q":"After isolating a three-phase board, all line tests read zero but N–E reads about 230 V. What should you do?","options":["Carry on – the lines are dead","Stop, treat it as live and report it – the neutral is live from another source","Disconnect the neutral and continue","Re-prove the tester and start work"],"answer":1,"explain":"A live neutral indicates a borrowed neutral, cross-connection or second source. It is not safe to work.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 13","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p006","topic":"AM2: Safe isolation & risk assessment","q":"In a written risk assessment, which entry belongs in the 'control measures' column?","options":["Electric shock from adjacent live bay","Candidate and assessor","Isolate, lock off and prove dead; cover adjacent live parts","Working at height"],"answer":2,"explain":"The other options are a hazard, the people at risk and another hazard. Controls describe what you will do about it.","ref":"EAWR 1989","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p007","topic":"AM2: Installation skills","q":"What is the recommended maximum length of untwisted pair when terminating Cat 5e/Cat 6 data cable?","options":["50 mm","25 mm","75 mm","About 13 mm"],"answer":3,"explain":"Keeping untwist to about 13 mm (half an inch) preserves the cable's performance; more degrades crosstalk performance.","ref":"ANSI/TIA-568 (13 mm maximum untwist, Cat 5e/6)","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p008","topic":"AM2: Installation skills","q":"How is the armour of an SWA cable normally connected to earth at a gland?","options":["Via an earth tag (banjo) under the gland with a fly lead to the earth terminal","By wrapping the armour around the earth terminal","By relying on the shroud","It isn't – armour is left floating"],"answer":0,"explain":"The banjo tag clamped under the gland, with a green/yellow fly lead to the earth terminal, gives a reliable earth connection.","ref":"BS 7671 Chapter 54","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p009","topic":"AM2: Installation skills","q":"What is the main purpose of a grommet or bush where a cable enters a metal box?","options":["To make the box waterproof","To earth the cable","To protect the cable from damage by sharp edges","To hold the cable in tension"],"answer":2,"explain":"Cable entries into metal must not damage insulation or sheaths; grommets and bushes protect from sharp edges.","ref":"BS 7671 Chapter 52","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p010","topic":"AM2: Installation skills","q":"A blue core of a multicore cable is used as a switch line. What is required?","options":["Nothing – blue is acceptable as a line","It must be identified brown (sleeve or tape) at its terminations","It must be replaced with a green/yellow core","It must be cut back and not used"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cores used as line conductors must be identified at terminations; brown sleeving is normal practice.","ref":"BS 7671 Table 51","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p011","topic":"AM2: Installation skills","q":"Using the T568B scheme, which colours are on pins 1 and 2?","options":["White/green and green","Blue and white/blue","White/orange and orange","White/brown and brown"],"answer":2,"explain":"T568B puts the orange pair on pins 1 and 2; T568A puts green on pins 1 and 2.","ref":"TIA-568 wiring schemes","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p012","topic":"AM2: Installation skills","q":"Why should FP200 cable supported on a ceiling be fixed with metal clips or ties rather than plastic alone?","options":["So the wiring system doesn't collapse prematurely in a fire","For better earth continuity","Because plastic clips damage the sheath","To reduce voltage drop"],"answer":0,"explain":"Wiring systems must be supported so they do not collapse early in a fire and endanger people escaping or firefighters.","ref":"BS 7671 Reg 521.10.202","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p013","topic":"AM2: Motor & control circuits","q":"In a DOL starter control circuit with one local and one remote station, how are the stop buttons connected?","options":["Normally open, in parallel","Normally closed, in series","Normally closed, in parallel","Normally open, in series"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stops must each be able to break the coil circuit, so they are NC in series.","ref":"Standard DOL control practice","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p014","topic":"AM2: Motor & control circuits","q":"How are the local and remote start buttons connected?","options":["Normally closed, in series","Normally closed, in parallel","Normally open, in series","Normally open, in parallel"],"answer":3,"explain":"Either start button closing must energise the coil, so they are NO in parallel.","ref":"Standard DOL control practice","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p015","topic":"AM2: Motor & control circuits","q":"A motor runs only while the start button is held, and stops when it is released. What is the most likely fault?","options":["The retaining (hold-in) contact is missing, open or wired as NC","The overload is set too low","Two phases are reversed","The stop button is wired NO"],"answer":0,"explain":"The retaining contact in parallel with the start button keeps the coil energised after start is released.","ref":"Standard DOL control practice","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p016","topic":"AM2: Motor & control circuits","q":"How is the direction of rotation of a three-phase induction motor reversed?","options":["Reverse the coil connections A1 and A2","Swap line and neutral","Swap any two line conductors (with the supply isolated)","Reverse the overload connections"],"answer":2,"explain":"Swapping any two lines reverses the phase sequence at the motor and so its rotation.","ref":"BS 7671 Reg 643.9 (phase sequence)","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p017","topic":"AM2: Motor & control circuits","q":"What should the thermal overload relay be set to?","options":["The rating of the upstream circuit-breaker","The full-load current on the motor rating plate","The maximum setting on the dial","Twice the full-load current"],"answer":1,"explain":"Setting to motor FLC gives protection against sustained overload without nuisance tripping on normal running.","ref":"Motor manufacturer data","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p018","topic":"AM2: Motor & control circuits","q":"In the usual three-wire arrangement, how many control cores (excluding cpc) run to a remote stop/start station?","options":["1","2","4","3"],"answer":3,"explain":"Core 1: feed from the local stop out to the remote stop. Core 2: remote stop output back to the starter to feed the starts and retaining contact (linked to the remote start at the station). Core 3: remote start output back to the coil side.","ref":"Standard DOL control practice","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p019","topic":"AM2: Lighting & heating controls","q":"Where is an intermediate switch connected in a multi-way lighting circuit?","options":["In the strappers between the two two-way switches","In the feed before the first two-way switch","In the switch line to the lamp","In the neutral to the lamp"],"answer":0,"explain":"The intermediate either passes the strappers straight through or crosses them over.","ref":"Standard lighting practice","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p020","topic":"AM2: Lighting & heating controls","q":"How many intermediate switches are needed for a light controlled from four positions?","options":["1","4","2","3"],"answer":2,"explain":"Two two-way switches at the ends plus (4 − 2) = 2 intermediates.","ref":"Standard lighting practice","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p021","topic":"AM2: Lighting & heating controls","q":"In an S-plan system, what normally switches the boiler and pump on?","options":["The programmer directly","The auxiliary (end) switch in either zone valve","The room thermostat directly","The cylinder thermostat directly"],"answer":1,"explain":"When a zone valve reaches fully open its auxiliary switch makes, sending a switched live to the boiler and pump.","ref":"Manufacturers' S-plan wiring diagrams","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p022","topic":"AM2: Lighting & heating controls","q":"In an S-plan system, what does the cylinder thermostat control?","options":["The boiler permanent live","The CH zone valve","The pump directly","The HW zone valve motor"],"answer":3,"explain":"HW on from the programmer passes through the cylinder stat to the HW valve motor.","ref":"Manufacturers' S-plan wiring diagrams","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p023","topic":"AM2: Lighting & heating controls","q":"When does a solar thermal differential controller normally run the solar pump?","options":["When the collector is warmer than the cylinder by the set differential","Whenever the programmer calls for hot water","Whenever the boiler is firing","Only when the cylinder stat is satisfied"],"answer":0,"explain":"It compares collector and cylinder sensors and runs the pump when there is useful heat to collect.","ref":"Solar controller manufacturer data","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p024","topic":"AM2: Lighting & heating controls","q":"How should a two-way and intermediate lighting circuit be functionally tested?","options":["Operate one switch only","Measure the lamp voltage","Operate every switch in every combination – the lamp must change state each time","Check continuity of the strappers only"],"answer":2,"explain":"A functional test proves the switching logic works from every control point in every state.","ref":"BS 7671 Reg 643.10","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p025","topic":"AM2: Inspection, testing & certification","q":"For a 230/400 V circuit, what is the insulation resistance test voltage and minimum acceptable value?","options":["250 V DC, 0.5 MΩ","500 V DC, 1 MΩ","1000 V DC, 1 MΩ","500 V DC, 0.5 MΩ"],"answer":1,"explain":"Table 64 requires 500 V DC and at least 1 MΩ for circuits up to 500 V (other than SELV/PELV).","ref":"BS 7671 Table 64","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p026","topic":"AM2: Inspection, testing & certification","q":"A ring final circuit is wired in 2.5 mm² singles with a 2.5 mm² cpc. What should the end-to-end readings show?","options":["r2 about 1.67 × r1","r2 about half r1","rn about twice r1","r1, rn and r2 all about the same"],"answer":3,"explain":"Same conductor size means the same resistance; the 1.67 ratio applies to 2.5/1.5 twin-and-earth.","ref":"IET Guidance Note 3","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p027","topic":"AM2: Inspection, testing & certification","q":"What is the maximum trip time for a general (non-delay) 30 mA RCD tested at its rated residual current?","options":["300 ms","40 ms","500 ms","1 s"],"answer":0,"explain":"A general non-delay RCD must operate within 300 ms at 1×IΔn.","ref":"BS 7671 Reg 643.8; IET Guidance Note 3","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p028","topic":"AM2: Inspection, testing & certification","q":"What is the Table 41.3 maximum Zs for a Type B 32 A circuit-breaker (0.4 s)?","options":["2.73 Ω","1.09 Ω","1.37 Ω","0.68 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1.37 Ω is the table value; compare measured readings against about 80% of it (about 1.1 Ω) to allow for conductor temperature.","ref":"BS 7671 Table 41.3","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p029","topic":"AM2: Inspection, testing & certification","q":"A box on the schedule of test results doesn't apply to that circuit. What should be written?","options":["Leave it blank","N/A","A dash","0"],"answer":1,"explain":"Blank boxes suggest a test was forgotten; N/A shows it was considered and not applicable.","ref":"BS 7671 Appendix 6","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p030","topic":"AM2: Inspection, testing & certification","q":"Which of these is a live test?","options":["Continuity of protective conductors","Insulation resistance","Ring final continuity","Earth fault loop impedance (Zs)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Zs is measured with the circuit energised; the others are dead tests done first.","ref":"IET Guidance Note 3","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p031","topic":"AM2: Fault diagnosis","q":"With lamps removed and switches on, insulation resistance between line and neutral on a lighting circuit reads 0.00 MΩ. What type of fault is this?","options":["Open circuit","High resistance","Short circuit","Mis-connection"],"answer":2,"explain":"Near-zero resistance between two live conductors with no load connected is a short circuit.","ref":"IET Guidance Note 3","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p032","topic":"AM2: Fault diagnosis","q":"A continuity test on the line conductor of a radial reads OL (out of range) from the board to the last socket. What type of fault is this?","options":["Open circuit","Short circuit","High resistance","Earth fault"],"answer":0,"explain":"No continuity where there should be a path is an open circuit.","ref":"IET Guidance Note 3","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p033","topic":"AM2: Fault diagnosis","q":"A radial circuit works and insulation resistance is fine, but R1+R2 is several times higher than expected for its length. What is the most likely fault?","options":["Short circuit","Open circuit","Mis-connection","High resistance joint"],"answer":3,"explain":"Continuity present but too high points to a loose or poor termination or damaged conductor.","ref":"IET Guidance Note 3","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p034","topic":"AM2: Fault diagnosis","q":"Ring final end-to-end readings: r1 = 0.50 Ω, rn = 0.50 Ω, r2 = OL. What is the fault?","options":["Short circuit between line and cpc","Open circuit on the cpc somewhere on the ring","High resistance on the line","Reversed polarity at one socket"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only the cpc end-to-end is open, so the cpc ring is broken between two points.","ref":"IET Guidance Note 3","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p035","topic":"AM2: Fault diagnosis","q":"What is the half-split technique?","options":["Testing every accessory from one end","Splitting the circuit into two new circuits","Testing at the midpoint and discarding the half that tests good, then repeating","Halving the test voltage"],"answer":2,"explain":"Halving the search area each time finds the fault location in the fewest tests.","ref":"Fault-finding practice","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p036","topic":"AM2: Fault diagnosis","q":"After rectifying a fault, what proves the repair?","options":["Repeating the relevant dead tests, then energising and functional testing","Visual inspection only","Pressing the RCD test button","Asking the assessor to check it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Repeat the test that found the fault plus anything the repair could affect, then prove it works live.","ref":"BS 7671 Chapter 64","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p037","topic":"AM2: Knowledge test & the day","q":"Where in BS 7671 are the maximum earth fault loop impedance tables for circuit-breakers?","options":["Chapter 54","Chapter 41","Chapter 64","Appendix 6"],"answer":1,"explain":"Chapter 41 (protection against electric shock) contains Tables 41.2 to 41.4.","ref":"BS 7671 Chapter 41","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p038","topic":"AM2: Knowledge test & the day","q":"What does Part 7 of BS 7671 cover?","options":["Definitions","Inspection and testing","Selection and erection","Special installations or locations"],"answer":3,"explain":"Part 7 covers locations such as bathrooms (701) and EV charging (722).","ref":"BS 7671 Part 7","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p039","topic":"AM2: Knowledge test & the day","q":"Where are the model forms such as the EIC found in BS 7671?","options":["Appendix 6","Appendix 4","Appendix 15","Part 2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Appendix 6 contains the model forms for certification and reporting.","ref":"BS 7671 Appendix 6","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p040","topic":"AM2: Knowledge test & the day","q":"Which section of BS 7671 deals with electric vehicle charging installations?","options":["Section 701","Section 705","Section 722","Section 710"],"answer":2,"explain":"Section 722 covers EV charging installations.","ref":"BS 7671 Section 722","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p041","topic":"AM2: Knowledge test & the day","q":"What is the best first action when you receive a practical task brief?","options":["Start the first job straight away to save time","Read it twice, the second time highlighting quantities, ratings and anything unusual","Ask the assessor to summarise it","Skim the drawing only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reading carefully up front prevents costly mistakes against the spec later.","ref":"Assessment practice","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"},{"id":"p042","topic":"AM2: Knowledge test & the day","q":"What is the maximum disconnection time for a 230 V final circuit rated up to 32 A in a TN system?","options":["5 s","0.2 s","1 s","0.4 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"Table 41.1 gives 0.4 s for 230 V TN final circuits within Reg 411.3.2.2 (up to 63 A with socket-outlets, up to 32 A fixed only); 0.2 s for TT.","ref":"BS 7671 Reg 411.3.2.2, Table 41.1","level":"AM2E","area":"Practical (AM2 / AM2E)"}];
window.BF_AM2E = {"updated":"2026-09","note":"Original checklist written for an experienced electrician (2391 holder) sitting AM2E / AM2E v1 at the end of the EWA route. Section codes follow NET's AM2E page (A1, A2-A6, B, C, D, E); the older all-versions Pre-Assessment Manual letters them A-F instead. Timings and knowledge-test format differ between NET sources and between AM2E and AM2E v1, so confirm the version you are registered on and its timings with NET or your centre.","sources":[{"title":"NET - AM2E Assessment for Experienced Workers (section list, timings, versions, fees, re-sit policy, booking evidence)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/am2e/"},{"title":"NET - AM2E V1.1 Full Candidate Checklist, July 2024 (hosted by South East AM2 Centres)","url":"https://southeastam2centres.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/NET-AM2E-V1.1-Full-Candidate-Checklist-July-24.pdf"},{"title":"NET - Pre-Assessment Manual: AM2 all versions (v2023.01) (tasks per section, what is provided, tools, common failure indicators, results process)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/NET-AM2-Pre-Assessment-Manual-v2023.01.pdf"},{"title":"NET - Pre-Assessment Manual index page","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/pre-assessment-manual/"},{"title":"NET - FAQs (objective marking, re-sit timing, 2-year validity, permitted tools and publications)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/faq/"},{"title":"NET - AM2 Candidate Self-Assessment Checklist, May 2025 (cable types, comparison only)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/NET-AM2-Candidate-Checklist-2505-WE2.pdf"}],"sections":[{"id":"s1","title":"A1 - Safe isolation and risk assessment","time":"45 min (NET AM2E page and V1.1 checklist); the manual adds about 10 min for reading and preparation","what":"Safe isolation of a distribution board in the correct order, a full set of voltage-indicator checks across every conductor combination, and a written risk assessment for the work. Most people treat this as the section where one missed step fails you. The marking is yes/no against set criteria.","items":["Can I do a written risk assessment for the installation task (hazards, who is at risk, controls, residual risk) without a template to copy from?","Can I identify the correct point of isolation from the drawings and labelling before touching anything?","Can I prove my approved voltage indicator (GS38: fused leads, shrouded probes, minimal exposed tip) on a proving unit or known live source before testing?","Can I carry out the full 10-point test on a TP&N board (L1-L2, L2-L3, L3-L1, each line to N, each line to E, N-E) without losing my place?","Do I test on the correct side of the isolator, with the switch in the correct position, and check that nothing is backfed?","Can I secure the isolation with a lock and keep the key on me, then fit a warning notice in the right place every time?","Do I re-prove the voltage indicator on the proving unit AFTER the dead test, every time, without being prompted?","Can I say out loud why each step is there? (The assessor may ask, and saying it keeps you in sequence.)","Do I check for separate or alternative supplies and stored energy before calling the circuit safe?","Can I get through the whole sequence calmly inside the time, with nothing left out?"],"fails":["Leaving out one or more of the 10 test combinations","Not proving the voltage indicator before AND after","Isolation not locked, key left in or lying around, or no warning notice","Testing with the isolator in the wrong position or on the wrong side","A thin or generic risk assessment that doesn't match the actual task"]},{"id":"s2","title":"A2-A6 - Composite installation","time":"10 h per NET AM2E page (and the manual's AM2S/E figure), but 10.5 h per AM2E V1.1 checklist - check with NET for your version. The manual adds about 15 min for preparation.","what":"A realistic installation built exactly to a specification and drawings. It includes a three-phase board and sub-circuits, several cable types and containment, terminated and working. It's split into sub-sections, and since 2021 you can re-sit a failed sub-section on its own. The V1.1 checklist lists: TP&N DB and protective devices; a 20A TP&N BS EN 60309 supply in XLPE SWA for an EV pillar; a 3-phase DOL motor circuit in SY with remote start/stop; lighting with 2-way, intermediate and key switches; a BS 1363 ring in singles; a CO detector safety circuit in FP200; Cat 5 data outlets; main bonding to gas and water; an S-plan heating/hot water system with a solar thermal element in heat-resistant flex; metal and PVC conduit.","items":["Can I read a full spec and drawing pack and pull out every dimension, height, cable type/size and device rating before I start fixing?","Can I bend steel and PVC conduit (90s, sets, bridge/saddle sets) to marked measurements, and cut, ream, thread and couple it neatly?","Can I install trunking and tray (or whatever containment the spec gives) level, secure and to the stated fixing centres?","Can I gland and terminate XLPE SWA properly (armour cut to length, gland fully tightened, banjo/earth tag, shroud) at both ends?","Can I dress, terminate and label a three-phase TP&N board with the correct MCB/RCBO/RCD types and ratings as specified, with line conductors on the right phases?","Can I wire a DOL starter for a 3-phase motor with remote start/stop (control circuit, holding contact, overload) and get the rotation right?","Can I wire two-way, intermediate and key-switch lighting in singles and T&E with correct switch-line identification (brown sleeving on switched conductors)?","Can I wire a ring final in singles in containment and keep both legs clearly identified at the board?","Can I install FP200-type cable with fire-rated clips/supports and terminate it correctly for a safety service (CO detector)?","Can I wire an S-plan system (programmer, room and cylinder stats, two 2-port valves, pump, boiler interlock, solar thermal controller) from a wiring centre diagram in heat-resistant flex?","Can I terminate Cat 5 data outlets to the correct standard (T568A/B, as specified) and keep ELV data properly segregated from LV?","Can I install main protective bonding to gas and water with the correct csa, BS 951 clamps and labels, in the right place relative to the meter/stopcock?","Is every termination tight, with the correct strip length (no bare copper showing, no insulation under the screw), and correct conductor colours and sleeving?","Can I plan my time across sub-sections so every circuit is finished and working, not just the parts I enjoy?","Do I leave the work area safe and tidy, with all covers and blanks fitted and nothing live that shouldn't be?"],"fails":["Not following the specification (wrong position or height, wrong cable type/size, wrong device), even when the work is safe","Wrong protective device type or rating, or undersized cable","Loose terminations or glands not fully tightened","Too much or too little insulation stripped; incorrect conductor identification or missing sleeving","Circuits that don't work when energised (motor control and S-plan are the classic ones)","Poor segregation of ELV data/safety circuits from LV","Running out of time and leaving a sub-section incomplete"]},{"id":"s3","title":"B - Inspection, testing and certification","time":"3.5 h (all NET sources); the manual adds about 20 min for preparation","what":"Inspect and test your own installation to GN3 methods, record results as you go, compare them against BS 7671 limits and complete an Electrical Installation Certificate with the schedule of circuit details and schedule of test results. Your 2391 background helps a lot here, but the assessor is marking method and paperwork as well as numbers.","items":["Can I do a methodical visual inspection and complete the inspection schedule honestly?","Can I check the test instrument (condition, leads, calibration in date), null the leads and select the right function and range for each test?","Can I test continuity of protective conductors, including main and any supplementary bonding, and record R1+R2 or R2 correctly?","Can I do the three-step ring final continuity test (end-to-end r1, rn, r2; cross-connect; check at every socket) and explain what the readings tell me?","Can I do insulation resistance at the correct test voltage, with sensitive equipment disconnected or accounted for and every switch and isolator operated so the whole circuit is tested?","Can I confirm polarity on every circuit, including switch lines and ES lampholder centre contacts where fitted?","Can I measure Ze and Zs safely and compare Zs against the BS 7671 tabulated maximums (using the 0.8 rule of thumb or corrected values)?","Can I measure and record PFC/PSCC for three-phase correctly (and know when the value needs doubling if the instrument measures phase to neutral only; check the current guidance)?","Can I check phase sequence with a rotation tester and confirm motor rotation?","Can I test RCD/RCBO operation (additional protection) at the required test current and record the trip time, and press the test button?","Can I functionally test every switch, isolator, interlock, motor control and heating control?","Can I fill in an EIC, schedule of circuit details and schedule of test results clearly and completely, with nothing left blank that should have an entry or N/A?","Do I record each result immediately, not from memory at the end?","Do I reconnect and re-tighten everything I disconnected for testing and refit all covers?"],"fails":["Instrument not checked, leads not nulled, or wrong range or test voltage","Not following GN3 methods or sequence","Results not recorded as the test was done","Not checking results against BS 7671 limits","Incomplete or inaccurate EIC or schedules","Not operating all switches or isolators during insulation resistance or continuity, so parts of the circuit go untested","Loose connections left after disconnecting for tests"]},{"id":"s4","title":"C - Safe isolation of circuits","time":"30 min (NET AM2E page and V1.1 checklist); the manual adds about 5 min for preparation","what":"Three separate isolations while other circuits stay live: single-phase equipment, three-phase equipment, and a whole distribution board (the one used for fault diagnosis). You must isolate only what is needed and prove it dead every time.","items":["Can I pick out and isolate a single piece of single-phase equipment without killing other circuits, and prove it dead at the point of work?","Can I isolate a three-phase load and prove all phases, neutral and earth combinations dead at the equipment?","Can I isolate a complete DB and do the full 10-point test?","Do I prove the tester before and after on every one of the three isolations, not just the first?","Do I lock off and fit notices on every isolation, keeping each key on me?","Can I tell a functional switch from a proper isolating device, and choose the right one?","Can I spot and deal with the possibility of a second supply, borrowed neutral or backfeed?"],"fails":["Getting lazy on the second or third isolation and skipping prove-test-prove","Isolating more than needed, or using a device that isn't suitable for isolation","Testing only L-N on three-phase equipment","No lock or notice on one of the three tasks"]},{"id":"s5","title":"D - Fault diagnosis and rectification","time":"2 h (all NET sources); the manual adds about 5 min for preparation","what":"You're given symptom information for faults on a rig. For each one, work out the fault type (open circuit, short circuit, high resistance or mis-connection), exactly where it is (between which two points and on which conductor), and how you'd fix it and prove the repair. How many faults you get isn't published - check with NET or your centre.","items":["Can I read symptom sheets and circuit diagrams and write a short plan before testing?","Can I safely isolate and prove dead before any dead testing (the safe-isolation rules apply here too)?","Can I choose the right instrument and range (low-ohm continuity vs insulation resistance) for each suspected fault type?","Can I narrow a fault down by splitting the circuit at accessible points and recording readings as I go?","Can I tell the four fault types apart from readings: open (infinite/very high continuity), short (near-zero IR between conductors), high resistance (a raised but finite reading), mis-connection (wrong polarity or crossed conductors)?","Can I find faults on lighting (2-way/intermediate), rings (including an interconnection or a broken leg), motor control circuits and heating controls?","Can I state the location precisely: 'between point X and point Y on the CPC', not 'somewhere in the lighting'?","Can I write down a sensible repair method and the tests I'd do afterwards to prove it?","Can I leave the rig as I found it: covers back, connections tight, isolation removed properly only when instructed?"],"fails":["Vague fault locations (not naming both points and the conductor)","Naming the wrong fault type (for example calling high resistance an open circuit)","Doing live work or dead testing without proper isolation","Guessing instead of testing, and running out of time","No verification tests stated after rectification"]},{"id":"s6","title":"E - Assessment of applied knowledge (online)","time":"Check with NET: the NET AM2E page says 1 h / 30 questions; the AM2E V1.1 checklist says 1.5 h / 40 questions (AM2S v1 is 1.5 h / 45 questions)","what":"A computer-based multiple-choice test on health and safety, BS 7671, Building Regulations (including Part P) and inspection, testing and fault finding. The provided publications are open-book, and no other documents are allowed.","items":["Can I find things quickly in BS 7671: Part 4 disconnection times, Appendix 3 max Zs tables, Appendix 4 cable ratings and correction factors, Part 7 special locations, Part 6 test values?","Can I find answers fast in the IET On-Site Guide (cable selection tables, standard final circuits, max lengths)?","Can I find things in GN3 (test methods, minimum IR values, test voltages, RCD test requirements)?","Do I know my way around the Electrician's Guide to the Building Regulations (Part P notifiable work, Parts B, E, F, L, M and how they affect electrical work)? Check the Welsh versus English Approved Document differences.","Can I answer core H&S law questions (HASAWA, EAWR 1989, CDM, RIDDOR, COSHH, working at height, safe isolation principles)?","Can I do quick calcs: Ib/In/Iz selection with correction factors, voltage drop, Zs from R1+R2 plus Ze, the 0.8 rule for Zs?","Have I done timed practice multiple-choice papers against the clock?","Do I know which BS 7671 edition and amendment the centre supplies? (FAQ says edition-neutral with A2 incorporated since 2022; check with NET whether A3/A4 is now used.)"],"fails":["Not being able to find the right table or regulation in BS 7671 fast enough","Weak Building Regulations knowledge (commonly flagged by NET)","Spending too long on one question and leaving others unanswered"]}],"tips":["Confirm which version you're on (AM2E if EWA registered before 03/09/2023, AM2E v1 from 04/09/2023). Timings, checklist and fee differ.","Before booking, get the AM2E checklist signed by you, your training provider and employer/nominated person (valid for 6 months). Have your EWQ certificate, learner history and skills scan front page ready.","Download and read the current NET Pre-Assessment Manual for your version from the NET site. Everything provided on the day (drawings, forms) follows it.","Your own tools: only insulated side cutters, strippers and pliers (BS EN IEC 60900 recommended), in good condition. The centre supplies everything else and you must use theirs.","Bring safety boots and suitable workwear. Check with your centre whether they provide other PPE (glasses, gloves).","Phones and smartwatches are collected at the start of each day. Plan for no clock on your wrist and use the centre clock.","Publications provided: BS 7671, GN3, On-Site Guide, Electrician's Guide to Building Regulations. Don't take the Candidate Manual out of the assessment area (disqualification).","NET gives a total of about 17 h 45 min, usually spread over about 2.5 days (check the exact schedule with your centre). Sleep well and eat properly; the install day is long.","Read the whole spec first and highlight every dimension, cable type and device rating. Most installation fails are 'didn't follow the spec', not unsafe work.","Treat safe isolation as automatic: prove, test, prove, lock, notice. Every time, in every section, including fault finding.","Record test results as you take them, and fill every box on the certificate or put N/A.","Practise conduit bending, the DOL starter and S-plan wiring before the day. These are the areas experienced domestic or commercial sparks most often haven't done recently.","There's no feedback on the day. Results come by email about 5 working days after marking, with a general statement if you fail.","Re-sits: at least 10 days later (NET suggests 3+ weeks with training), all re-sit sections in one booking on one date, and passed sections expire after 2 years. You can re-sit individual failed installation sub-sections.","If you need reasonable adjustments, apply in advance under NET's Special Considerations and Reasonable Adjustments policy."]};
window.BF_AM2 = {"updated":"2026-09","name":"AM2 / AM2S","note":"AM2 is for Level 3 NVQ candidates (C&G 2357 / EAL 1605) and must be the last unit taken; AM2S is the end-point assessment for Installation & Maintenance Electrician apprentices who started before Sept 2023, and AM2S v1 is for those who registered from Sept 2023 (longer planning, composite and knowledge sections, plus conduit and smart heating). Confirm with your centre exactly which version and board layout you are booked on, the knowledge-test pass mark, current fees, and what ID/PPE to bring.","sources":[{"title":"NET – AM2 assessment (who it's for, sections, fees)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/am2/"},{"title":"NET – AM2S assessment (pre-Sept 2023 apprentices)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/am2s/"},{"title":"NET – AM2S v1 assessment (apprentices from Sept 2023)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/am2s-v1/"},{"title":"NET – AM2E assessment (experienced workers)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/am2e/"},{"title":"NET – AM2 versions explained","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/am2-versions/"},{"title":"NET – Pre-Assessment Manual hub","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/pre-assessment-manual/"},{"title":"NET – AM2 Pre-Assessment Manual (all versions, v2023.01) PDF","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/NET-AM2-Pre-Assessment-Manual-v2023.01.pdf"},{"title":"NET – AM2S v1 Pre-Assessment Manual PDF","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/NET-AM2S-v1-Pre-Assessment-Manual-2503.pdf"},{"title":"NET – AM2 Candidate Checklist PDF","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/NET-AM2-Candidate-Checklist-2505-WE2.pdf"},{"title":"NET – AM2S v1 Candidate Checklist PDF","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/NET-AM2S-v1-Candidate-Checklist-25-12-WE2.pdf"},{"title":"NET – Blank BS 7671 certificate templates (practice forms)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/NET-18th-Ed-BS-7671-Certs-Blank-V1022-2.pdf"},{"title":"NET – Top Tips videos","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/videos/"}],"sections":[{"id":"s1","title":"A1 – Safe isolation and risk assessment (AM2S v1: safe working practices and planning)","time":"45 min (AM2 / AM2S) or 1 hr (AM2S v1), plus about 10 min reading/prep time","what":"Fully observed safe isolation of the TP&N distribution board in your bay, then a written risk assessment for the installation task. AM2S v1 adds a written work plan for how you will tackle the composite installation.","items":["Can I recite and perform my safe isolation sequence in the same order every time without prompting?","Can I tell the person responsible/customer before isolating?","Can I identify the correct isolator, switch off, lock off and keep the key on me?","Can I fit a warning notice at the point of isolation?","Can I prove my voltage indicator on a known source (proving unit) before testing?","Can I carry out the full 10-point test on a three-phase board: L1-L2, L1-L3, L2-L3, each line to N, each line to E, and N-E?","Can I re-prove the voltage indicator on the proving unit after testing dead?","Can I check for and deal with any secondary or alternative supplies?","Can I write a risk assessment that names hazards, who is at risk and the controls, in the format the centre gives me?","(AM2S v1) Can I write a clear, sequenced work plan for the composite installation within the time?","Do I know the centre's emergency and first-aid arrangements from the induction?"],"fails":["Steps out of order or a step missed (e.g. not re-proving the tester afterwards)","Not proving the tester before use","Incomplete 10-point test (missing N-E or a line-to-line combination)","Key left in the lock or not kept secure","No warning notice fitted","Risk assessment too generic or missing people at risk / control measures","Using a multimeter or non-approved tester rather than a GS38 voltage indicator"]},{"id":"s2","title":"A2–A5 (AM2) / A2–A6 (AM2S, AM2S v1) – Composite installation","time":"8 hr 30 min (AM2), 10 hr (AM2S), 10 hr 30 min (AM2S v1), plus about 15 min prep; usually split across days","what":"Install, terminate and connect a small commercial-style installation from the Candidate Manual drawings and specification, fed from a TP&N distribution board. Typical content: two-way/intermediate lighting, 13 A ring final, fire/CO detector circuit in FP200, Cat 5/data outlets, TP&N supply in XLPE SWA, 3-phase DOL motor with remote stop/start in SY, main protective bonding to gas and water, and a central heating control scheme (AM2: S-plan with solar thermal; AM2S v1: smart heating/hot water). AM2 excludes containment; AM2S/AM2S v1 include bending and installing conduit (AM2S v1: both metal and PVC), and v1 adds an emergency lighting supply and ultra-flex with data. Exact layout: check with your centre.","items":["Can I read the drawings and spec and set out every accessory to the stated dimensions (plumb, level, within tolerance)?","Can I choose the right protective device type and rating for each circuit from the spec and fit them in the TP&N board in the right ways?","Can I prepare and terminate PVC singles, PVC/PVC twin & cpc, SY flex, heat-resistant flex and FP200 neatly with correct fixings/clips?","Can I gland off XLPE SWA properly (armour earthed, shroud fitted, gland tight, correct cut-back)?","Can I terminate Cat 5/data cable to outlets to the correct wiring standard and keep it segregated from LV cables?","Can I wire two-way and intermediate switching correctly, with correct sleeving of switched/borrowed conductors?","Can I wire a 3-phase DOL starter including remote stop/start (stops in series, starts in parallel) and get the motor running the right way?","Can I wire the heating control scheme (motorised valves, programmer/thermostats, wiring centre) from the schematic?","Can I fit main protective bonding clamps (BS 951) with correct labels and conductor size to gas and water?","(AM2S / v1) Can I bend and install conduit (sets, offsets, bends) to measurement, with bushes/couplers secure and no burrs? v1: both steel and PVC","Can I strip insulation so no bare copper shows outside the terminal but no insulation is trapped under the screw?","Can I tighten every terminal, gland and clamp correctly and give each a tug test?","Can I identify and sleeve every conductor correctly (brown/black/grey/blue, green-yellow, L1/L2/L3)?","Can I keep a steady pace with the timesheet and finish with time to check my own work?","Can I functionally check each circuit works as the spec describes (switching, motor, heating logic)?"],"fails":["Not following the specification exactly (wrong cable type, route, height or accessory) even if it would be safe","Wrong protective device type/rating or cable size","Loose terminations, glands or bonding clamps","Too much insulation stripped (copper showing) or insulation under terminals","Extra-low voltage/data not segregated from LV cables","Circuits that do not work, or motor/heating logic wired incorrectly","Missing or incorrect conductor identification/sleeving","Running out of time because of slow start or rework"]},{"id":"s3","title":"B – Inspection, testing and certification","time":"3 hr 30 min, plus about 20 min prep","what":"Visual inspection and full initial verification of your installation, under direct observation, following IET Guidance Note 3, then completion of an Electrical Installation Certificate, schedule of circuit details and schedule of test results, with results checked against BS 7671. Faults you find may be corrected within the time, and all connections must be put back safely.","items":["Can I safely isolate before dead testing and take account of voltage-sensitive equipment (disconnect or protect it)?","Can I carry out and record a methodical visual inspection using the inspection schedule?","Can I check my test instrument, leads and fuses and select the correct range/function for each test?","Can I test continuity of protective conductors, including main and supplementary bonding (R1+R2 or R2 method), and null my leads?","Can I do the three-step ring final continuity test (end-to-end r1, rn, r2, then cross-connected readings at each socket)?","Can I carry out insulation resistance at the correct test voltage, knowing what to disconnect and the minimum acceptable values?","Can I confirm polarity, including switch-lines on lighting and centre contacts of ES lampholders where fitted?","Can I measure Ze and Zs safely and compare Zs with the BS 7671 maximum (applying the 0.8 rule of thumb where appropriate)?","Can I measure prospective fault current on a three-phase supply and record the correct (highest) value?","Can I check phase sequence/rotation with the right instrument?","Can I test RCD/additional protection and record the result? (listed in the manual; the v1 checklist omits it – check with your centre)","Can I functionally test every circuit, switch and control, including operating every lighting switch?","Can I fill in the EIC, schedule of circuit details and schedule of test results fully and legibly with no blanks?","Can I spot a result that does not comply and state what it means?","Can I reinstate every connection I disturbed and tighten terminals afterwards?"],"fails":["Not following Guidance Note 3 methods or order of tests","Wrong instrument set-up or range, or leads not nulled","Results not recorded, or recorded in the wrong box/units","Not checking results against BS 7671 limits","Incomplete or inaccurate certificate and schedules","Functional testing skipped, or lighting switches not all operated","Loose terminations or disconnected items left after testing","Testing live without a safe method, or not isolating before dead tests"]},{"id":"s4","title":"C – Safe isolation of circuits","time":"30 min, plus about 5 min prep","what":"Three fully observed isolation tasks: isolate a single-phase circuit to replace equipment while everything else stays live; isolate a three-phase circuit to replace equipment while everything else stays live; and isolate the distribution board in the fault-diagnosis bay.","items":["Can I pick out and isolate only the circuit I need, leaving all others live?","Can I lock off an individual MCB/RCBO with the right locking device and keep the key?","Can I post the warning notice at the point of isolation?","Can I prove the tester, test dead at the point of work on all conductors, and re-prove the tester?","Can I do the full 10-point test for the three-phase circuit and the board?","Can I check that downstream switches or other points of isolation do not leave anything live?","Can I tell the customer/responsible person before isolating?","Can I do all three tasks in the same disciplined order under time pressure?"],"fails":["Isolating more than the circuit being worked on","Sequence out of order or a step missed","Not proving the tester before and after","Key not secured or no warning notice","Not checking beyond switches/other isolation points","Missing combinations in the 10-point test"]},{"id":"s5","title":"D – Fault diagnosis and rectification","time":"2 hr, plus about 5 min prep","what":"Using the symptom information provided, find faults placed on circuits in the fault-diagnosis bay and record, for each one, the fault type (short circuit, open circuit, high resistance, mis-connection), its exact location, how you would put it right and how you would prove the repair. The number of faults is not published – check with your centre.","items":["Can I isolate the fault bay safely before starting and re-isolate between tests where needed?","Can I choose the right instrument (continuity, IR, voltage indicator) for each symptom?","Can I plan a logical approach from the symptom rather than testing at random (half-split, compare with drawings)?","Can I classify each fault correctly as short circuit, open circuit, high resistance or mis-connection?","Can I locate the fault precisely: between which two points and on which conductor(s)?","Can I write a clear rectification method (e.g. replace the cable between X and Y, re-terminate at Z)?","Can I state how I would prove the repair (which tests, what result I'd expect)?","Can I recognise typical faults on lighting, rings, radials, 3-phase motor control and heating circuits?","Can I record everything neatly on the fault sheet within the time?"],"fails":["Vague location (e.g. 'in the lighting circuit' instead of between two named points on a named conductor)","Wrong or imprecise fault type","No rectification method given, or it is incomplete","No verification/proving method stated","Unsafe working or poor instrument use in the bay"]},{"id":"s6","title":"E – Assessment of applied knowledge","time":"AM2 / AM2S: 1 hr, 30 questions. AM2S v1: 1 hr 30 min, 45 questions. Plus about 5 min prep","what":"Online multiple-choice test. AM2/AM2S: health and safety, BS 7671 and Building Regulations. AM2S v1 widens this to inspection and testing, installation practice, fault diagnosis, design and planning, and behaviours. The centre supplies BS 7671, GN3, the On-Site Guide and the IET Electrician's Guide to the Building Regulations; nothing else is allowed. Pass mark not published – check with your centre.","items":["Can I find things in BS 7671 quickly using the index, Part 2 definitions and the Appendix 3 max Zs tables?","Do I know the current amendment of BS 7671 and where key regs sit (Parts 4, 5, 6, 7 special locations)?","Can I use the On-Site Guide for cable sizing, installation methods and max lengths?","Can I use GN3 for test methods, IR test voltages/minimum values and certification guidance?","Can I find Building Regs content (Part P notifiable work, Parts B, E, L, M) in the Electrician's Guide?","Do I know the main H&S law: HASAWA, EAWR, CDM, COSHH, RIDDOR, PUWER, working at height?","Can I do voltage drop, Zs, and simple Ib/In/Iz calculations quickly by hand?","(v1) Am I comfortable with design/planning, fault finding theory and customer/behaviour questions?","Can I pace myself (about 2 min per question) and flag hard ones to come back to?"],"fails":["Slow at navigating BS 7671 and running out of time","Weak Building Regulations knowledge","Not knowing which of the four books holds the answer","Misreading questions under time pressure"]}],"tips":["Get the correct NET Candidate Checklist signed (you, employer, training provider) – every item at least 'Adequate', signatures within 6 months – and send it to the centre, not NET head office; you can't book without it.","AM2S gateway also needs the technical qualification (C&G 601/6299-5 or EAL 601/7345/2) and Level 2 maths/English if you were under 19 at the start.","You may bring only insulated side cutters, wire strippers and pliers (BS EN IEC 60900 preferred, in good condition); the centre supplies all other tools and can refuse unsafe personal tools, which get photographed.","Phones, smart watches and other electronic devices are handed in; only the assessor photographs work. Ask the centre about ID, safety boots and other PPE – check with your centre.","The Candidate Manual must stay in the assessment area; taking it out can disqualify you.","Keep your timesheet accurate for each section including breaks.","The specification is king: work that is safe but doesn't match the drawings/spec can still fail.","Practise your safe isolation sequence until it is automatic – it appears in A1 and C and is the easiest place to fail on a single slip.","Practise filling in the NET blank EIC/schedules and run full test sequences against the GN3 order before the day.","Assessors give no feedback on the day; results come via NET about 5 working days after marking, and unsuccessful candidates only get general feedback.","Re-sits are by section and can be taken at the original or any other NET centre; fees vary by section and version (see NET pages) – check with your centre. For apprentices the employer decides how many re-sits, with an action plan required after two (AM2S) or three (AM2); failing several sections means retraining and a full retake.","Watch NET's Top Tips videos and consider the Inspection & Testing AM2 Prep app mentioned in the manual.","AM2E (experienced workers, via the EWA) uses the same sections and timings as AM2S; if you already hold an AM2 you only do the extra units rather than the full assessment."]};
window.BF_LESSONS = [{"id":"L-regs","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","title":"Finding your way round the Big Red Book","mins":6,"body":"In an open-book exam the book is only useful if you can find things fast. You already know most of the rules from the job – the trick is knowing which drawer they live in.\n**How the numbering works.** Every regulation number tells you where it sits. Take 411.3.3: Part **4**, Chapter **41**, Section **411**, then the regulation within that section. Read the number left to right and you know it is protection for safety, protection against electric shock, automatic disconnection. Once that clicks you stop flicking pages and start going straight there.\n**The Parts, as you'd use them on a job:**\n- Part 1 – scope, object and fundamental principles. The \"why\" behind everything.\n- Part 2 – definitions. If a question hinges on one word, it is defined here.\n- Part 3 – assessment of general characteristics: supply, earthing arrangement, external influences, maintainability. The survey you do before you quote.\n- Part 4 – protection for safety: shock, thermal effects, overcurrent, voltage disturbances, and isolation and switching.\n- Part 5 – selection and erection: cables, containment, devices, earthing and bonding. The first-fix and second-fix Part.\n- Part 6 – inspection and testing. Your EIC and EICR work.\n- Part 7 – special installations or locations: bathrooms, pools, sites, caravan parks, PV, EV and the rest.\n- Appendices – the reference tables: time/current curves (Appendix 3), current-carrying capacity and volt drop (Appendix 4), external influences (Appendix 5) and the model forms (Appendix 6).\n**Two numbering tricks worth knowing.** Regulations numbered 200 and up in the last group (for example 522.6.202) are UK-specific additions. And in Part 7 the digits after the section number mirror the main Parts: 701.411.3.3 is the bathroom version of 411.3.3. So if a question asks about a bathroom, check the Part 7 section first, because it modifies or adds to the general rule rather than replacing the whole book.\n**Definitions that catch people out.** In the van you say \"earth\", \"bonding\" and \"live\" loosely. The exam doesn't.\n- **Exposed-conductive-part** – metalwork of the electrical equipment itself that can be touched and could become live in a fault. The steel case of a cooker, a metal backbox.\n- **Extraneous-conductive-part** – metalwork NOT part of the electrical installation that could bring in a potential, usually earth. Incoming water or gas pipe, structural steel.\n- **Live part** – includes the neutral. A classic trap: neutral is a live conductor.\n- **Basic protection** – protection in normal, fault-free conditions (insulation, enclosures). **Fault protection** – protection when a fault occurs (earthing, bonding, automatic disconnection).\n- **Skilled person / instructed person / ordinary person** – \"electrically\" is part of the definitions. The householder is an ordinary person. Several rules depend on who uses or supervises the installation – for example the cable-depth RCD rule in 522.6.202 applies where the installation is not under the supervision of skilled or instructed persons, and the RCD risk-assessment exception in 411.3.3 is not available in a dwelling.\n- **Earthing conductor** runs from the MET to the means of earthing. It is not a main protective bonding conductor, and neither is a circuit protective conductor.\n**Exam traps.** Look-alike answers often swap exposed for extraneous, or basic for fault protection. Before choosing, ask yourself: is this metal part of the electrical kit, or something else that could import earth? Is this protection working all the time, or only when something has gone wrong?\n**Site example.** You find a copper pipe in a loft feeding a tank. Is it extraneous? Only if it can introduce a potential. A plastic-fed section may not be. The definition, not the fact it's metal, decides whether it needs bonding.","worked":[{"q":"A question refers to Regulation 543.1.3. Without the book, where are you?","steps":["Think: Read the number left to right.","Rule: First digit = Part, first two = Chapter, first three = Section.","So: Part 5 (selection and erection), Chapter 54 (earthing and protective conductors), Section 543 (protective conductors)."],"answer":"Protective conductor sizing, in the earthing chapter of Part 5."},{"q":"Is the neutral of a lighting circuit a live part?","steps":["Think: On site we treat neutral as 'the return', but the exam uses the Part 2 definition.","Rule: A live part is a conductor intended to be energised in normal use, which includes the neutral (but not a PEN conductor).","So: Neutral counts as live – which is why you isolate it and test it during safe isolation."],"answer":"Yes."},{"q":"The metal case of a washing machine and the copper supply pipe to it – which is the exposed-conductive-part?","steps":["Think: Which item belongs to the electrical equipment?","Rule: Exposed-conductive-part = part of electrical equipment; extraneous = not part of the installation but liable to introduce a potential.","So: The machine case is exposed; the pipe (if it can import earth) is extraneous."],"answer":"The machine case is exposed; the pipe is potentially extraneous."}],"keys":["Regulation numbers read as Part / Chapter / Section / regulation – 411.3.3 = Part 4, Ch 41, Sec 411.","Part 7 numbers mirror the general rules (701.411.3.3 modifies 411.3.3) – check Part 7 first for special locations.","Numbers of 200 and up are UK-specific regulations.","Neutral is a live conductor.","Exposed = part of the electrical equipment; extraneous = not part of the installation but can import a potential.","Basic protection = normal conditions; fault protection = single-fault conditions."],"ref":"BS 7671 Part 1, Part 2 (Definitions); regulation numbering convention"},{"id":"L-shock","topic":"Protection against shock","title":"Basic, fault and additional protection","mins":7,"body":"Everything in Chapter 41 comes down to one idea: stop current flowing through a person at a level or for a time that hurts them. BS 7671 does it in layers.\n**Basic protection** stops you touching live parts in the first place: insulation, barriers and enclosures. Enclosures need at least IPXXB or IP2X (a finger can't get in), and readily accessible top surfaces need IPXXD or IP4X – that's why you don't leave a knockout missing on top of a CU.\n**Fault protection** deals with the moment insulation fails and a metal case goes live. In ordinary installations that is **automatic disconnection of supply (ADS)**, Section 411: earth the exposed metalwork, bond the extraneous metalwork at the main earthing terminal, and make sure the device disconnects fast enough.\n**Disconnection times – Table 41.1.** For final circuits up to 63 A with sockets, or up to 32 A supplying only fixed equipment, at 230 V: **0.4 s in TN**, **0.2 s in TT**. Distribution circuits and final circuits above those ratings: **5 s TN**, **1 s TT**. Whether the device actually achieves that depends on Zs, so the maximum Zs tables (Tables 41.2 to 41.4 for fuses and circuit-breakers, Table 41.5 for RCDs on TT) do the work for you. On TT you usually rely on an RCD, and the condition is **RA × IΔn ≤ 50 V**.\n**Additional protection** (Regulation 415.1) is a 30 mA RCD, as a back-up in case basic and fault protection fail or someone is careless. It is required for:\n- socket-outlets rated up to 32 A (411.3.3) – the only exception is where, other than in a dwelling, a documented risk assessment determines RCD protection is not necessary (the old 17th Edition \"labelled socket for a particular item\" exception was removed in 2018);\n- mobile equipment up to 32 A used outdoors (411.3.3);\n- final circuits supplying luminaires in domestic premises (411.3.4);\n- cables concealed in walls at less than 50 mm that aren't otherwise protected (522.6.202/203).\n**Other protective measures** in Chapter 41 you should recognise by name: double or reinforced insulation (412), electrical separation (413), SELV and PELV (414). Obstacles and placing out of reach (417) are only for installations controlled by skilled or instructed persons.\n**Exam traps.**\n- \"Additional protection\" is never a protective measure on its own. An RCD does not replace earthing.\n- Don't mix the TN and TT figures: 0.4 s vs 0.2 s is the favourite distractor.\n- Maximum Zs tables already include the 0.95 voltage factor. Measured values are at ambient temperature, so guidance compares them against roughly 80% of the table figure to allow for the conductor warming up in service.\n- SELV has no connection to earth; PELV does. Both are extra-low voltage.\n**Site example.** Old house, TN-C-S, new kitchen radial on a B32 MCB. The MCB handles overload and short circuit, and it will clear an earth fault within 0.4 s if Zs is under the table value. You still fit it on a 30 mA RCD/RCBO because it feeds sockets (411.3.3). If Zs is too high you don't \"let the RCD cover it\" and walk away without thinking – you find out why Zs is high, because it could be a loose CPC.","worked":[{"q":"A domestic TN-C-S ring final on a Type B 32 A MCB measures Zs 1.20 Ω. Does it meet disconnection?","steps":["Think: It's a socket circuit up to 63 A on TN, so 0.4 s applies; Table 41.3 gives the maximum Zs for a B32 as 1.37 Ω.","Rule: Measured value must be within the table value, and in practice within about 80% (1.10 Ω) to allow for temperature rise.","So: 1.20 Ω is under 1.37 Ω but above the 80% figure – check it against a corrected value rather than just ticking it."],"answer":"Under the table limit, but borderline once temperature is allowed for – look closer."},{"q":"A TT installation has an earth electrode RA of 140 Ω, protected by a 100 mA time-delayed RCD at the origin. Does RA × IΔn ≤ 50 V work?","steps":["Think: Multiply the electrode resistance by the RCD's rated residual current.","Rule: 411.5.3: RA × IΔn ≤ 50 V.","So: 140 × 0.1 = 14 V, well under 50 V. (Max RA for 100 mA would be 500 Ω.)"],"answer":"Yes – 14 V."},{"q":"A client in a small office wants the socket for the server rack not on an RCD. Can you do it?","steps":["Think: Is it a dwelling? If not, has a risk assessment been done?","Rule: 411.3.3 exception: only where, other than in a dwelling, a documented risk assessment determines RCD protection is not necessary. Labelling the socket for one item is no longer an exception (removed in 2018).","So: Possible in an office, but only backed by a documented risk assessment (keep it with the certificate) – and never in a house."],"answer":"Only if a documented risk assessment determines RCD protection is not necessary. A labelled dedicated socket on its own is not enough."}],"keys":["Basic = keep fingers off live parts; fault = ADS; additional = 30 mA RCD back-up.","Table 41.1 at 230 V: final circuits 0.4 s TN / 0.2 s TT; distribution 5 s TN / 1 s TT.","TT with RCD: RA × IΔn ≤ 50 V.","30 mA RCD for sockets ≤32 A, outdoor mobile kit ≤32 A, domestic lighting circuits, and unprotected cables <50 mm in walls.","Enclosures IPXXB/IP2X; top surfaces IPXXD/IP4X.","An RCD never replaces earthing and bonding."],"ref":"BS 7671 Chapter 41 (411, 412, 413, 414, 415, 416); Table 41.1; 411.3.3; 411.3.4; 411.5.3"},{"id":"L-earth","topic":"Earthing & bonding","title":"Earthing, main and supplementary bonding","mins":7,"body":"Earthing and bonding do different jobs, and exams love swapping them.\n**Earthing** gives fault current a low-impedance path back to the source so the protective device operates. It's the CPCs, the MET, the earthing conductor and the means of earthing.\n**Bonding** keeps metalwork you can touch at the same potential, so if there's a fault nobody bridges a dangerous voltage between a tap and a switch plate. Bonding isn't there to carry fault current back to the source – its job is to limit the voltage between metal parts. (On PME it can still carry diverted neutral current, which is why its size matters.)\n**Earthing arrangements (Part 3 assessment, Chapter 54 requirements).**\n- **TN-S** – separate earth from the DNO, usually the cable sheath.\n- **TN-C-S (PME)** – combined neutral and earth in the supply; the earth terminal is taken from the neutral. Very common in UK dwellings.\n- **TT** – your own earth electrode, usually rural or where PME isn't allowed.\nOn PME the risk is a lost supply neutral lifting all your earthed metalwork. That's why main bonding on PME is sized off the supply neutral, and why BS 7671 restricts using a PME earth for caravan pitch supplies (708), marinas (709) and EV charge points (722, unless specific conditions are met).\n**Main protective bonding – Section 544.**\n- Connect incoming water, gas, oil, structural steel and other extraneous-conductive-parts to the MET.\n- Non-PME (544.1.1): at least half the cross-sectional area required for the earthing conductor, minimum 6 mm² copper (need not exceed 25 mm² copper).\n- PME: **Table 54.8**, based on the supply neutral. For a supply neutral up to 35 mm² that's **10 mm²** copper. 35–50 mm² neutral gives 16 mm², and it steps up from there.\n- Location (544.1.2): as near as practicable to point of entry, on the consumer's side of any insulating insert or meter, before any branch. Where practicable **within 600 mm of the gas meter outlet union**, or at the point of entry if the meter is outside.\n**Supplementary bonding – 415.2 and 544.2.** It connects simultaneously accessible exposed and extraneous parts in a location. Minimum 2.5 mm² if mechanically protected, 4 mm² if not. It's an additional measure and must satisfy R ≤ 50 V / Ia (for an RCD, Ia = IΔn). In bathrooms it can often be left out if the conditions in 701.415.2 are met.\n**Protective conductors – Section 543.** CPC sized by Table 54.7 (relative to line conductor) or by the adiabatic equation in 543.1.3. Twin and earth CPCs are smaller than the line, which is why the adiabatic check matters.\n**Exam traps.**\n- Bonding size on PME comes from the supply neutral, not your meter tails or the earthing conductor.\n- Main bonding goes to the MET; supplementary bonding joins parts locally. Different Regulations, different sizes.\n- Plastic incoming water main = no main bonding needed for that service, but check for metal pipes further in.\n- The earthing conductor is sized by Section 543 (and Table 54.1 for buried ones), not Table 54.8.\n**Site example.** Consumer unit change on a PME supply with 25 mm² tails and a 10 mm² earth. Gas bond is 6 mm² and clamped on the far side of the meter behind a boiler. It's undersized (Table 54.8 wants 10 mm²) and too far from the meter outlet. Upgrade it to 10 mm², fit it within 600 mm of the meter outlet on the consumer's side, before any branch, and label it.","worked":[{"q":"PME supply with a 25 mm² supply neutral. What size main protective bonding conductor?","steps":["Think: It's PME, so Table 54.8 applies, based on the supply neutral size.","Rule: Table 54.8: supply neutral up to 35 mm² → 10 mm² copper main bonding.","So: 25 mm² is under 35 mm², so 10 mm²."],"answer":"10 mm² copper."},{"q":"Gas meter is in an external box; pipe enters the house 1.5 m away through the wall. Where does the gas bond go?","steps":["Think: The meter is external, so bonding 'within 600 mm of the meter outlet' would put the clamp outside.","Rule: 544.1.2: as near as practicable to point of entry; within 600 mm of meter outlet or at point of entry if the meter is external; before any branch.","So: Clamp the consumer's pipe where it enters the building, before any tee."],"answer":"At the point of entry inside the building, before any branch."},{"q":"Supplementary bond in a plant room between a pipe and a motor, RCD 30 mA protecting the circuit. What's the maximum resistance allowed?","steps":["Think: Supplementary bonding must keep touch voltage to 50 V at the device operating current.","Rule: 415.2.2: R ≤ 50 V / Ia; for an RCD, Ia = IΔn.","So: 50 / 0.03 = about 1667 Ω – easily achieved with a proper conductor."],"answer":"About 1667 Ω."}],"keys":["Earthing = fault current path; bonding = equal potential.","PME main bonding from Table 54.8 – 10 mm² for supply neutral up to 35 mm².","Non-PME main bonding: half the CSA required for the earthing conductor, min 6 mm² (max needed 25 mm²).","Gas/water bond on consumer side, before any branch, within 600 mm of the meter outlet where practicable.","Supplementary bonding: 2.5 mm² protected / 4 mm² unprotected, and R ≤ 50 V / Ia.","CPC sizing: Table 54.7 or the adiabatic equation (543.1.3)."],"ref":"BS 7671 Chapter 54 (542, 543, 544); Table 54.7; Table 54.8; 544.1.2; 415.2"},{"id":"L-isol","topic":"Isolation & switching","title":"Four kinds of 'off'","mins":6,"body":"On site \"switch it off\" means one thing. BS 7671 has four different kinds of \"off\", each with its own job and its own rules. Most exam questions are really asking \"which one is this?\"\n**1. Isolation** – cutting off the supply so someone can work safely on or near the equipment. It's for skilled people doing electrical work.\n- Must cut all live conductors, except that in TN systems the neutral need not always be isolated where it can be regarded as reliably at earth potential. In TT, isolate the neutral too.\n- The device must have a clear, reliable off position and be securable – lockable, or in a lockable room or enclosure.\n- It can be off-load (you may have to switch the load off first). Semiconductor devices are not suitable for isolation.\n**2. Switching off for mechanical maintenance** – for non-electrical work, like changing a fan belt or cleaning a machine, where the danger is moving parts, heat or radiation rather than shock.\n- Must be manually operated, with a clear off position, and placed so it can't be switched back on by accident – ideally under the control of the person doing the work.\n- Must be able to switch the full load current.\n**3. Emergency switching** – removing danger fast, e.g. a machine trapping someone.\n- Must act as directly as possible on the supply conductors, cut the full load current, and work in one action.\n- Handle or button easy to spot and reach, typically red against a contrasting background.\n- A plug and socket is not acceptable for emergency switching.\n**4. Functional switching** – normal control: light switches, thermostats, contactors. No safety function, so semiconductors and dimmers are fine here.\n**Where to look.** The requirements are in Part 4, Chapter 46 (isolation and switching), and selection of the devices is in Part 5, Chapter 53, Section 537. Check both if a question is about which device can be used.\n**Other points worth remembering.**\n- Every installation needs a main linked switch or linked circuit-breaker as near as practicable to the origin (462.1.201). The same regulation says a main switch intended for operation by ordinary persons, e.g. in a household or similar installation, must interrupt both live conductors (line and neutral) of a single-phase supply – i.e. double-pole.\n- Where isolation is remote from the equipment, provide means of securing it in the off position.\n- A plug and socket is a perfectly good means of isolation for a portable appliance, as long as the plug stays under the control of the person working on it.\n**Exam traps.**\n- \"Isolation\" vs \"switching off for mechanical maintenance\" – if the danger isn't electrical, it's mechanical maintenance.\n- An isolator that isn't rated to break load isn't an emergency switch.\n- Functional switch (like a dimmer) is never an isolator.\n**Site example.** Extract fan in a commercial kitchen on a rooftop. You want a local lockable switch-disconnector next to the fan so the fitter changing a bearing can lock it off himself – that covers switching for mechanical maintenance and isolation. Emergency stop buttons by the cooking line are a separate item and have to cut the supply on load in one action.","worked":[{"q":"A cleaner needs to stop a conveyor quickly if someone's sleeve gets caught. What type of switching is needed?","steps":["Think: The danger is immediate and mechanical; it has to be removed fast.","Rule: Emergency switching: one action, direct on supply, on load, easily recognised.","So: An emergency stop device, not just a local isolator or a control switch."],"answer":"Emergency switching."},{"q":"A fitter will change a belt on an AHU. Is a TP isolator in the plant room downstairs, not lockable, acceptable?","steps":["Think: The work is non-electrical, so the risk is the fan starting while he's in it.","Rule: Switching off for mechanical maintenance: suitably placed and secured so it can't be switched back on accidentally.","So: Not acceptable as it stands – make it lockable or fit a local lockable switch-disconnector."],"answer":"No – it must be securable or under his control."},{"q":"Can a TRIAC dimmer be used as the means of isolation for a lighting circuit?","steps":["Think: A dimmer is a semiconductor device doing functional control.","Rule: Semiconductor devices are not suitable for isolation.","So: Isolate at the circuit-breaker or a proper isolator instead."],"answer":"No."}],"keys":["Four types: isolation, mechanical maintenance switching, emergency switching, functional switching.","Isolation must be securable in the off position and can be off-load.","Emergency switching: on load, direct on supply, one action – never a plug and socket.","Semiconductor devices can do functional switching but not isolation.","Main switch for operation by ordinary persons (e.g. household) must break both line and neutral on single-phase (462.1.201).","Requirements in Chapter 46; device selection in Section 537."],"ref":"BS 7671 Chapter 46 (462.1.201); Section 537"},{"id":"L-select","topic":"Selection & erection","title":"Putting it in right: Part 5","mins":7,"body":"Part 5 is the Part closest to what you actually do all day. Chapter 51 covers general rules, Chapter 52 wiring systems, Chapter 53 protection/isolation/switching/control devices, Chapter 54 earthing and protective conductors, Chapter 55 other equipment and Chapter 56 safety services.\n**Cable selection.** For overcurrent, the basic relationship is **Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz**: design current ≤ device rating ≤ cable capacity after correction factors (Chapter 43). Current-carrying capacities, correction factors and volt drop are in **Appendix 4**. For a public LV supply the usual volt drop limits are 3% for lighting and 5% for other uses. In the exam, reach for Appendix 4 for anything with \"rating factor\", \"grouping\" or \"mV/A/m\".\n**Cables in walls – 522.6.202 and 522.6.203.** A cable concealed in a wall less than 50 mm from the surface must either:\n- run in a safe zone (horizontally or vertically in line with an accessory, or within 150 mm of the top of the wall or a corner), and be protected by a 30 mA RCD in installations not under the supervision of skilled or instructed persons; or\n- have earthed metallic covering or mechanical protection that stops nails and screws getting through.\nWalls or partitions with metal parts (like metal stud) need RCD protection or earthed protection regardless of depth, where not under supervision.\n**Fire and support.** Wiring systems must be supported so they won't collapse early in a fire (521.10.202). That means non-metallic clips, cable ties or trunking must not be the only means of support – use metal or fire-resistant fixings. Where cables pass through fire-rated walls or floors, the hole has to be sealed back to the original fire resistance (Section 527).\n**External influences (Section 522 and Appendix 5).** Choose equipment for where it lives: water (AD), dust/foreign bodies (AE), impact (AG), sunlight (AN) and so on. IP code: first digit solids, second digit water.\n**Connections (Section 526).** Must be accessible for inspection, testing and maintenance, except maintenance-free types such as crimped or soldered joints or maintenance-free junction boxes. Screw terminals buried under plaster are a common fail.\n**Identification (Section 514).** Brown/black/grey for line conductors, blue neutral, green-and-yellow protective. Warning notices for mixed colours, RCD testing, periodic inspection, dual supplies.\n**Proximity (Section 528).** Keep Band I (data, fire alarm, ELV) and Band II (230 V) segregated or insulated for the highest voltage present, and keep clear of other services.\n**Exam traps.**\n- Safe zones alone are not enough in a house – RCD protection is the other half of the condition.\n- \"Maintenance-free\" joints can be hidden; screw terminals can't.\n- Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz – look-alike answers reverse the order.\n- Collapse-in-fire applies to all wiring systems, not just escape routes, since 2018.\n**Site example.** Rewire in a Victorian terrace in Llandudno. Chasing down from the ceiling above a new socket keeps you in a safe zone; the socket circuit is RCBO protected. Surface cables in the loft on plastic clips need metal clips or fire-resistant fixings at intervals, and the hole through the party-wall firebreak needs sealing.","worked":[{"q":"A cable is buried 25 mm deep, running diagonally across a bedroom wall to a socket. RCD protected. Compliant?","steps":["Think: It's under 50 mm, so it must be in a safe zone AND RCD protected, or mechanically protected.","Rule: 522.6.202: safe zones are horizontal/vertical in line with accessories or within 150 mm of wall top/corner.","So: A diagonal run is outside the safe zones; RCD alone doesn't fix that."],"answer":"No – re-route into a safe zone or give it earthed/mechanical protection."},{"q":"Design current 26 A, device 32 A, cable Iz after correction 29 A. OK?","steps":["Think: Put the numbers into Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz.","Rule: Chapter 43: the device rating must not exceed the corrected cable capacity.","So: 26 ≤ 32 but 32 > 29 – the cable isn't protected against overload."],"answer":"No – bigger cable or smaller device."},{"q":"A joint in a floor void uses a standard screw-terminal junction box. The floor is going to be boarded over and tiled. Allowed?","steps":["Think: Will anyone be able to get at it for inspection later?","Rule: Section 526: connections accessible unless maintenance-free type.","So: Use a maintenance-free box or crimp, or provide an access point."],"answer":"No, not with a screw-terminal box."}],"keys":["Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz; Appendix 4 for ratings, correction factors and volt drop.","Concealed <50 mm: safe zone + 30 mA RCD, or earthed/mechanical protection (522.6.202).","Metal-stud walls: RCD or earthed protection at any depth (522.6.203).","All wiring systems supported against premature collapse in fire (521.10.202).","Screw terminals must stay accessible; maintenance-free joints can be hidden.","IP code: first digit solids, second water."],"ref":"BS 7671 Part 5 (Chapters 51–56); 522.6.202; 522.6.203; 521.10.202; Section 526; Appendix 4"},{"id":"L-special","topic":"Special locations","title":"Part 7: where the rules get tighter","mins":8,"body":"Part 7 covers locations where the risk goes up: wet skin, bare feet, animals, portable kit, the public. Each section adds to or modifies the general rules; it doesn't replace them. The first three digits give the location, and the rest mirror the general Regulation being changed.\n**The sections you're most likely to meet:**\n- **701** Bathrooms and shower rooms\n- **702** Swimming pools and other basins\n- **703** Saunas\n- **704** Construction and demolition sites\n- **705** Agricultural and horticultural premises\n- **708** Caravan and camping parks\n- **711** Exhibitions, shows and stands\n- **712** Solar PV systems\n- **717** Mobile or transportable units\n- **721** Electrical installations in caravans and motor caravans\n- **722** EV charging\n**Bathrooms (701).** Zones are based on the bath or shower:\n- Zone 0 – inside the bath or shower tray.\n- Zone 1 – above zone 0 up to 2.25 m above the finished floor, or the height of the highest fixed shower head or water outlet if that is higher.\n- Zone 2 – 0.6 m out from the edge of zone 1.\nInstalled equipment must be at least **IPX7 in zone 0** and **IPX4 in zones 1 and 2** (701.512.2), and IPX5 where water jets are used for cleaning. Zone 0 only allows fixed, permanently connected equipment suitable for zone 0 and supplied by SELV at no more than 12 V AC or 30 V ripple-free DC. All circuits in the location need 30 mA RCD protection. Socket-outlets (other than shaver supply units) must be at least 2.5 m from the zone 1 boundary. Supplementary bonding can be left out where all final circuits of the location meet the ADS disconnection times, all have 30 mA RCD protection, and all extraneous-conductive-parts of the location are effectively connected to the main protective bonding (701.415.2).\n**Pools (702).** Similar zone idea (0, 1, 2), much stricter – SELV in the wettest zones and tight limits on what is allowed where.\n**Construction sites (704).** HSE and industry guidance favour **110 V reduced low voltage** for portable tools and hand lamps, centre-tapped to earth, so maximum 55 V to earth single-phase (63.5 V on three-phase). BS 7671 requires socket-outlets up to 32 A on site to be protected by a 30 mA RCD, reduced low voltage, SELV or electrical separation. Site distribution needs to stand up to rough treatment and weather.\n**Agricultural (705).** Livestock are more sensitive to shock than people, so supplementary bonding in animal areas, RCD protection for sockets and fire protection, and equipment rated for dust, water and corrosion.\n**Caravan parks (708).** Pitch sockets at least IP44, each socket individually protected by its own overcurrent device and 30 mA RCD. The caravan's protective conductor must not be connected to a PME earth.\n**PV (712) and EV (722).** Both deal with DC or DC-influenced faults and with PME issues. EV charge points have specific RCD and PME requirements – check 722 in full before answering.\n**Exam traps.**\n- Zone 2 is measured horizontally from zone 1, not from the bath.\n- Socket distance is from the zone 1 boundary, not the bath.\n- 110 V CTE is 55 V to earth, not 110 V.\n- Supplementary bonding in bathrooms is not always required – read the conditions.\n**Site example.** Ensuite refit in Conwy. A new LED downlight over the shower is in zone 1, so it needs IPX4 minimum (and fire-rated if the ceiling needs it). The whole ensuite is on a 30 mA RCD. The extractor is in zone 2, IPX4. The shaver point is a shaver supply unit to BS EN 61558-2-5, so it can sit in zone 2 where direct spray from the shower is unlikely.","worked":[{"q":"A customer wants a standard 13 A double socket in a large bathroom, 3 m from the zone 1 boundary. RCD protected. Allowed?","steps":["Think: Sockets other than shaver units are allowed in a bathroom only if far enough from zone 1.","Rule: 701.512.3: at least 2.5 m horizontally from the zone 1 boundary, and RCD protected.","So: 3 m is more than 2.5 m and it's RCD protected."],"answer":"Yes."},{"q":"House bathroom, all circuits RCBO protected, main bonding in place. Do you need supplementary bonding?","steps":["Think: Check the three conditions for omitting it.","Rule: 701.415.2: omit where all final circuits of the location meet ADS disconnection times, all have 30 mA RCD protection, and all extraneous-conductive-parts are connected to main bonding.","So: All three are met (check disconnection times are achieved too)."],"answer":"No, it can be omitted."},{"q":"On a building site, what's the voltage to earth of a 110 V centre-tapped transformer output?","steps":["Think: The centre tap is earthed, so each end is half the output.","Rule: Reduced low voltage system, centre-tapped to earth.","So: 110 / 2 = 55 V to earth."],"answer":"55 V."}],"keys":["Part 7 adds to or modifies the general rules – read both.","Bathroom: zone 1 to 2.25 m high, zone 2 0.6 m beyond zone 1, IPX7 in zone 0, IPX4 min in zones 1 and 2.","Bathroom sockets ≥ 2.5 m from zone 1 boundary; all circuits 30 mA RCD.","Construction sites: 110 V CTE = 55 V to earth.","Caravan pitch sockets: IP44, individual RCD and overcurrent protection, no PME to the caravan.","Section numbers: 701 bath, 702 pool, 704 site, 705 farm, 708 caravan park, 712 PV, 722 EV."],"ref":"BS 7671 Part 7 (701, 702, 704, 705, 708, 712, 722); 701.415.2; 701.512.3"},{"id":"L-surge","topic":"Surge & AFDD","title":"SPDs and AFDDs after A2","mins":6,"body":"Two devices that used to be \"nice to have\" and now show up on almost every install and in the exam.\n**Surge protection (SPDs).** A transient overvoltage is a very short, very high voltage spike – from lightning nearby or on the overhead network, or from switching big loads. It doesn't trip an MCB, but it can wreck boilers, TVs, alarm panels and LED drivers, and it can degrade insulation.\n**Where it lives in the book.** The decision on whether you need it is in **Section 443** (Chapter 44, protection against voltage disturbances). How to select and install the SPD is in **Section 534**. Exams sometimes put these as look-alikes.\n**The A2 rule (443.4.1).** Protection against transient overvoltages must be provided where the consequence of an overvoltage could result in:\n- serious injury to, or loss of, human life;\n- failure of a safety service (as defined in Part 2);\n- significant financial or data loss.\nFor **all other cases**, SPDs must be fitted **unless the owner of the installation declines** them, declaring any loss or damage tolerable and accepting the risk. Dwellings no longer get a blanket exemption. So in practice: fit one, or get the client's written decision not to.\n**SPD types.**\n- **Type 1** – handles a direct lightning current, used where there's a lightning protection system or high risk of direct strike.\n- **Type 2** – the usual one in a consumer unit, dealing with induced and switching surges.\n- **Type 3** – near sensitive equipment, as a supplement to Type 2.\nKeep the connecting leads as short as possible – long leads add voltage and make the SPD far less effective. Preferably under 0.5 m total.\n**AFDDs – 421.1.7.** An arc fault detection device watches the current waveform for the signature of an arc: a loose terminal, a nail through a cable, a crushed flex behind a sofa. MCBs and RCDs can miss these because the current can be low and there may be no earth leakage.\nUnder A2, AFDDs **are required** for single-phase AC final circuits supplying socket-outlets up to 32 A in:\n- higher-risk residential buildings (HRRBs);\n- houses in multiple occupation (HMOs);\n- purpose-built student accommodation;\n- care homes.\nEverywhere else they are **recommended**. It sits in Chapter 42 – protection against thermal effects – because it's about fire, not shock.\n**Exam traps.**\n- SPDs protect against overvoltage, not overcurrent.\n- Section 443 decides whether; Section 534 decides how.\n- AFDDs are about fire (Chapter 42), not shock (Chapter 41).\n- AFDD requirement is for socket circuits ≤32 A in the listed buildings, not \"all circuits in all houses\".\n**Site example.** CU change in a bungalow. The customer doesn't want to pay for an SPD. Under A2 you either fit one or get their decision in writing and record it. If the house has been converted into an HMO, the socket circuits also need AFDDs.","worked":[{"q":"CU replacement in a normal house. The owner signs to say they don't want an SPD and accept the risk. Compliant?","steps":["Think: It's not one of the listed high-consequence cases.","Rule: 443.4.1 (A2): for all other cases, protection is required unless the owner declares it is not needed and accepts the risk.","So: The owner's declaration covers it – record it on the certificate."],"answer":"Yes, if the decision is recorded."},{"q":"A five-bed house let as an HMO needs a new ring final. Do you need an AFDD?","steps":["Think: Single-phase socket circuit ≤32 A in an HMO.","Rule: 421.1.7 (A2): AFDDs required for such circuits in HMOs.","So: Fit an AFDD (or AFDD/RCBO combination) on the circuit."],"answer":"Yes."},{"q":"Which section of BS 7671 do you check for how to connect an SPD in a CU?","steps":["Think: Deciding whether and how to install are separate questions.","Rule: Section 443 = whether needed; Section 534 = selection and erection of SPDs.","So: The install details are in Section 534."],"answer":"Section 534."}],"keys":["Section 443 = whether SPD protection is needed; Section 534 = selection and installation.","A2: fit SPDs unless the owner declares loss is tolerable and accepts the risk – dwellings not exempt. Mandatory cases: risk to life, safety service failure, significant financial/data loss.","Type 1 direct lightning, Type 2 at the CU, Type 3 near sensitive equipment.","Keep SPD leads short – preferably under 0.5 m total.","AFDDs (421.1.7) required on single-phase socket circuits ≤32 A in HRRBs, HMOs, student blocks and care homes; recommended elsewhere.","AFDDs are fire protection (Chapter 42), not shock protection."],"ref":"BS 7671 Section 443 (443.4.1); Section 534; 421.1.7"},{"id":"L-law","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","title":"EAWR, GS38 and proving dead","mins":7,"body":"BS 7671 is a British Standard – not law in itself. The **Electricity at Work Regulations 1989 (EAWR)** are law, made under the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974. Following BS 7671 is a good way of showing you've met EAWR, but the legal duty comes from EAWR.\n**The EAWR regulations you should know by number:**\n- **Reg 4** – systems must be built and maintained to prevent danger, work activities carried out safely, and protective equipment suitable and maintained.\n- **Reg 12** – suitable means for cutting off the supply and for isolation.\n- **Reg 13** – precautions when working on equipment made dead so it can't become live again while work is done (lock off, prove dead).\n- **Reg 14** – no live working unless it's unreasonable for it to be dead, reasonable in all the circumstances to work live, and suitable precautions are taken. All three conditions, not one.\n- **Reg 16** – people must be competent (or properly supervised) to prevent danger and injury.\nSome duties are **absolute** – they must be met whatever the cost – and others are **so far as reasonably practicable**. For some absolute duties the law gives a defence of having taken all reasonable steps and exercised due diligence (Reg 29).\n**GS38 (HSE guidance on test equipment).** Probes and leads for testing should have:\n- finger guards and shrouded or short tips (no more than 4 mm exposed, preferably 2 mm or less);\n- fuses or current limiting;\n- good insulation, flexible, long enough but not excessively long, and in good condition.\nUse a proper two-pole voltage indicator to prove dead. GS38 warns that a multimeter set on the wrong range can give a false reading, or draw heavy current on a current or resistance range. A neon screwdriver is not an acceptable way to prove dead.\n**Safe isolation sequence.**\n1. Identify the circuit or equipment and the right means of isolation.\n2. Isolate.\n3. Secure – lock off, keep the key, fit a warning notice.\n4. Prove the tester on a known source or proving unit.\n5. Test dead at the point of work: L–N, L–E, N–E on single-phase.\n6. Re-prove the tester on the proving unit.\nOn three-phase with neutral that's **10 tests**: L1–L2, L1–L3, L2–L3, each line to N, each line to E, and N–E.\n**Exam traps.**\n- Regulation 14 needs all three conditions.\n- Proving the tester before AND after is part of the sequence.\n- EAWR is law; BS 7671 isn't (though it's referenced in other laws and contracts).\n- Removing the fuse and putting it in your pocket is securing, but a lock and key with a notice is the expected answer.\n**Site example.** Changing a light fitting in an occupied office. Identify the lighting circuit, lock off the MCB with a lock-off device and your own padlock, tag it, prove the tester, test the terminals at the fitting, re-prove. Switch lines and borrowed neutrals can still be live after isolating one circuit – test every conductor you're going to touch.","worked":[{"q":"A shop owner won't allow a shutdown and asks you to swap a faulty socket live. Can you?","steps":["Think: Is it unreasonable to make it dead? Just inconvenient for the business isn't enough.","Rule: EAWR Reg 14: live work only if unreasonable to be dead, reasonable to work live, and suitable precautions taken.","So: Isolating one socket circuit is very reasonable, so the first condition fails."],"answer":"No – isolate it."},{"q":"How many tests to prove dead on a three-phase-and-neutral isolator?","steps":["Think: Test every combination of conductors that could be live.","Rule: Three line-to-line, three line-to-neutral, three line-to-earth, one neutral-to-earth.","So: 3 + 3 + 3 + 1 = 10."],"answer":"10."},{"q":"You've locked off and tested dead. When should you re-prove the tester?","steps":["Think: The tester could have failed during the test, giving a false 'dead'.","Rule: Safe isolation: prove – test – re-prove on a known source.","So: Straight after the dead tests, before you touch anything."],"answer":"Immediately after testing dead."}],"keys":["EAWR 1989 is law; BS 7671 is a standard that helps you comply.","Reg 4 systems/work/PPE; 12 isolation means; 13 working dead; 14 live working; 16 competence.","Reg 14 needs all three conditions to work live.","GS38: finger guards, max 4 mm exposed tip (2 mm preferred), fused or current-limited leads.","Safe isolation: identify, isolate, secure, prove, test, re-prove.","Three-phase with neutral: 10 tests."],"ref":"Electricity at Work Regulations 1989 (Regs 4, 12, 13, 14, 16, 29); HSE GS38; safe isolation"},{"id":"L-partp","topic":"Part P & certification","title":"Part P, notification and paperwork","mins":6,"body":"**Part P of the Building Regulations** is about electrical safety in dwellings: electrical installations must be designed and installed to protect people from fire and injury. It applies to homes (and some associated areas like gardens and shared parts of flats), not to commercial jobs.\n**Notifiable work.** Some jobs must be notified to building control, either through a competent person scheme (like NICEIC or NAPIT) or by going to building control before you start. **In England** the notifiable list is:\n- installing a new circuit;\n- replacing a consumer unit;\n- any addition or alteration to existing circuits in a special location (within the zones of a bath or shower, a swimming pool or a sauna).\nLike-for-like replacements, repairs and additions to existing circuits outside special locations aren't notifiable in England.\n**Wales has its own Approved Document P, and it catches more.** The Welsh Government lists as NOT notifiable: replacing fittings (sockets, light fittings, switches), repairs and maintenance, adding a fused spur or extra points to an existing circuit **but not in a kitchen, bathroom or outdoors**, installing or upgrading main or supplementary bonding, and ELV signalling/communication cabling. So in Wales, adding to a circuit in a kitchen, a bathroom or outdoors is notifiable, as well as new circuits and CU changes. In the exam, read exactly which jurisdiction the question means.\nNon-notifiable work still has to meet Part P and BS 7671 and still gets a certificate.\n**Competent person schemes.** If you're registered, you self-certify and the scheme notifies building control. The householder then gets a Building Regulations compliance certificate. If you're not registered, notifiable work goes via building control, which adds cost and delay – the whole point of joining a scheme.\n**The certificates (Appendix 6 model forms).**\n- **EIC – Electrical Installation Certificate.** New installation, new circuit, or CU change. Three signatures: design, construction, and inspection and testing. If one person did all three, they can sign all three (or use a single-signature form). Must include the schedule of inspections and the schedule of test results.\n- **MEIWC – Minor Electrical Installation Works Certificate.** Additions or alterations to an existing circuit that don't include a new circuit – an extra socket, moving a light.\n- **EICR – Electrical Installation Condition Report.** Assesses the condition of an existing installation. It's a report, not a certificate – it doesn't certify new work. Observations are coded **C1** (danger present), **C2** (potentially dangerous), **C3** (improvement recommended) and **FI** (further investigation). Any C1, C2 or FI makes the overall condition unsatisfactory.\n**Exam traps.**\n- A new circuit needs an EIC, not a MEIWC, even if it's small.\n- An EICR can't be used to certify your own new work.\n- C3 alone doesn't make an EICR unsatisfactory.\n- Part P applies to dwellings; a shop fit-out isn't Part P work (though BS 7671 and EAWR still apply).\n**Site example.** You add a fused spur for a new cooker hood off an existing ring in a kitchen in Llandudno. That's an addition, so a MEIWC. Because it is in a kitchen in Wales, it IS notifiable under the Welsh Approved Document P (it would not be in England). If you'd run a new radial from the CU instead, it's a new circuit: EIC and notify.","worked":[{"q":"You replace a consumer unit in a house. Which certificate, and is it notifiable?","steps":["Think: A CU change affects the whole installation.","Rule: EIC for new work including CU replacement; CU replacement is notifiable under Part P.","So: Issue an EIC with schedules and notify via your scheme."],"answer":"EIC; yes, notifiable."},{"q":"On an EICR you find no main bonding to a metal gas pipe and one C3 for missing labels. Overall result?","steps":["Think: Missing main bonding is potentially dangerous (typically C2).","Rule: Any C1, C2 or FI = unsatisfactory; C3 alone doesn't.","So: The C2 drives the result."],"answer":"Unsatisfactory."},{"q":"You designed and installed a new radial, and a colleague did the testing. Who signs the EIC?","steps":["Think: Three responsibilities on the EIC.","Rule: EIC: separate signatures for design, construction, and inspection and testing.","So: You sign design and construction; your colleague signs inspection and testing."],"answer":"You sign design and construction; the colleague signs I&T."}],"keys":["Part P applies to dwellings. England: new circuits, CU replacement and additions/alterations in special locations are notifiable.","Wales has its own Approved Document P: additions to circuits in kitchens, bathrooms or outdoors are also notifiable there.","EIC for new installations/new circuits/CU change; MEIWC for additions without a new circuit; EICR for condition reports.","EIC has three signatures: design, construction, inspection and testing.","EICR codes: C1 danger, C2 potentially dangerous, C3 improvement, FI – any C1/C2/FI = unsatisfactory.","Scheme members self-certify; the customer gets a Building Regs compliance certificate."],"ref":"Building Regulations Part P (Approved Document P – England, and the Welsh Government edition); BS 7671 Part 6 and Appendix 6"},{"id":"L-sci","topic":"Electrical science","title":"Ohm's law, power and heat","mins":5,"body":"Everything else in the exam sits on a handful of formulas. On site you never think about them because the app or the OSG table has done it for you. In the exam you have a calculator, a pen and a question written to see if you can pick the right formula first time.\n**The core four**\n- Ohm's law: V = I × R (V volts, I amps, R ohms). Cover the one you want: I = V ÷ R, R = V ÷ I.\n- Power (resistive load): P = V × I, also P = I² × R and P = V² ÷ R. P is watts.\n- Power with power factor (single-phase): P = V × I × cos φ, where cos φ is the power factor (1 for a heater, less for motors and some lighting).\n- Three-phase balanced power: P = √3 × VL × IL × cos φ, where VL is the line voltage (400 V) and IL the line current.\n**Series and parallel**\n- Series: Rt = R1 + R2 + R3. Same current through everything; voltages add up.\n- Parallel: 1/Rt = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3. Same voltage across everything; the total is always lower than the smallest branch. Two equal resistances in parallel give half of one.\n**Heat changes resistance**\nCopper resistance rises as it warms. The rough rule is R hot = R cold × (1 + α × Δt), with α about 0.004 per °C for copper and Δt the temperature rise. Go from 20 °C (where tables and your meter live) to 70 °C (the working limit of ordinary PVC cable) and you get 1 + 0.004 × 50 = 1.2. That is where the famous **× 1.2** comes from. Knowing that helps you remember when to use it: any time you turn a cold resistance into a working-temperature resistance.\n**Exam traps**\n- Using 400 V on a single-phase question. Single-phase is 230 V (Uo). Only use 400 V with the √3 formula or where the question clearly gives a line-to-line figure.\n- Forgetting to convert kW to W (3 kW = 3000 W) or mΩ to Ω (divide by 1000).\n- Using P = V × I on a motor when a power factor is given. If cos φ is in the question, it is there to be used.\n- Adding parallel resistances straight up.\n**Sanity checks**\n- About 4.3 A per kW at 230 V. A 3 kW heater drawing 13 A looks right; 130 A doesn't.\n- A parallel result must be smaller than the smallest branch.\n- A hot resistance is always a bit bigger than the cold one, never smaller.","worked":[{"q":"A 3 kW immersion heater runs on a 230 V supply. Find the current it draws and the resistance of the element.","steps":["Formula: I = P ÷ V, then R = V ÷ I","Substitute: I = 3000 ÷ 230; R = 230 ÷ 13.043 (or V² ÷ P = 230² ÷ 3000)","Result: I = 13.04 A, R = 17.63 Ω","Compare / conclusion: 3 kW × about 4.3 A per kW = 13 A, so the current looks right. A 16 A circuit is fine for it."],"answer":"13.04 A, 17.63 Ω"},{"q":"A circuit's R1+R2 measures 0.60 Ω at 20 °C. Using α = 0.004 per °C for copper, what will it be at a conductor temperature of 70 °C?","steps":["Formula: R hot = R cold × (1 + α × Δt)","Substitute: R hot = 0.60 × (1 + 0.004 × 50) = 0.60 × 1.2","Result: R hot = 0.72 Ω","Compare / conclusion: this is exactly the × 1.2 multiplier used for 70 °C PVC cable when checking Zs."],"answer":"0.72 Ω"},{"q":"A single-phase motor on 230 V draws 10 A at a power factor of 0.8. A separate balanced three-phase load on 400 V draws 20 A per line at a power factor of 0.85. Find the power of each.","steps":["Formula: single-phase P = V × I × cos φ; three-phase P = √3 × VL × IL × cos φ","Substitute: P = 230 × 10 × 0.8; P = 1.732 × 400 × 20 × 0.85","Result: single-phase = 1840 W; three-phase = 11778 W (about 11.8 kW)","Compare / conclusion: without the power factor the motor would come out at 2300 W, which is apparent power in VA, not real power in W."],"answer":"1840 W and about 11.8 kW"}],"keys":["V = I × R; P = V × I = I²R = V²/R","Single-phase uses 230 V; 400 V only with √3 for three-phase","Parallel total is always less than the smallest branch","× 1.2 comes from 1 + 0.004 × 50 (20 °C to 70 °C copper)","Roughly 4.3 A per kW at 230 V for a quick check"],"ref":"Electrical science"},{"id":"L-cable","topic":"Cable sizing","title":"Sizing a cable by hand","mins":7,"body":"On site you pick 2.5 for sockets, 6 or 10 for a shower, and you're usually right. The exam wants to see the actual process, because the times you're wrong are when the cable is hot, bunched or buried in insulation.\n**The chain: Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz**\n- Ib = design current, what the load actually draws (P ÷ V).\n- In = rated current of the protective device (the 32 in B32).\n- Iz = what the cable can carry in its real installed conditions.\nPick In at or above Ib, then find a cable whose tabulated rating It is big enough once you knock it down for the conditions.\n**The correction formula**\nIt ≥ In ÷ (Ca × Cg × Ci × Cc)\n- It: tabulated current rating you look up in Appendix 4 for the installation method.\n- Ca: ambient temperature factor (1.0 at 30 °C for 70 °C PVC; less when hotter).\n- Cg: grouping factor, for circuits bunched together.\n- Ci: thermal insulation factor. Cable totally surrounded by insulation for more than 0.5 m takes 0.5.\n- Cc: 0.725 where the device is a BS 3036 rewireable fuse; 0.9 where the cable is buried in the ground (direct or in ducts); otherwise 1 (MCBs, RCBOs and BS 88 fuses in air).\nOnly factors that actually apply go in. If there is no grouping, Cg is 1 and you leave it out.\n**Doing it by hand**\n1. Work out Ib from the load.\n2. Choose In, the next standard device size at or above Ib.\n3. Divide In by all the factors multiplied together. That gives the minimum It.\n4. Go to the right table and the right installation method column. Pick the first cable whose It is equal to or above your figure.\n5. Then check voltage drop, Zs and the CPC (adiabatic). Current rating alone isn't the end of it.\n**Exam traps**\n- Dividing Ib instead of In. The factors are applied to In.\n- Multiplying by the factors instead of dividing. Correction factors make the required It bigger, never smaller.\n- Using the wrong installation method column. Clipped direct (method C) ratings are higher than cable in insulation.\n- Applying 0.725 to an MCB. It's only for rewireable fuses.\n- Stopping at \"near enough\". If you need 47.9 A and the cable is rated 47 A, it fails.\n**Sanity check**\nThe required It should always be equal to or higher than In. If your answer is smaller, you multiplied instead of divided.","worked":[{"q":"A 9.5 kW shower on 230 V is protected by a 45 A MCB. The flat twin and earth cable is clipped direct (method C) where the ambient is 35 °C, Ca = 0.94. Tabulated ratings for method C: 6 mm² = 47 A, 10 mm² = 64 A. Which cable size is needed?","steps":["Formula: Ib = P ÷ V; It ≥ In ÷ Ca","Substitute: Ib = 9500 ÷ 230 = 41.3 A, so a 45 A device is fine (45 ≥ 41.3). It ≥ 45 ÷ 0.94","Result: It ≥ 47.9 A","Compare / conclusion: 6 mm² at 47 A is below 47.9 A, so it fails. 10 mm² at 64 A passes."],"answer":"10 mm²"},{"q":"A 32 A radial on a B32 MCB is bunched with two other circuits, Cg = 0.70. Ambient is 30 °C (Ca = 1). Method C tabulated ratings: 4 mm² = 37 A, 6 mm² = 47 A. Which cable is needed?","steps":["Formula: It ≥ In ÷ (Ca × Cg)","Substitute: It ≥ 32 ÷ (1 × 0.70)","Result: It ≥ 45.7 A","Compare / conclusion: 4 mm² (37 A) is too small; 6 mm² (47 A) is the first cable at or above 45.7 A."],"answer":"6 mm²"},{"q":"An old circuit is protected by a 30 A BS 3036 rewireable fuse (Cc = 0.725), cable clipped direct, no other factors. Method C tabulated ratings: 4 mm² = 37 A, 6 mm² = 47 A. What minimum cable size is needed?","steps":["Formula: It ≥ In ÷ Cc","Substitute: It ≥ 30 ÷ 0.725","Result: It ≥ 41.4 A","Compare / conclusion: 4 mm² (37 A) fails; 6 mm² (47 A) passes. Protected by a device that needs no Cc (e.g. a 30 A BS 88-3 cartridge fuse), 4 mm² at 37 A would have been fine."],"answer":"6 mm²"}],"keys":["Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz, always in that order","It ≥ In ÷ (Ca × Cg × Ci × Cc): divide, never multiply","Cc = 0.725 for BS 3036 rewireable fuses (0.9 for cables buried in the ground); never 0.725 for an MCB","Ci = 0.5 for more than 0.5 m totally surrounded by insulation","Just under the requirement is a fail, not near enough","After current rating, still check voltage drop and Zs"],"ref":"Appendix 4"},{"id":"L-vd","topic":"Voltage drop","title":"Voltage drop the long way","mins":6,"body":"The app just shows green or red. The exam wants the sum. It's easy once you know that the table does most of the work.\n**Why it matters**\nIf voltage drops too much along a cable, motors struggle, lamps dim and some electronics misbehave. From a public low voltage supply, BS 7671 limits the drop from the origin to the load to:\n- Lighting: 3% of 230 V = 6.9 V\n- Other uses: 5% of 230 V = 11.5 V\n**The formula**\nVD = (mV/A/m × Ib × L) ÷ 1000\n- mV/A/m: millivolts dropped per amp per metre, from the voltage drop column of the Appendix 4 table for that cable.\n- Ib: design current in amps (what the load draws, not the breaker rating, unless the question says otherwise).\n- L: length of the run in metres, one way. The table already allows for the route out and back.\n- ÷ 1000: turns millivolts into volts.\n**Ring finals**\nFor a ring with the load at the midpoint, each leg is half the length and carries half the current, so the drop is a quarter of the radial figure: divide by 4.\n**Exam traps**\n- Forgetting ÷ 1000. You'll get an answer like 3270 V and hopefully notice.\n- Doubling the length for the return conductor. The mV/A/m figure already covers both conductors.\n- Using 400 V as the base on a single-phase circuit. The percentage is of 230 V.\n- Adding × 1.2. The tabulated mV/A/m values are already at conductor working temperature, so don't add it again.\n- Using the lighting 3% on a socket circuit or vice versa.\n**Rearranging for max length**\nL max = (allowed VD × 1000) ÷ (mV/A/m × Ib). Handy for \"how far can I run this?\" questions.\n**Sanity check**\nA few volts on a normal domestic circuit is typical. Tens of volts means a wrong decimal. Also compare: bigger cable, smaller mV/A/m, less drop. If going up a size made it worse, something is off.","worked":[{"q":"A 9.5 kW shower draws 41.3 A. It is fed by 18 m of 10 mm² twin and earth, voltage drop 4.4 mV/A/m. Is the voltage drop within 5%?","steps":["Formula: VD = (mV/A/m × Ib × L) ÷ 1000","Substitute: VD = (4.4 × 41.3 × 18) ÷ 1000","Result: VD = 3.27 V","Compare / conclusion: 3.27 V is below the 11.5 V (5%) limit, so it complies."],"answer":"3.27 V, complies"},{"q":"A lighting circuit in 1.5 mm² twin and earth (29 mV/A/m) is 35 m long and carries 4 A. Is it within the lighting limit?","steps":["Formula: VD = (mV/A/m × Ib × L) ÷ 1000","Substitute: VD = (29 × 4 × 35) ÷ 1000","Result: VD = 4.06 V","Compare / conclusion: the lighting limit is 3% of 230 V = 6.9 V. 4.06 V is below it, so it complies."],"answer":"4.06 V, complies"},{"q":"A ring final in 2.5 mm² twin and earth (18 mV/A/m) is 60 m round. A 20 A load is plugged in near the middle of the ring. What is the voltage drop?","steps":["Formula: VD ring = (mV/A/m × Ib × L) ÷ 1000 ÷ 4","Substitute: VD = (18 × 20 × 60) ÷ 1000 ÷ 4","Result: VD = 5.4 V","Compare / conclusion: 5.4 V is below 11.5 V. Without the ÷ 4 you'd get 21.6 V and wrongly fail it."],"answer":"5.4 V"}],"keys":["VD = mV/A/m × Ib × L ÷ 1000","Limits from a public supply: 6.9 V lighting, 11.5 V other","Length is one way; don't double it","No × 1.2 on tabulated mV/A/m","Ring with load at the middle: ÷ 4"],"ref":"Regulation 525; Appendix 4"},{"id":"L-adiab","topic":"Adiabatic","title":"Adiabatic: can the CPC take it?","mins":6,"body":"When a fault happens, a big current flows through the circuit protective conductor for a short time until the breaker or fuse opens. The adiabatic equation checks the CPC doesn't overheat and damage its insulation in that time. \"Adiabatic\" just means we assume none of the heat escapes during the fault: all of it stays in the copper.\n**The formula**\nS = √(I² × t) ÷ k\n- S: minimum cross-sectional area of the protective conductor in mm².\n- I: fault current in amps. Usually I = Uo ÷ Zs, with Uo = 230 V.\n- t: disconnection time in seconds for that fault current, read off the device's time/current curve.\n- k: a factor for the conductor material and its insulation. For a copper CPC that is a core in 70 °C PVC cable (the earth in twin and earth), k = 115. For a separate single-core copper CPC in 70 °C PVC, not bunched with other cables, k = 143.\nOrder of work on the calculator: square I, times t, square root, then divide by k.\n**Turning it round**\nTo find the longest time a given CPC can stand: t = (k × S)² ÷ I². Compare this with the device's disconnection time.\n**Where t comes from**\nFor an MCB, a fault current well above the instantaneous trip level (5 × In for B type) clears very fast. Exam questions often tell you to use 0.1 s in that case. For fuses read the curve; that's where longer times come in.\n**Exam traps**\n- Forgetting the square root, or square-rooting only I² and not I² × t.\n- Using kA as amps: 1.5 kA is 1500 A.\n- Picking the wrong k. The earth inside T&E is 115, not 143.\n- Using the circuit line conductor size. The question is about the CPC.\n- Rounding the answer down. If S works out at 8.25 mm², you need the next standard size up: 10 mm².\n**Sanity check**\nFor normal domestic final circuits on MCBs the answer usually comes out well under 1.5 mm². If you get 50 mm² for a lighting circuit, check your t or the order you pressed the buttons.","worked":[{"q":"A B32 circuit wired in 2.5/1.5 mm² twin and earth has Zs = 1.01 Ω, Uo = 230 V. The MCB clears this fault in 0.1 s. k = 115. Is the 1.5 mm² CPC big enough?","steps":["Formula: I = Uo ÷ Zs; S = √(I² × t) ÷ k","Substitute: I = 230 ÷ 1.01 = 227.7 A; S = √(227.7² × 0.1) ÷ 115","Result: S = 0.63 mm²","Compare / conclusion: 0.63 mm² is below the 1.5 mm² fitted, so the CPC is adequate."],"answer":"0.63 mm² needed, 1.5 mm² OK"},{"q":"A submain has a fault current of 1.5 kA and the fuse takes 0.4 s to clear it. Find the minimum CPC size if it is a core in the PVC cable (k = 115), and if it is a separate single-core PVC conductor (k = 143).","steps":["Formula: S = √(I² × t) ÷ k","Substitute: S = √(1500² × 0.4) ÷ 115 and √(1500² × 0.4) ÷ 143","Result: S = 8.25 mm² (k 115); S = 6.63 mm² (k 143)","Compare / conclusion: both round up to the next standard size, 10 mm²."],"answer":"10 mm² in both cases"},{"q":"A 2.5 mm² copper CPC in PVC cable (k = 115) carries a fault current of 800 A. What is the longest time it can carry this before overheating?","steps":["Formula: t = (k × S)² ÷ I²","Substitute: t = (115 × 2.5)² ÷ 800²","Result: t = 0.129 s","Compare / conclusion: the device must clear the fault in under about 0.13 s. An MCB operating instantly would be fine."],"answer":"About 0.13 s"}],"keys":["S = √(I²t) ÷ k","I = Uo ÷ Zs, with Uo = 230 V","k = 115 for copper core in 70 °C PVC cable; 143 for separate PVC single core","Always round S up to the next standard size","t = (kS)² ÷ I² to check how long a CPC can hold"],"ref":"Regulation 543.1.3; Tables 54.2 and 54.3"},{"id":"L-zs","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","title":"Zs by hand","mins":7,"body":"Zs is the resistance (strictly impedance) of the whole loop an earth fault current travels round: out from the transformer on the line, through the fault, back on the CPC and the supply earth. Low enough Zs means enough current flows to trip the device fast. Too high and the breaker sits there while the metalwork is live.\n**The formulas**\n- Zs = Ze + (R1 + R2)\n- Ze: the external loop impedance, the supply side only. The maximum values DNOs typically declare are 0.35 Ω for TN-C-S and 0.8 Ω for TN-S; the real measured Ze is often lower, and exams often use these maxima.\n- R1 + R2: resistance of the line conductor plus the CPC for the circuit.\n- R1 + R2 from length = (mΩ/m × L × 1.2) ÷ 1000. mΩ/m is the combined line and CPC figure per metre at 20 °C, e.g. 2.5/1.5 T&E = 19.51, 1.5/1.0 = 30.20, 1.0/1.0 = 36.20, 4/1.5 = 16.71, 6/2.5 = 10.49, 10/4 = 6.44. The × 1.2 takes it to 70 °C working temperature.\n**Where the maximum comes from**\nZs max = Cmin × Uo ÷ Ia\n- Cmin = 0.95 (allows for the supply running a bit low).\n- Uo = 230 V.\n- Ia = current that makes the device trip in the required time. For MCBs and RCBOs: B type 5 × In, C type 10 × In, D type 20 × In.\nSo B32: 0.95 × 230 ÷ 160 = 1.37 Ω. B6: 7.28 Ω. C20: 1.09 Ω. These match Table 41.3, so you can work them out if your memory goes blank.\n**Measured versus calculated**\nYour tester measures at room temperature, around 20 °C, so readings look better than the circuit will be when hot. Quick rule: a measured Zs should be no more than 80% of the table maximum. If a reading fails the 80% check, don't fail it straight away. Correct it properly: Ze + (measured R1 + R2 × 1.2).\n**Exam traps**\n- Forgetting × 1.2 when working from cable length.\n- Forgetting ÷ 1000 (mΩ to Ω).\n- Using the wrong multiplier: C type is 10 × In, not 5.\n- Rings: R1 + R2 for a ring is (r1 + r2) ÷ 4, not the end-to-end figure.\n**Sanity check**\nDomestic final circuit Zs is usually somewhere from 0.4 to 1.5 Ω. R1 + R2 for a normal circuit is well under 1 Ω unless it's a long lighting run.","worked":[{"q":"A B32 radial is wired in 2.5/1.5 mm² twin and earth (19.51 mΩ/m at 20 °C), 28 m long. Ze = 0.35 Ω. Use 1.2 for temperature. Does it comply with the 1.37 Ω maximum?","steps":["Formula: R1 + R2 = mΩ/m × L × 1.2 ÷ 1000; Zs = Ze + (R1 + R2)","Substitute: R1 + R2 = 19.51 × 28 × 1.2 ÷ 1000; Zs = 0.35 + R1 + R2","Result: R1 + R2 = 0.656 Ω; Zs = 1.01 Ω","Compare / conclusion: 1.01 Ω is below 1.37 Ω, so it complies."],"answer":"1.01 Ω, complies"},{"q":"A B6 lighting circuit in 1.5/1.0 mm² twin and earth (30.20 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is supplied from a TN-S system with Ze = 0.8 Ω. Max Zs for a B6 is 7.28 Ω. Using × 1.2, what is the maximum length before Zs is exceeded?","steps":["Formula: L max = (Zs max − Ze) ÷ (mΩ/m × 1.2 ÷ 1000)","Substitute: L max = (7.28 − 0.8) ÷ (30.20 × 1.2 ÷ 1000) = 6.48 ÷ 0.03624","Result: L max = 178.8 m","Compare / conclusion: about 179 m. On lighting, voltage drop will usually limit the length before Zs does."],"answer":"About 179 m"},{"q":"A C20 circuit (max Zs 1.09 Ω) has Ze = 0.35 Ω and measured R1 + R2 = 0.57 Ω at about 20 °C, giving measured Zs 0.92 Ω. Check it with the 80% rule, then correct properly with × 1.2.","steps":["Formula: 80% limit = 0.8 × Zs max; corrected Zs = Ze + (R1 + R2) × 1.2","Substitute: 0.8 × 1.09; corrected Zs = 0.35 + 0.57 × 1.2","Result: 80% limit = 0.87 Ω; corrected Zs = 1.03 Ω","Compare / conclusion: 0.92 Ω fails the 80% quick check, but the corrected 1.03 Ω is under 1.09 Ω, so it complies. It is tight, so note it."],"answer":"Complies: corrected Zs 1.03 Ω"}],"keys":["Zs = Ze + (R1 + R2)","Zs max = 0.95 × 230 ÷ Ia; Ia = 5, 10, 20 × In for B, C, D","B32 1.37 Ω, B6 7.28 Ω, C20 1.09 Ω","From length: mΩ/m × L × 1.2 ÷ 1000","Measured Zs quick check: no more than 80% of the table value"],"ref":"Table 41.3; Regulation 411.4"},{"id":"L-pfc","topic":"Fault current","title":"Prospective fault current","mins":5,"body":"Prospective fault current (PFC) is the biggest current that could flow if there was a dead short at that point. You need it for two reasons: every protective device must be able to break that current safely, and there must be enough of it to trip the device fast.\n**The two kinds**\n- Prospective earth fault current (PEFC): line to earth. Uses the earth loop impedance: I = Uo ÷ Ze at the origin.\n- Prospective short-circuit current (PSCC): line to neutral. Uses the line-neutral loop impedance: I = Uo ÷ Zpn.\nRecord the higher of the two as the PFC on the certificate. Your tester does both; in the exam you do them with Ohm's law.\n**The formula**\nI = Uo ÷ Z\n- Uo = 230 V (line to earth voltage).\n- Z = the loop impedance in ohms, Ze for earth faults at the origin or Zs at the end of a circuit.\n**Three-phase**\nFor a three-phase supply, a common rule of thumb is that the three-phase fault current is about twice the single-phase line-neutral value. Don't just stick 400 V into I = V ÷ Z with the single-phase loop: that gives the wrong answer.\n**Breaking capacity**\nEach device has a rated short-circuit capacity, printed on it, e.g. 6 kA or 10 kA for MCBs. The PFC at that point must not be higher than this (unless it's backed up by an upstream device the manufacturer allows).\n**Fault current at the end of a circuit**\nSame formula with Zs. Compare with Ia, the current needed for instant trip: 5 × In for B type. If fault current is bigger than Ia, it trips within the time allowed.\n**Exam traps**\n- Mixing up kA and A. 4600 A = 4.6 kA.\n- Using Zs when the question wants PFC at the origin (use Ze or Zpn).\n- Using 400 V on a single-phase loop.\n- Recording the lower of PEFC and PSCC instead of the higher.\n**Sanity check**\nLower impedance means higher current. Domestic PFCs usually sit somewhere from a few hundred amps up to a few kA. Near the transformer it can be much higher.","worked":[{"q":"At the origin of a TN-C-S supply, Ze = 0.35 Ω and the line-neutral loop impedance Zpn = 0.28 Ω. Uo = 230 V. What PFC should be recorded?","steps":["Formula: PEFC = Uo ÷ Ze; PSCC = Uo ÷ Zpn","Substitute: PEFC = 230 ÷ 0.35; PSCC = 230 ÷ 0.28","Result: PEFC = 657 A; PSCC = 821 A","Compare / conclusion: record the higher value, 821 A (about 0.82 kA)."],"answer":"821 A (≈0.82 kA)"},{"q":"Near a substation, a single-phase Zpn of 0.05 Ω is measured at 230 V. The three-phase board is fitted with 6 kA MCBs. Estimate the three-phase fault current using the 2 × single-phase rule and say whether the MCBs are suitable.","steps":["Formula: PSCC = Uo ÷ Zpn; three-phase ≈ 2 × PSCC","Substitute: PSCC = 230 ÷ 0.05; three-phase ≈ 2 × 4600","Result: single-phase = 4600 A; three-phase ≈ 9200 A (9.2 kA)","Compare / conclusion: 9.2 kA is above the 6 kA rating, so 6 kA MCBs aren't suitable on their own. Use 10 kA devices, or back-up protection the manufacturer approves."],"answer":"About 9.2 kA; 6 kA devices unsuitable"},{"q":"A B32 circuit has Zs = 1.01 Ω at 230 V. What is the earth fault current at the end of the circuit, and will the MCB trip instantly (Ia = 5 × In)?","steps":["Formula: If = Uo ÷ Zs; Ia = 5 × In","Substitute: If = 230 ÷ 1.01; Ia = 5 × 32","Result: If = 227.7 A; Ia = 160 A","Compare / conclusion: 228 A is above 160 A, so the MCB trips in its instantaneous region."],"answer":"≈228 A, trips instantly"}],"keys":["I = Uo ÷ Z with Uo = 230 V","PEFC uses Ze, PSCC uses Zpn; record the higher","Three-phase fault current is roughly 2 × single-phase PSCC","Device breaking capacity must be at least the PFC","Fault current above 5 × In trips a B type instantly"],"ref":"Regulation 434.1"},{"id":"L-tt","topic":"TT systems","title":"TT: electrodes and RCDs","mins":6,"body":"On a TT system the supplier gives you no earth. Your earth comes from an electrode in the ground at the property: a rod, mostly. Soil resistance is high compared with a copper cable, so the fault current is far too small to trip an MCB. That's why TT installations rely on RCDs for fault protection.\n**The formula**\nRA × IΔn ≤ 50 V\n- RA: resistance of the earth electrode plus the protective conductors connecting it (in practice, your measured electrode resistance).\n- IΔn: rated residual operating current of the RCD, in amps (30 mA = 0.03 A).\n- 50 V: the highest touch voltage allowed to stay on exposed metalwork.\nRearranged: RA max = 50 ÷ IΔn.\n- 30 mA RCD: 50 ÷ 0.03 = 1667 Ω\n- 100 mA RCD: 50 ÷ 0.1 = 500 Ω\n- 300 mA RCD: 50 ÷ 0.3 = 167 Ω\n**The real-world limit**\nThe sum can allow a huge figure, but guidance notes that an electrode reading above about 200 Ω may not be stable, because soil dries out in summer and freezes in winter. On site aim well under that.\n**Disconnection times**\nFor TT final circuits at 230 V, Table 41.1 requires 0.2 s. Table 41.1 covers final circuits up to 63 A with one or more socket-outlets and up to 32 A supplying only fixed equipment (Regulation 411.3.2.2); TT distribution circuits and other circuits are allowed 1 s. A working RCD trips much faster than 0.2 s at its rated current.\n**Measuring it**\n- Earth electrode tester, three terminal method: current spike well away, potential spike placed between; take readings with the potential spike moved either side of its position to check they agree. About 62% of the electrode-to-current-spike distance is the classic position.\n- Earth loop tester: isolate the installation, disconnect the earthing conductor from the MET, then carry out the loop test (it is a live test, so treat the disconnected conductor and all metalwork as potentially live). Reconnect the earthing conductor before putting the installation back into service. On TT this reading is mainly the electrode.\n**Exam traps**\n- Using mA without converting: 30 mA is 0.03 A. Using 30 gives a silly answer.\n- Using the MCB to meet TT fault protection. It won't trip on a 100 Ω fault.\n- Forgetting the RCD at the origin might be time-delayed (S type) for discrimination.\n**Sanity check**\nTouch voltage = RA × IΔn. If that product comes out above 50 V, it fails. Electrode readings are usually tens to low hundreds of ohms.","worked":[{"q":"What is the maximum electrode resistance allowed by RA × IΔn ≤ 50 V for a 30 mA RCD and for a 100 mA RCD?","steps":["Formula: RA max = 50 ÷ IΔn","Substitute: 50 ÷ 0.03; 50 ÷ 0.1","Result: 1667 Ω for 30 mA; 500 Ω for 100 mA","Compare / conclusion: these are the calculation limits, but a reading above about 200 Ω should be treated as possibly unstable."],"answer":"1667 Ω and 500 Ω"},{"q":"A TT installation has a measured electrode resistance of 120 Ω, protected at the origin by a 100 mA S-type RCD. Does it meet RA × IΔn ≤ 50 V?","steps":["Formula: touch voltage = RA × IΔn","Substitute: 120 × 0.1","Result: 12 V","Compare / conclusion: 12 V is below 50 V, so it complies."],"answer":"12 V, complies"},{"q":"An outbuilding on a TT system is protected by a 300 mA RCD. The electrode measures 180 Ω. Does it comply?","steps":["Formula: touch voltage = RA × IΔn","Substitute: 180 × 0.3","Result: 54 V","Compare / conclusion: 54 V is above 50 V, so it fails. The limit with 300 mA is 167 Ω. Improve the electrode or use a lower IΔn RCD."],"answer":"54 V, fails"}],"keys":["RA × IΔn ≤ 50 V","RA max: 1667 Ω (30 mA), 500 Ω (100 mA), 167 Ω (300 mA)","Above about 200 Ω may not be stable","TT final circuits (up to 63 A with sockets, 32 A fixed only): 0.2 s; distribution circuits 1 s","Convert mA to A before multiplying"],"ref":"Regulation 411.5.3; Table 41.1"},{"id":"L-ring","topic":"Ring final testing","title":"Ring final testing in three steps","mins":7,"body":"The ring test proves the ring is actually a ring: no breaks, no interconnections, no bridges with a spur hanging off wrongly. It also gives you R1 + R2 for the circuit.\n**Step 1: End to end**\nMeasure each conductor from one end to the other at the board: r1 (line), rn (neutral), r2 (CPC).\n- r1 and rn should be within 0.05 Ω of each other (same size conductors).\n- In 2.5/1.5 T&E, r2 is bigger than r1, about 1.67 times, because the CPC is thinner (2.5 ÷ 1.5 ≈ 1.67).\n**Step 2: Cross-connect line and neutral**\nJoin the line of one end to the neutral of the other end, and vice versa. Then at every socket measure line to neutral. Each reading should be about the same, roughly (r1 + rn) ÷ 4.\n**Step 3: Cross-connect line and CPC**\nSame again with line and CPC. At every socket measure line to earth. Each reading should be about (r1 + r2) ÷ 4. The highest reading is the circuit's R1 + R2, which also proves polarity at each socket.\n**Why ÷ 4?**\nWith the ends cross-connected, any socket sits on two parallel paths. At the midpoint each path is half the loop (r1 + rn) ÷ 2, and two equal halves in parallel is half of that again: ÷ 4. Wherever you are on the ring, the result works out the same, which is why readings should all be similar.\n**What the readings tell you**\n- Readings rising towards the middle and falling at the ends: you've joined the line and neutral of the same end instead of opposite ends. Swap the cross-connection.\n- A single socket much higher: a spur off it, or a poor connection.\n- Infinite reading end to end: a break.\n**Exam traps**\n- Forgetting ÷ 4 and quoting the end-to-end value as R1 + R2.\n- Using (r1 + rn) ÷ 4 for the Zs sum. Zs uses (r1 + r2) ÷ 4.\n- Forgetting to add Ze to get Zs.\n**Sanity check**\nFor 2.5 mm², r1 ≈ 7.41 mΩ per metre. Divide r1 by 0.00741 and you get the ring length. If that comes out as 700 m, you've made a mistake.","worked":[{"q":"A 2.5/1.5 ring gives end-to-end readings r1 = 0.52 Ω, rn = 0.52 Ω, r2 = 0.85 Ω. What should the socket readings be at step 2 and step 3, and what is Zs if Ze = 0.35 Ω?","steps":["Formula: step 2 ≈ (r1 + rn) ÷ 4; step 3 ≈ (r1 + r2) ÷ 4; Zs = Ze + (R1 + R2)","Substitute: (0.52 + 0.52) ÷ 4; (0.52 + 0.85) ÷ 4; Zs = 0.35 + 0.3425","Result: step 2 ≈ 0.26 Ω; step 3 ≈ 0.343 Ω (0.34); Zs ≈ 0.69 Ω","Compare / conclusion: r1 and rn are within 0.05 Ω, and Zs 0.69 Ω is well below 80% of 1.37 Ω (1.10 Ω). Pass."],"answer":"0.26 Ω, 0.34 Ω, Zs ≈ 0.69 Ω"},{"q":"On the same ring, r1 = 0.52 Ω. The 2.5 mm² line conductor is 7.41 mΩ/m and the 1.5 mm² CPC is 12.10 mΩ/m. Estimate the ring length and check r2 = 0.85 Ω is sensible.","steps":["Formula: length = r1 ÷ (mΩ/m ÷ 1000); expected r2 = length × 12.10 ÷ 1000","Substitute: 0.52 ÷ 0.00741; expected r2 = 70.2 × 0.0121","Result: length ≈ 70.2 m; expected r2 ≈ 0.85 Ω","Compare / conclusion: the measured 0.85 Ω matches, so the CPC ring is complete."],"answer":"About 70 m; r2 matches"},{"q":"At step 2 on that ring, every socket reads 0.26 Ω except one, which reads 0.33 Ω. A socket is spurred off it in 5 m of 2.5 mm² (7.41 mΩ/m) and you tested at the spur socket. Does the spur explain the extra?","steps":["Formula: extra = (line + neutral of the spur) = 2 × mΩ/m × L ÷ 1000","Substitute: extra = 2 × 7.41 × 5 ÷ 1000","Result: extra = 0.074 Ω; 0.26 + 0.074 = 0.33 Ω","Compare / conclusion: this matches the 0.33 Ω reading, so the higher reading is the spur, not a fault."],"answer":"Yes, the 5 m spur adds ≈0.07 Ω"}],"keys":["r1 and rn within 0.05 Ω end to end","Step 2: each socket ≈ (r1 + rn) ÷ 4","Step 3: each socket ≈ (r1 + r2) ÷ 4; highest = R1 + R2","Zs = Ze + (r1 + r2) ÷ 4","Readings rising to the middle: wrong cross-connection"],"ref":"GN3 (ring final circuit continuity)"},{"id":"L-ir","topic":"Insulation resistance","title":"Insulation resistance","mins":6,"body":"Insulation resistance (IR) testing checks that the insulation between conductors, and between conductors and earth, is sound. You put a high DC voltage across it and measure the tiny leakage. Big number good, small number bad.\n**Test voltages and minimums (Table 64)**\n- SELV and PELV circuits: test at 250 V DC, minimum 0.5 MΩ.\n- Circuits up to 500 V (including 230 V and 400 V): test at 500 V DC, minimum 1 MΩ.\n- Circuits above 500 V: test at 1000 V DC, minimum 1 MΩ.\nWhere surge protective devices or other equipment can't be disconnected, the test voltage can be dropped to 250 V DC, but the minimum is still 1 MΩ.\n**Doing the test**\n1. Isolate and prove dead. Lock off.\n2. Disconnect or unplug anything that will be damaged or will spoil the reading: electronics, dimmers, SPDs, lamps.\n3. Test between live conductors (line to neutral), and between live conductors and earth (line and neutral joined, to earth).\n4. On a whole board you can test the circuits together, then split them up if the combined reading is low.\n**Parallel paths**\nCircuits tested together are in parallel, so the total is lower than any one circuit: 1/Rt = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3. A whole board reading lower than each circuit is normal.\n**Length**\nLonger cable means more insulation in parallel, so lower readings. IR is roughly inversely proportional to length: double the length, half the reading.\n**Exam traps**\n- Testing a 230 V circuit at 250 V. It should be 500 V.\n- Remembering the old 0.5 MΩ minimum for 230 V circuits. It's 1 MΩ now.\n- Adding parallel readings instead of using reciprocals.\n- Only testing line to earth. Neutral to earth faults are just as real, so for the live-to-earth test join line and neutral together, or test each separately.\n- Doing the test with the main switch on or the supply not isolated. IR is a dead test.\n**Sanity check**\nNew wiring often reads hundreds of MΩ, or off the scale (over the meter's range). A reading just above 1 MΩ passes the regs, but on new work it's a warning sign, not a comfortable pass.","worked":[{"q":"Three circuits are tested together at 500 V. Individually they read 200 MΩ, 50 MΩ and 100 MΩ. What would the combined reading be?","steps":["Formula: 1/Rt = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3","Substitute: 1/Rt = 1/200 + 1/50 + 1/100 = 0.005 + 0.02 + 0.01 = 0.035","Result: Rt = 1 ÷ 0.035 = 28.57 MΩ","Compare / conclusion: lower than the smallest circuit (50 MΩ), as expected for parallel paths. Well above 1 MΩ, so it passes."],"answer":"28.57 MΩ"},{"q":"A 50 m cable reads 400 MΩ on IR. Assuming the same cable and condition, what would a 200 m run be expected to read?","steps":["Formula: IR is inversely proportional to length: IR2 = IR1 × L1 ÷ L2","Substitute: IR2 = 400 × 50 ÷ 200","Result: IR2 = 100 MΩ","Compare / conclusion: four times the length, a quarter of the reading. Still a healthy pass."],"answer":"100 MΩ"},{"q":"What test voltage and minimum IR apply to (a) a 230 V socket circuit and (b) a SELV lighting circuit?","steps":["Formula: from Table 64 by circuit voltage","Substitute: (a) 230 V is up to 500 V; (b) SELV","Result: (a) 500 V DC, minimum 1 MΩ; (b) 250 V DC, minimum 0.5 MΩ","Compare / conclusion: don't use 250 V on the socket circuit just because it's safer for kit. Only drop to 250 V for SPDs and the like, keeping the 1 MΩ minimum."],"answer":"(a) 500 V, 1 MΩ; (b) 250 V, 0.5 MΩ"}],"keys":["230/400 V circuits: 500 V DC test, minimum 1 MΩ","SELV/PELV: 250 V DC, minimum 0.5 MΩ; above 500 V: 1000 V, 1 MΩ","Circuits together are in parallel: reciprocals","Longer cable, lower IR","Disconnect electronics and SPDs first"],"ref":"Table 64; Regulation 643.3"},{"id":"L-rcd","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","title":"RCDs and polarity","mins":6,"body":"An RCD compares the current going out on the line with the current coming back on the neutral. If some goes missing (through a person or down the CPC) and it reaches the trip level, it opens. Testing proves it trips fast enough.\n**Trip times**\nFor general (non-delayed) RCDs to BS EN 61008 and BS EN 61009:\n- At IΔn (e.g. 30 mA): must trip within 300 ms.\n- At 5 × IΔn (e.g. 150 mA for a 30 mA device): must trip within 40 ms.\nFor S type (time-delayed) RCDs:\n- At IΔn: between 130 ms and 500 ms.\n- At 5 × IΔn: between 50 ms and 150 ms.\nBS 7671:2018 only requires the test at IΔn. The 5 × test is no longer required, but many electricians and testers still do it and it can still come up in exam questions. Test on both the 0° and 180° half-cycles and record the longer time. Then press the test button to check the mechanism.\n**Where 30 mA additional protection is needed**\nSocket-outlets rated up to 32 A (Regulation 411.3.3), mobile equipment up to 32 A for use outdoors, final circuits supplying luminaires in domestic premises (411.3.4), and cables concealed in walls or partitions less than 50 mm deep where not otherwise protected (522.6.202/203), among others. That's why most domestic circuits end up on 30 mA devices.\n**Nuisance tripping**\nNormal equipment leaks a little to earth. Regulation 531.3.2 requires you to keep the total protective conductor current on one RCD to no more than 30% of IΔn: 9 mA on a 30 mA device. Too many circuits on one RCD adds up.\n**Polarity**\nPolarity checks that:\n- Every single-pole switch, fuse and breaker is in the line conductor, not the neutral.\n- The centre contact of Edison screw lampholders is on the line (except E14 and E27 lampholders to BS EN 60238, where it doesn't matter).\n- Sockets are wired the right way round.\nIt's done dead during R1 + R2 (the reading at each point with line and CPC linked proves which is which), and confirmed live at the origin.\n**Exam traps**\n- Mixing up 300 ms and 40 ms.\n- Using the general times for an S type.\n- Forgetting to multiply: 5 × 30 mA = 150 mA, not 35 mA.\n- Thinking a switched neutral is fine because the lamp goes off. The lamp is still live.\n**Sanity check**\nA healthy 30 mA RCD usually trips in a few tens of milliseconds at IΔn, far quicker than the 300 ms limit. A reading close to the limit is worth noting.","worked":[{"q":"A 30 mA general-type RCBO trips in 28 ms at IΔn and 19 ms at 5 × IΔn. What test current is used for the 5 × test, and does it pass?","steps":["Formula: 5 × test current = 5 × IΔn; limits 300 ms at IΔn, 40 ms at 5 × IΔn","Substitute: 5 × 30 mA","Result: 150 mA test current; times 28 ms and 19 ms","Compare / conclusion: 28 ms is under 300 ms and 19 ms is under 40 ms. Pass."],"answer":"150 mA; passes"},{"q":"A 100 mA S-type RCD at the origin of a TT installation trips in 210 ms at IΔn and 95 ms at 5 × IΔn. Does it pass?","steps":["Formula: S type limits: 130–500 ms at IΔn; 50–150 ms at 5 × IΔn","Substitute: 210 ms against 130–500 ms; 95 ms against 50–150 ms","Result: both inside their windows","Compare / conclusion: pass. Using the general 300 ms / 40 ms limits by mistake would wrongly fail the 5 × result."],"answer":"Passes"},{"q":"Six circuits are on one 30 mA RCD. Each has about 4 mA of protective conductor current from its equipment. Is this within the 30% of IΔn guidance?","steps":["Formula: total leakage = number of circuits × leakage each; limit = 0.3 × IΔn","Substitute: 6 × 4 mA; 0.3 × 30 mA","Result: total = 24 mA; limit = 9 mA","Compare / conclusion: 24 mA is well over 9 mA. Expect nuisance tripping; split the circuits across more RCDs or RCBOs."],"answer":"No: 24 mA against a 9 mA guide"}],"keys":["General RCD: 300 ms at IΔn, 40 ms at 5 × IΔn","S type: 130–500 ms at IΔn, 50–150 ms at 5 × IΔn","Keep standing leakage to 30% of IΔn","Single-pole devices in the line only","ES lampholder centre contact to line (except E14/E27 to BS EN 60238)"],"ref":"Regulations 411.3.3 and 531.3.2; GN3"},{"id":"L-seq","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","title":"Test order and the right meter","mins":5,"body":"The order matters because each test relies on the one before. There's no point doing a loop test on a circuit with no CPC continuity; the metalwork could go live while you're testing.\n**The sequence (GN3)**\nDead tests first, supply isolated:\n1. Continuity of protective conductors, including main and supplementary bonding.\n2. Continuity of ring final circuit conductors.\n3. Insulation resistance.\n4. Polarity (done alongside continuity with the R1 + R2 method).\n5. Earth electrode resistance, where there is one (TT).\nWhere relevant, the dead tests also include protection by SELV, PELV or electrical separation, and insulation of non-conducting floors and walls.\nThen live tests:\n6. Earth fault loop impedance: Ze at the origin, Zs on circuits.\n7. Prospective fault current.\n8. RCD operation.\n9. Functional testing of switchgear and controls, plus checks like phase sequence and voltage drop where needed.\n**Why continuity first**\nIt proves the protective conductors exist before you rely on them. IR comes after continuity because IR assumes the circuit is correctly connected.\n**Instruments and their standards**\n- Low-resistance ohmmeter (continuity): BS EN 61557-4.\n- Insulation resistance tester: BS EN 61557-2.\n- Earth loop impedance tester: BS EN 61557-3.\n- Earth electrode resistance tester: BS EN 61557-5.\n- RCD tester: BS EN 61557-6.\nTest leads and probes should meet HSE GS38: fused where needed, shrouded, minimal exposed tip.\n**Nulling and wander leads**\nThe meter reads the leads too. Null them (zero them) before continuity testing, or subtract the lead resistance. For a long wander lead used to measure R2 or bonding, measure the lead on its own and take it off the reading.\n**Ze**\nMeasure Ze with the main earthing conductor disconnected from the MET, so parallel paths through bonding don't make it look better than it is. Isolate the installation before you disconnect it, do the live loop test at the supply side, then isolate again and reconnect the earthing conductor before the installation goes back into use.\n**Exam traps**\n- Putting IR before continuity.\n- Doing a live test before confirming CPC continuity.\n- Forgetting to subtract lead resistance.\n- Measuring Ze with bonding still connected.\n**Sanity check**\nA calculated Zs (Ze + R1 + R2) should be close to a measured Zs. Big differences point to parallel paths or a bad connection.","worked":[{"q":"Your continuity tester reads 0.84 Ω on R1 + R2 but you forgot to null the leads, which measure 0.12 Ω together. What is the true R1 + R2?","steps":["Formula: true reading = measured − lead resistance","Substitute: 0.84 − 0.12","Result: 0.72 Ω","Compare / conclusion: record 0.72 Ω. Leaving leads in overstates the circuit resistance."],"answer":"0.72 Ω"},{"q":"Using a wander lead to measure R2 on a long circuit, the reading is 1.35 Ω. The wander lead alone measures 0.95 Ω. What is R2?","steps":["Formula: R2 = reading − wander lead resistance","Substitute: 1.35 − 0.95","Result: R2 = 0.40 Ω","Compare / conclusion: the wander lead is most of the reading, which is why you must measure and deduct it."],"answer":"0.40 Ω"},{"q":"Measured Ze is 0.35 Ω and the corrected R1 + R2 from the previous nulling example is 0.72 Ω. What Zs would you expect to measure, and what would a live reading of 0.62 Ω suggest?","steps":["Formula: Zs = Ze + (R1 + R2)","Substitute: 0.35 + 0.72","Result: expected Zs ≈ 1.07 Ω","Compare / conclusion: a live reading of 0.62 Ω is much lower than expected. That points to parallel earth paths (bonded metalwork, pipes), not a better circuit. Trust the R1 + R2 method for proving the CPC."],"answer":"≈1.07 Ω; lower reading means parallel paths"}],"keys":["Dead tests first: continuity, ring, IR, polarity, electrode","Then live: Zs/Ze, PFC, RCD, functional","Null leads; deduct wander lead resistance","Measure Ze with the earthing conductor disconnected; isolate to disconnect and reconnect","Instruments to BS EN 61557; leads to GS38"],"ref":"GN3; Part 6"},{"id":"L-eicr","topic":"EICR coding","title":"Coding EICR observations","mins":5,"body":"An EICR doesn't say whether the installation meets the current edition. It says whether it's safe to keep using. The coding is how you tell the client how urgent each problem is.\n**The codes**\n- C1: Danger present. Risk of injury. Immediate remedial action required. You should make it safe there and then, or tell the person responsible in writing.\n- C2: Potentially dangerous. Urgent remedial action required. Not dangerous right now, but would be if a fault or another event happened.\n- C3: Improvement recommended. Doesn't meet the current regs but isn't dangerous.\n- FI: Further investigation required without delay. You've seen something that suggests a problem but couldn't get to the bottom of it during the inspection.\n**The overall result**\nAny C1, C2 or FI makes the report unsatisfactory. Only C3s (or nothing) can give satisfactory.\n**Well-established examples (Electrical Safety First Best Practice Guide 4)**\n- Accessible exposed live parts, e.g. a smashed socket front with terminals showing: C1.\n- No effective main protective bonding to incoming gas or water: C2.\n- Measured Zs too high for the device to disconnect in time, with no RCD on the circuit: C2.\n- Sockets likely to feed mobile equipment outdoors with no 30 mA RCD protection: C2.\n- Indoor sockets without RCD protection that are unlikely to supply mobile equipment outdoors: C3.\n- No identification of circuits at the consumer unit (no circuit chart or labels): C3.\n**The thinking**\nAsk yourself: \"If I walked away now, could someone get hurt straight away?\" That's C1. \"Could someone get hurt if something else went wrong?\" C2. \"It's older than current standards but it's safe?\" C3. \"I can't tell?\" FI.\n**Exam traps**\n- Coding something C2 just because it doesn't meet the 18th edition. An older installation built to its time and still safe gets C3 or nothing.\n- Giving an overall satisfactory with an FI in there.\n- Leaving a C1 without making it safe or telling the client in writing.\n- Using a code for a limitation. A limitation (couldn't lift the floor) goes in the limitations section, not as an FI, unless you've got a real reason to suspect a problem.\n**Sanity check**\nRead your codes back and imagine explaining each to the client. If you can't say why it's dangerous, it probably isn't C1 or C2.","worked":[{"q":"On an EICR, a B32 ring with no RCD protection measures Zs = 1.62 Ω. The Table 41.3 maximum for a B32 is 1.37 Ω. What is the outcome and how would you code it?","steps":["Formula: compare measured Zs with the Table 41.3 maximum (and, as a quick check, with 80% of it)","Substitute: 0.8 × 1.37; 1.62 against 1.10 and 1.37","Result: 80% limit = 1.10 Ω; 1.62 Ω is over both figures","Compare / conclusion: the MCB may not disconnect in time under an earth fault and there is no RCD to do it. Code C2; report unsatisfactory."],"answer":"Exceeds max; C2, unsatisfactory"},{"q":"During an EICR you find a socket in a hallway with a cracked faceplate exposing live terminals. How is it coded and what must you do?","steps":["Formula: is danger present now? Yes: exposed live parts can be touched","Substitute: accessible live parts = danger present","Result: C1","Compare / conclusion: make safe immediately (isolate or replace) or inform the person responsible in writing. Report unsatisfactory."],"answer":"C1, make safe"},{"q":"An EICR has three observations: C3 (no RCD on indoor sockets, older installation), C3 (no circuit chart), and FI (a circuit that trips intermittently for an unknown reason). What is the overall result?","steps":["Formula: any C1, C2 or FI = unsatisfactory","Substitute: C3, C3, FI","Result: an FI is present","Compare / conclusion: overall unsatisfactory, despite no C1 or C2."],"answer":"Unsatisfactory"}],"keys":["C1 danger now; C2 potentially dangerous; C3 improvement; FI investigate","Any C1, C2 or FI = unsatisfactory","Not meeting the current edition isn't automatically a code","No main bonding to gas/water: C2","Accessible live parts: C1"],"ref":"Chapter 65; ESF Best Practice Guide 4"},{"id":"L-fault","topic":"Fault finding","title":"Fault finding with numbers","mins":6,"body":"Fault finding in the exam is less about gut feeling and more about proving a theory with a reading. The same tests you do for certification are your tools. What changes is how you read the numbers.\n**A method that works**\n1. Get the symptoms: what happened, when, what's on the circuit.\n2. Look first: burnt accessories, crushed cables, water, recent work.\n3. Isolate and test dead where you can: continuity and IR.\n4. Split the circuit in half and test each half. Keep halving until you find the section with the fault. This is the half-split method and saves hours on long runs.\n5. Fix, then retest to prove it's gone.\n**Using resistance to find a fault**\nIf a line has shorted to the CPC somewhere along a run, the resistance you measure from one end, line to CPC, is the R1 + R2 of the cable up to the fault. Divide by the mΩ/m figure to get the distance:\nDistance = measured Ω ÷ (mΩ/m ÷ 1000)\nUse the 20 °C figure (you're measuring a cold cable), so no × 1.2.\n**Reading ring faults**\n- r1 or rn infinite end to end: a break in that conductor.\n- r1 and rn more than 0.05 Ω apart: a loose or high-resistance joint.\n- One socket reading much higher on the cross-connection tests: poor connection or spur at that point.\n**RCD tripping**\n- Trips straight away, one circuit only: IR test that circuit, both line and neutral to earth. A neutral to earth fault is a classic that only shows on an RCD.\n- Trips randomly, many circuits on one RCD: think leakage adding up. Total standing leakage should stay under 30% of IΔn.\n**Exam traps**\n- Applying × 1.2 when finding distance from a cold reading.\n- Testing line to earth only and missing a neutral to earth fault.\n- Swapping a breaker before you've proved the circuit is healthy.\n**Sanity check**\nThe distance you work out must be less than the circuit length. If the cable is 20 m and your answer is 40 m, you've used the wrong mΩ/m or forgotten a lead null.","worked":[{"q":"A 2.5/1.5 radial (19.51 mΩ/m at 20 °C) has a line to CPC short somewhere along its 25 m length. From the board, line to CPC measures 0.30 Ω (leads nulled). How far along is the fault?","steps":["Formula: distance = measured Ω ÷ (mΩ/m ÷ 1000)","Substitute: 0.30 ÷ 0.01951","Result: distance = 15.4 m","Compare / conclusion: about 15 m from the board. That's inside the 25 m run, so start looking there."],"answer":"About 15.4 m"},{"q":"A ring gives end-to-end readings r1 = 0.51 Ω, rn = open circuit, r2 = 0.84 Ω. What does this tell you?","steps":["Formula: compare r1, rn and r2; r1 and rn should be within 0.05 Ω","Substitute: r1 = 0.51, rn = infinite, r2 = 0.84 (about 1.65 × r1)","Result: line and CPC rings are complete; the neutral ring is broken","Compare / conclusion: look for a loose or missed neutral at a socket or joint. Don't carry on to the cross-connection tests until it's fixed."],"answer":"Break in the neutral ring"},{"q":"A 30 mA RCD trips every time one circuit is switched on. IR tests at 500 V give: line to earth 250 MΩ, neutral to earth 0.02 MΩ, line to neutral 300 MΩ. Minimum is 1 MΩ. What is the fault?","steps":["Formula: compare each reading with the 1 MΩ minimum","Substitute: 250 MΩ OK; 0.02 MΩ against 1 MΩ; 300 MΩ OK","Result: only neutral to earth fails","Compare / conclusion: a neutral to earth fault. It lets current return on the CPC, unbalancing the RCD. Half-split the circuit to find it."],"answer":"Neutral to earth fault"}],"keys":["Half-split the circuit to narrow the fault down","Distance to short = measured Ω ÷ (mΩ/m ÷ 1000), no × 1.2","Test neutral to earth too: it causes RCD trips","rn open end to end = break in neutral ring","Always retest after the fix"],"ref":"Fault finding"},{"id":"P-iso","topic":"AM2: Safe isolation & risk assessment","title":"Safe isolation and a risk assessment that actually fits the job","mins":7,"body":"Safe isolation is the section where one missed step can sink the whole task. It is marked yes/no against set criteria, and the safety-critical ones are not the kind you can make up for elsewhere. Check your centre's candidate pack for exactly how it is weighted, but assume the assessor is watching every step, in order, and that \"I always do that, I just forgot to say it\" earns nothing.\n**The single-phase sequence.** This follows the order NET publishes for the AM2/AM2E (identify, inform, isolate and lock off with a warning notice, prove the tester, test, re-prove). Say it out loud as you go if the centre allows – it keeps you in order.\n1. Identify the point of isolation and the circuit or equipment from the drawing and the labels.\n2. Inform the customer or person in control (on the day, the assessor) that you are isolating the supply.\n3. Isolate at a device suitable for isolation – main switch, isolator or circuit-breaker – not a functional switch or a light switch.\n4. Secure it: lock-off device, padlock, key in your pocket, and a danger/warning notice on the device.\n5. Select approved test equipment – a two-pole voltage indicator to HSE GS38: shrouded or spring-shrouded probes with minimal exposed tip, finger guards, sound leads, fused or current-limited – and prove it on the proving unit (or a known live source). No multimeters, no non-contact pens as the test for dead.\n6. Test at the point of work: L–N, L–E, N–E.\n7. Re-prove the voltage indicator on the proving unit.\n8. Only now is it dead. Anything you find later that was not tested, test before you touch.\n**The three-phase board.** Same bones, more tests. NET's procedure tests all ten combinations on the outgoing side of the isolator. Lock off the main switch or the upstream isolator, then test on the load side of it (outgoing terminals or busbar – whatever the brief points you at). The full 10-point test is:\n- L1–L2, L1–L3, L2–L3\n- L1–N, L2–N, L3–N\n- L1–E, L2–E, L3–E\n- N–E\nProve before, prove after. If the tester fails on the re-prove, every reading in between is worthless: get a working one, prove it, repeat all ten, re-prove. Do the tests in a fixed order every time so you never lose count. The N–E test is not a formality – a neutral can be live from a borrowed neutral or a second source, and that is the reading that finds it.\n**Securing properly.** One lock per person working. Keep the key on you – not on the board, not on the bench. The label says what, who and why: \"Danger – do not switch on – person working on this circuit\". If the device will not take a lock, the brief has usually told you what to use instead; if it has not, ask.\n**The risk assessment.** EAWR 1989 Reg 12 requires suitable means of cutting off the supply and isolating, Reg 13 requires adequate precautions to stop equipment made dead becoming charged while you work on it, and Reg 14 allows live work only where it is unreasonable for it to be dead, reasonable to work live, and suitable precautions are taken. The general duty to assess risk comes from the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999 (Reg 3). The written risk assessment is you showing you have thought about this bay, today. Generic \"electricity – be careful\" is exactly what gets marked down. Use three columns in your head: hazard, who might be harmed, control. Then residual risk, sign and date.\n- Electric shock from the board and adjacent live circuits – you, assessor, other candidates – isolate, lock off, prove dead, GS38 kit, barrier or cover adjacent live parts.\n- Cuts from sharp metal edges, conduit burrs, SWA armour – you – deburr, gloves when handling armour, tidy offcuts.\n- Slips and trips from cables, drums and tools on the floor – everyone in the area – keep the walkway clear, tools back in the bag.\n- Working from steps – you – check steps, three points of contact, no overreaching.\n- Swarf and dust from drilling and cutting – you and others – safety glasses, vacuum not blow.\nWrite it in the first few minutes of the slot, before you pick up a screwdriver. Short sentences, specific to what you can see.","worked":[{"q":"You complete all ten tests on an isolated three-phase board. On re-proving, the voltage indicator shows nothing on the proving unit.","steps":["Think: A failed re-prove means the tester might have been dead for all ten readings. None of them can be trusted.","Do: Stop. Tell the assessor. Get a replacement or new battery, prove it on the proving unit, repeat all ten tests, re-prove.","Check: Only when the before-and-after proves both pass do you treat the board as dead."],"answer":"Readings void – replace/repair tester, prove, repeat all 10 tests, re-prove."},{"q":"Board locked off at the main switch. Your N–E test shows about 230 V; every line test reads zero.","steps":["Think: The neutral is live from somewhere – borrowed neutral, a cross-connection with another circuit, or a second supply.","Do: Do not start work. Leave it locked off, report it to the assessor and treat the board as live.","Check: Find and isolate the source, then repeat the full 10-point test with before-and-after proving."],"answer":"Not safe – neutral is live from another source; stop and report, do not work."},{"q":"The brief says: 'Complete a risk assessment for isolating and working on the three-phase distribution board.' You have about ten minutes.","steps":["Think: Hazard, who, control – for this bay, today. Not a textbook list.","Do: Write 4–6 lines: shock from board/adjacent live bays, cuts from armour and burrs, trips, steps, swarf. For each, who is at risk and the specific control. Add residual risk, name, date and signature.","Check: Every hazard has a control you are actually going to carry out, and the page is signed and dated."],"answer":"Short site-specific RA: hazard – who – control, residual risk, signed and dated."}],"keys":["Identify – inform – isolate, lock and label – prove – test – re-prove, in that order, every time.","Three-phase with neutral is ten tests: three line-line, three line-N, three line-E, and N–E.","A failed re-prove voids every reading taken since the first prove.","Key stays in your pocket, not on the board.","GS38 two-pole voltage indicator for testing dead – not a multimeter or a non-contact pen.","Risk assessment: hazard, who, control – specific to the bay, signed and dated."],"ref":"NET AM2/AM2E safe isolation procedure (netservices.org.uk); HSE GS38; EAWR 1989 Regs 12, 13, 14; MHSWR 1999 Reg 3; BS 7671 Section 537 (isolation and switching); HSE HSG85"},{"id":"P-inst","topic":"AM2: Installation skills","title":"Installation skills – what the assessor is ticking","mins":9,"body":"The composite installation is the long day, and most of the marks are lost to small, avoidable things rather than to anything technically hard. Treat the drawing and spec as the contract: position, height, route, cable type, device, colour. If the drawing says 300 mm from the corner, it is 300 mm, not \"about there\". If something on the drawing looks wrong, ask – do not quietly improve it.\n**Plan before you drill.** Read the whole spec first and put your containment in before any cable. A sensible order is: back boxes and board positions, containment (conduit, trunking, clips), SWA run, then pull or fix cables, then terminate, then dress and label. Keep a running check against the drawing as you go rather than at the end.\n**SWA.** Cut the armour cleanly without nicking the inner sheath, fit the shroud onto the cable first (it is the thing people forget), then the gland body, spread the armour evenly over the cone, tighten so the armour is clamped but the bedding is not crushed. The earth tag (banjo) goes on the gland's entry thread and is clamped against the enclosure by the gland or its locknut (where it sits – inside or outside the enclosure – follows the spec or the manufacturer), with a green/yellow fly lead to the earth terminal. Pull the shroud over the finished gland. Whether the armour alone is the cpc or there is a separate core is set by the spec – follow it.\n**Conduit and trunking.** Measure twice, mark the bend, and check it against the drawing before cutting. Sets, double sets and bridge sets want to be in one plane and square – a twisted set is obvious from across the room. Cut square, ream every cut end, fit bushes and couplers at boxes, and use grommets or bushes on every entry into metal. Where metal conduit or trunking is used, it has to be electrically continuous and earthed – tight couplers, earth links across trunking joints where required, and a continuity test to prove it. Lids on, screws in, no gaps.\n**FP200 and fire-resistant circuits.** Score the sheath with the right tool, do not nick the cores, and keep the drain/cpc sleeved green/yellow. Use the gland type the spec calls for. Fixings must not let the cable fall in a fire – metal clips or ties, not plastic alone (BS 7671 Reg 521.10.202).\n**Data cabling.** Keep the twists right up to the termination – no more than about 13 mm (0.5 in) untwisted, the TIA-568 limit for Cat 5e and Cat 6. Pick T568A or T568B and use the same one at both ends; mixing them gives a crossover you did not mean to build. Punch down with the proper tool, keep the sheath close to the IDC, and do not kink or crush the cable. Keep data separated from power as the spec demands.\n**Termination and dressing.**\n- Strip to length: no exposed copper outside the terminal, no insulation trapped under the screw.\n- Tighten to the manufacturer's torque – a torque screwdriver on the board terminals shows you know.\n- Sleeve bare cpcs green/yellow at every termination.\n- Identify blue or black cores used as lines (switch wires, strappers) with brown sleeving or tape.\n- Double back small conductors in large terminals if the device allows, and never mix too many conductors under one screw.\n- Dress cores square in the board, grouped by circuit, with enough slack to re-terminate.\n- Label circuits in the board to match the schedule, and label the bonding clamps.\n**The mistakes that cost marks.**\n- Missing grommets, bushes or cable entries into metal without protection.\n- Copper showing at terminals, or a strand left out.\n- Wrong colours or unsleeved switch wires and cpcs.\n- Wrong circuit order in the board against the drawing.\n- Loose accessories, missing screws, lids off, back boxes not flush or not level.\n- Cable damaged by clips, or sheaths stripped back so far that singles are exposed outside an enclosure.\nFinish by walking the whole installation with the drawing in hand, like an inspector would.","worked":[{"q":"The drawing shows the SWA to the EV point gland entering the bottom of a metal enclosure. You have fitted the gland but the shroud is still on the bench.","steps":["Think: The shroud goes on the cable before the gland – it cannot be fitted after without dismantling.","Do: Back the gland off, fit the shroud over the cable, re-make the gland with the earth tag clamped at the entry as the spec shows, fit the fly lead to earth.","Check: Armour spread and clamped evenly, earth tag tight, shroud pulled down over the gland."],"answer":"Remake the gland with the shroud fitted first; banjo and fly lead to earth."},{"q":"You terminate a Cat 6 outlet as T568B. The patch panel at the other end has been punched as T568A.","steps":["Think: A-to-B swaps the orange and green pairs – a crossover, which is not what a standard permanent link should be.","Do: Re-terminate one end so both use the same scheme the spec asks for, keeping untwisting to a minimum.","Check: Wire map on a cable tester if available; visually confirm colour order against the label on the outlet."],"answer":"Both ends must use the same scheme – re-terminate one end."},{"q":"Metal trunking has been installed in two lengths joined with a coupler. The spec says the trunking must be earthed.","steps":["Think: Earth continuity across a joint is not guaranteed by a painted or loose coupler.","Do: Fit an earth link across the joint (if required by the system), tighten fixings, and earth the trunking at the board.","Check: Continuity test from the far end of the trunking to the main earthing terminal – low and stable."],"answer":"Bond across joints and prove continuity back to the MET."}],"keys":["The drawing is the contract – position, route, colours and circuit order all get checked.","Shroud on the cable first, earth tag clamped at the gland entry, fly lead to earth.","Ream every cut, bush and grommet every entry into metal.","Metal containment must be continuous and earthed – prove it with a test.","Data: minimal untwist (around 13 mm max), same scheme at both ends.","No exposed copper, cpcs sleeved, switch wires identified brown.","FP200 fixings must not fail in a fire – no plastic-only support."],"ref":"BS 7671 Reg 521.10.202 (premature collapse of wiring systems); BS 7671 Chapter 52 (selection and erection of wiring systems); BS 7671 Table 51 (conductor identification)"},{"id":"P-motor","topic":"AM2: Motor & control circuits","title":"Three-phase DOL starter with remote stop/start","mins":8,"body":"A DOL starter looks busy but it is two separate circuits sharing a box: the **main (power) circuit** that feeds the motor, and the **control circuit** that decides when the contactor coil is energised. Get them clear in your head and the wiring and the fault-finding both get easy.\n**Main circuit.** Supply L1, L2, L3 into the contactor main contacts (commonly marked 1/L1, 3/L2, 5/L3 in, 2/T1, 4/T2, 6/T3 out), then through the thermal overload relay, then out to the motor terminals U, V, W. Motor cpc to the motor earth terminal and to the starter enclosure. Set the overload to the full-load current on the motor rating plate – not the maximum on the dial.\n**Control circuit.** The coil (A1–A2) is fed either line–neutral or line–line depending on the coil voltage – check the rating printed on the coil. The logic runs like this:\n1. Supply (usually via a control fuse) to the first stop button.\n2. **Stop buttons in series**, normally closed. Any one of them opening kills the coil. Remote stop and local stop are just two links in the same chain.\n3. **Start buttons in parallel**, normally open. Any one of them closing energises the coil.\n4. **Retaining (hold-in) contact** – a normally open auxiliary contact on the contactor (commonly 13–14) wired in parallel with the start buttons. Once the contactor pulls in, this contact closes and keeps the coil fed after you let go of start.\n5. The overload's normally closed contact (commonly 95–96) in series with the coil, so an overload trip drops the contactor out.\n6. Coil A2 to neutral (or the other line for a 400 V coil).\n**Remote station wiring.** In the usual arrangement the remote station needs three control cores plus cpc: one takes the feed from the local stop output out to the remote stop; the second brings the remote stop output back to the starter, where it feeds the local start and the retaining contact (at the remote station it is also linked to the remote start); the third brings the remote start output back to the coil side of the start/retaining circuit. Both starts must be fed from after **all** the stops – if a start is fed from between the two stops, pressing it bypasses the stop downstream. Draw it before you wire it – a two-minute sketch saves an hour.\n**Commissioning checks.**\n- Before energising: continuity of the control circuit (press start – coil circuit makes; press any stop – it breaks), insulation resistance of the main circuit and motor windings to earth, cpc continuity to the motor frame.\n- Check phase sequence at the supply with a phase rotation tester.\n- Energise, press start, confirm hold-in, press each stop in turn, then each start in turn.\n- Check direction of rotation. If it is wrong, isolate and swap any two lines at the motor or starter output – never swap with it live.\n- Trip test: the overload test button (if fitted) should drop the contactor out.\n**Classic faults and what they tell you.**\n- Motor runs only while start is held: retaining contact missing, open, or wired to an NC contact.\n- Motor starts on its own as soon as power goes on: a start button wired on its NC contact, or the start bridged out.\n- One stop button does not stop it: that stop is in parallel instead of series, or bridged.\n- Motor hums, runs slow or trips on overload: lost phase (single-phasing) – a loose main connection or an open main contact.\n- Contactor chatters: low coil voltage, wrong coil voltage for the supply used, or a high-resistance joint in the control circuit.\n- Wrong rotation: sequence at the motor reversed – swap two lines.\nLabel control cores at both ends – numbered ferrules or markers make the fault-finding section (and the assessor's job) much easier.","worked":[{"q":"Motor starts from both local and remote start but stops as soon as you release the button.","steps":["Think: Start works, so the coil, stops and start buttons are fine. The only thing that keeps it in is the retaining contact.","Do: Isolate, prove dead, check the auxiliary contact used is NO (e.g. 13–14) and that it is in parallel with the start buttons.","Check: Re-energise, press start, release – contactor stays in; each stop drops it out."],"answer":"Retaining (hold-in) contact missing, open or wired NC – correct it."},{"q":"Pressing the remote stop does nothing; the local stop works.","steps":["Think: Stops must be in series. If one does nothing, it is either bridged or in parallel with the other.","Do: Isolate, prove dead, trace the remote station cores – the feed must go through the remote stop and on to the local stop, not be joined across it.","Check: Continuity test – with the coil circuit made via start, opening either stop must break it. Then a live functional test."],"answer":"Remote stop bridged or wired in parallel – rewire in series."},{"q":"On first start the motor turns the wrong way.","steps":["Think: Direction depends on phase sequence at the motor terminals.","Do: Isolate and lock off, prove dead, swap any two line conductors at the motor or starter outgoing terminals.","Check: Re-energise and confirm direction against the arrow on the motor or the brief."],"answer":"Swap any two lines (isolated), then recheck rotation."}],"keys":["Two circuits: main (power) and control (coil logic).","Stops NC in series; starts NO in parallel.","Retaining contact is NO and sits in parallel with the start buttons.","Overload NC contact in series with the coil; set to motor full-load current.","Remote stop/start normally needs three control cores plus cpc.","Wrong rotation: isolate, swap any two lines.","Check coil voltage (230 V or 400 V) before connecting it."],"ref":"BS 7671 Chapter 55 (Section 552 rotating machines); BS 7671 Reg 643.9 (check of phase sequence)"},{"id":"P-light","topic":"AM2: Lighting & heating controls","title":"Two-way, intermediate, key switch and S-plan heating","mins":8,"body":"Both lighting and heating controls are really about following one live conductor from supply to load and seeing what switches it on the way. If you can draw that path, you can wire it and you can fault-find it.\n**Two-way switching.** Each two-way switch has a common (C or COM) and two strapper terminals (L1 and L2). The feed (permanent line) goes to the common of switch one. Two strappers link L1 to L1 and L2 to L2 between the switches. The common of switch two is the switch line to the lamp. Neutral goes to the lamp, not through the switches.\n**Intermediate switching.** An intermediate switch sits in the strappers – two strappers in, two strappers out – and either passes them straight through or crosses them over. Two-way at each end, intermediates in between: for n control points you need two two-ways and (n − 2) intermediates. Check the terminal markings on the actual switch; the crossing pattern differs between makes.\n**Identification.** In singles, any core used as a line (feed, strappers, switch line) is brown. In multicore cable, blue or black cores used as lines get brown sleeving at every termination – the assessor will look.\n**Key switch.** A key switch is just a switch with a lock – it may be a one-way control, a two-way position, or a local override, depending on the spec. Wire it exactly as the drawing shows and test that the key actually controls what the brief says it does.\n**S-plan heating – the concept.** S-plan uses two two-port motorised zone valves: one for central heating (CH), one for hot water (HW). Each valve has a motor and an auxiliary (end) switch that closes when the valve is fully open.\n- The **programmer** gives two switched outputs: CH on and HW on.\n- **CH on** goes through the **room thermostat** to the CH zone valve motor.\n- **HW on** goes through the **cylinder thermostat** (its \"call for heat\" contact) to the HW zone valve motor.\n- Each valve's auxiliary switch is fed from a permanent line and its output (often orange on common valves) goes to the **boiler switched live and pump**. Either valve fully open fires the boiler and runs the pump.\n- Where the boiler needs a permanent live (for example for pump overrun), it and the neutral go direct from the fused supply, not through the valves – follow the boiler manufacturer's diagram.\nA **wiring centre** is just a labelled junction box that brings all of this together on numbered terminals. Draw your own terminal plan before you wire, and check the manufacturer's diagram for the actual valve colours – do not wire from memory. Typical two-port valve flying leads have a motor pair, a permanent line for the aux switch, the aux switch output and a cpc, but makes differ.\n**Solar thermal at a high level.** A solar thermal controller is a differential temperature controller: one sensor on the collector, one on the cylinder. When the collector is warmer than the cylinder by a set differential, it runs the solar pump; when the difference drops, it stops. Most also have a high-limit to stop overheating the cylinder. Electrically, you are usually giving it a fused, isolatable supply and wiring the pump and sensors to its terminals per the manufacturer.\n**Functional checks.**\n- Two-way/intermediate: operate each switch in turn, from every combination – the lamp must change state every time any switch is operated.\n- Key switch: key in, key out, both positions.\n- S-plan: CH on with stat calling – CH valve opens, boiler and pump run. Turn stat down – valve closes, boiler stops. Repeat for HW with the cylinder stat. Then both together.\n- Solar: if the brief allows, simulate the sensor condition or use the controller's manual/test mode to prove the pump output.","worked":[{"q":"Two-way and intermediate landing lighting: the lamp works from both two-way switches, but operating the intermediate never makes any difference.","steps":["Think: The intermediate either is not in the strappers at all, or the strappers are on the wrong terminals so it connects them the same way in both positions.","Do: Isolate and prove dead, then check the strappers go in on one pair of terminals and out on the other pair per the switch marking.","Check: Re-energise and run every switch combination – the lamp changes state every time."],"answer":"Intermediate mis-wired – strappers in and out on the wrong terminals."},{"q":"S-plan: with CH on and the room stat calling, the CH valve opens fully but the boiler and pump do not run.","steps":["Think: The valve motor is getting power, so the programmer and room stat are fine. Firing the boiler depends on the valve's aux switch.","Do: Isolate, prove dead, check the aux switch permanent line feed and that its output goes to the boiler switched live/pump terminal in the wiring centre.","Check: Re-test live – valve opens, aux switch makes, boiler and pump start."],"answer":"Aux (end) switch feed or output to boiler/pump not connected."},{"q":"HW valve opens whenever HW is on, even with the cylinder stat turned right down.","steps":["Think: The cylinder stat should break the HW-on feed to the valve motor when satisfied.","Do: Check which cylinder stat terminal is in use – it is probably wired to the wrong contact or bypassed in the wiring centre.","Check: Turn the stat up and down with HW on – valve should open and close."],"answer":"Cylinder stat bypassed or wired to the wrong contact."}],"keys":["Two-way: feed to common of switch one, strappers L1–L1 and L2–L2, common of switch two to lamp.","Intermediates go in the strappers; n points need two two-ways and n − 2 intermediates.","Every core used as a line is brown or sleeved brown.","S-plan: programmer → stat → valve motor; valve aux switch → boiler and pump.","Solar controller runs the pump on a temperature differential between collector and cylinder.","Functional test every switch in every combination."],"ref":"BS 7671 Table 51 (conductor identification); BS 7671 Reg 643.10 (functional testing); manufacturers' wiring diagrams for valves and controllers"},{"id":"P-test","topic":"AM2: Inspection, testing & certification","title":"Inspection, testing and the EIC","mins":9,"body":"In section B you are marked on your method and your paperwork as much as the readings. A correct figure in the wrong box, or a safe method done in the wrong order, can cost the tick. Follow the GN3 sequence and let the schedule of test results drive you column by column.\n**Inspect first.** Walk the job with the schedule of inspections: correct devices and ratings, cable sizes, identification, bonding, labels, IP ratings, barriers, fire-resistant fixings. Tick, cross or N/A every line.\n**Dead tests, in order.**\n1. Continuity of protective conductors – main bonding, cpcs (R1+R2 or R2), and any supplementary bonding.\n2. Continuity of ring final circuit conductors – end-to-end r1, rn, r2, then cross-connect and read at every socket.\n3. Insulation resistance – 500 V DC for 230/400 V circuits, minimum 1 MΩ (Table 64). Disconnect or avoid equipment that could be damaged or give false readings.\n4. Polarity – proved during the continuity tests; switches and single-pole devices in the line.\n5. Earth electrode resistance, if there is one.\n**Live tests.**\n1. Ze at the origin (main earthing conductor disconnected, installation isolated, reconnect afterwards).\n2. Prospective fault current at the origin. On three-phase, the rule of thumb in the guidance is roughly double the line–neutral value unless you measure it directly between lines.\n3. Zs at the furthest point of each circuit, compared against the limit.\n4. RCD tests – at rated residual current (1×IΔn): a general non-delay RCD must trip within 300 ms; a time-delayed S-type between 130 and 500 ms. Then the test button.\n5. Phase sequence with a rotation tester on three-phase circuits and at the board.\n6. Functional tests – switchgear, controls, interlocks, the motor starter, the heating.\n**Ring final readings to expect.** End-to-end r1 and rn should be within 0.05 Ω of each other. With 2.5/1.5 twin-and-earth, r2 is about 1.67 × r1. In singles with a 2.5 cpc, r2 is about equal to r1 – worth remembering, because the AM2 ring is often in singles. At each socket after cross-connection, L–N reads about (r1 + rn)/4 and L–cpc about (r1 + r2)/4, and should be roughly the same everywhere. A reading that climbs towards the middle means you have cross-connected the wrong ends.\n**Limits to compare against.** Maximum Zs for circuit-breakers comes from Chapter 41 (e.g. Table 41.3); the tables give values at the design limit, so compare your measured Zs against about 80% of the table value (or the corrected figures in the OSG/GN3) to allow for conductor temperature. For a B32 at 0.4 s the table value is 1.37 Ω. Disconnection times are in Table 41.1 – 0.4 s for TN final circuits at 230 V, applied (Reg 411.3.2.2) to final circuits up to 63 A with one or more socket-outlets and up to 32 A supplying only fixed equipment.\n**What goes where on the EIC.**\n- Supply characteristics: system type (e.g. TN-C-S), number of phases and wires, nominal voltage (400/230 V), frequency, PFC and Ze with units.\n- Main switch: BS (EN) number, poles, rating; earthing and bonding conductor sizes and materials.\n- Schedule of circuit details: circuit number and description, wiring type and reference method, points served, conductor sizes, device BS EN, type, rating, breaking capacity, and the maximum permitted Zs.\n- Schedule of test results: continuity, IR with test voltage, polarity, Zs, RCD time and test button, and a remarks column.\n**Paperwork errors that fail people.**\n- Missing units (MΩ, Ω, ms, kA).\n- Wrong limit copied (e.g. Type C value for a Type B device).\n- Blank boxes – use N/A where an item genuinely does not apply. (\"LIM\" is a limitation code for the EICR, not something to lean on for an EIC.)\n- Unsigned or undated certificate, or signatures missing from design/construction/inspection sections.\n- IR recorded as a figure with no \">\" when the meter overranged.\n- Readings that do not agree with each other (Zs smaller than Ze, for example).","worked":[{"q":"Ring final in singles, 2.5 mm² L, N and cpc. End-to-end: r1 = 0.52 Ω, rn = 0.53 Ω, r2 = 0.86 Ω.","steps":["Think: In singles with the same size cpc, r2 should be close to r1. 0.86 is well high.","Do: Suspect a high-resistance cpc joint or termination on the ring. Check every cpc termination, especially the board and any joints.","Check: Re-measure r2 after rectifying – it should be close to r1 and rn."],"answer":"r2 too high for singles – high-resistance cpc joint; find and fix."},{"q":"A 30 mA RCBO on the socket circuit trips in 24 ms at 1×IΔn. The spare box for the 5× test is on the form.","steps":["Think: The required test is at 1×IΔn; 24 ms is well inside 300 ms.","Do: Record 24 ms with units, tick the test button column. Fill any additional columns the centre's form asks for, or mark N/A.","Check: No blank boxes; units shown."],"answer":"Pass – record 24 ms, test button ticked, N/A anything not done."},{"q":"Insulation resistance on a lighting circuit: meter shows '>299 MΩ'. How do you record it?","steps":["Think: The meter has overranged – the real value is above its range.","Do: Record '>299' in MΩ in the IR column with the test voltage (500 V).","Check: Units present, and the reading is well above 1 MΩ."],"answer":"Record >299 MΩ at 500 V – a pass."}],"keys":["Dead tests before live: continuity, ring, IR, polarity; then Ze, PFC, Zs, RCD, phase sequence, functional.","IR at 500 V DC, minimum 1 MΩ for 230/400 V circuits.","Ring in singles with 2.5 cpc: r1 ≈ rn ≈ r2.","General 30 mA RCD: trip within 300 ms at 1×IΔn; then press the test button.","Compare Zs against about 80% of the Table 41.3 value (B32 table value 1.37 Ω).","Units in every box, N/A not blanks, signed and dated."],"ref":"BS 7671 Chapter 64 (Reg 643 series, Table 64); BS 7671 Tables 41.1 and 41.3; BS 7671 Appendix 6 (model forms); IET Guidance Note 3; IET On-Site Guide"},{"id":"P-fault","topic":"AM2: Fault diagnosis","title":"Fault diagnosis – find it, name it, prove it","mins":8,"body":"In the fault section you are not just asked to make it work. NET's guidance asks you to state and record **what type** of fault it is (short or open circuit, high resistance or mis-connection), **where** it is – between which two points (or on what piece of equipment) and on which conductor – and **how** you would rectify it and prove the repair. Check your centre's candidate pack for the exact wording of the report sheet, and answer in its terms.\n**The method.** Assessors want to see a logical process, not random poking.\n1. Gather information: what does the brief say the symptom is? Operate the circuit (if allowed) and observe. Check the drawing so you know how it should be connected.\n2. Isolate safely: full safe isolation procedure, every time.\n3. Test: pick the test that will distinguish between the possible faults – continuity or insulation resistance, mostly.\n4. Interpret: compare readings against what the circuit should give.\n5. Locate: narrow it down to the section between two accessories and a specific conductor.\n6. Rectify (or state the rectification, depending on the brief).\n7. Prove: re-test the relevant dead tests, then energise and functional-test.\n**Reading the meter – the four fault types.**\n- **Open circuit**: a conductor path that should be continuous reads open or out of range on continuity. Example: L continuity from board to the last light shows OL; everything before a certain point reads normal.\n- **Short circuit**: insulation resistance between two live conductors, or live to earth, reads near zero (with loads and lamps removed). A true short reads 0.00 MΩ; a low but non-zero reading points to damaged or wet insulation.\n- **High resistance**: continuity is there but much higher than the cable length and size would give – loose terminal, strand damage, a poor crimp or joint. The circuit may work but get hot, show high Zs, or cause voltage drop.\n- **Mis-connection**: every conductor is continuous and IR is fine, but the circuit does the wrong thing – reversed polarity, swapped strappers, the ring connected to itself wrongly, a control core on the wrong terminal. Found by polarity, continuity between specific terminals, and functional testing against the drawing.\n**The half-split.** On a radial or a long run with several accessories, do not start at one end and test every point. Open the circuit near the middle, test each half, and throw away the good half. Repeat. Four accessories take two tests instead of four; eight take three.\n**Ring final clues.**\n- One end-to-end reading open: that conductor is broken somewhere on the ring.\n- End-to-end readings unequal (e.g. r1 twice rn): high resistance or a break with a spur-like path.\n- Cross-connected socket readings climbing towards the middle: ends cross-connected wrongly (or legs swapped at the board).\n- One socket much higher than the others: high-resistance termination at that socket.\n**Reporting exactly.** \"Open circuit on the cpc between socket 3 and socket 4\" earns marks. \"Earth fault on the ring\" does not. Always name the fault type, the conductor (line, neutral, cpc, strapper, control core) and the two points. Then the fix: \"Remake the cpc termination at socket 4, then re-test continuity and Zs.\"\n**Proving the repair.** After any rectification, repeat the test that found it, plus anything the repair could have affected: continuity, IR, polarity, then Zs and a functional check on re-energising.","worked":[{"q":"Lighting radial, four fittings. With lamps out and switches on, IR L–N reads 0.00 MΩ. L–E and N–E are >200 MΩ.","steps":["Think: Zero between line and neutral with no load connected is a short circuit between those two conductors.","Do: Half-split – disconnect at the second fitting and test each side to find which half has the zero reading; repeat until you are between two points.","Check: Once found and repaired, IR L–N returns to a high value; re-energise and functional test."],"answer":"Short circuit L–N; half-split to locate between two fittings; repair and re-test IR."},{"q":"Ring final: r1 = 0.48 Ω, rn = 0.49 Ω, r2 = OL.","steps":["Think: Line and neutral rings are fine; the cpc ring is broken somewhere.","Do: With the ring ends linked (cpc to cpc at the board), test cpc continuity from the board to each socket to find where the reading jumps.","Check: Repair, re-do the end-to-end and cross-connected tests."],"answer":"Open circuit on the cpc of the ring – locate between two sockets."},{"q":"Radial socket circuit works, but R1+R2 reads 1.9 Ω where a similar run reads about 0.4 Ω. IR is fine.","steps":["Think: Continuous but far too high – high resistance, usually a termination.","Do: Measure R1+R2 at each socket in turn to see where it jumps, then check line and cpc terminations at that point.","Check: After remaking, R1+R2 back to expected, then Zs within limit."],"answer":"High resistance joint – find where the reading steps, remake, re-test."}],"keys":["Report: fault type, conductor, and between which two points.","Open = no continuity; short = near-zero IR; high resistance = continuity but too high; mis-connection = everything reads fine but it's wired wrong.","Remove lamps and loads before IR, or you will 'find' a short that is the lamp.","Half-split: test in the middle and discard the good half.","Safe isolation before every hands-on test.","Prove the repair: repeat the test that found it, then functional test."],"ref":"IET Guidance Note 3 (test methods); BS 7671 Chapter 64"},{"id":"P-day","topic":"AM2: Knowledge test & the day","title":"The knowledge test and the day itself","mins":6,"body":"Section E (Assessment of Applied Knowledge) is an online test of 30 multiple-choice questions in one hour, covering health and safety, BS 7671 and the Building Regulations. It is open book using the publications provided: NET lists BS 7671, GN3 and the OSG, and for the AM2E also the IET Electrician's Guide to the Building Regulations – no other documentation is allowed. Open book is only an advantage if you can find things fast. Candidates who know the structure of the books finish early; candidates who flick through from page one run out of time. Check your centre's candidate pack for current details.\n**Know where things live in BS 7671.**\n- Part 1 scope and fundamental principles; Part 2 definitions.\n- Part 3 assessment of general characteristics.\n- Part 4 protection for safety – Chapter 41 shock protection (Table 41.1 disconnection times, Tables 41.2–41.4 max Zs, Table 41.5 for RCDs), Chapter 43 overcurrent.\n- Part 5 selection and erection – Chapter 52 wiring systems, Chapter 53 switchgear and isolation, Chapter 54 earthing and protective conductors (Tables 54.7 and 54.8 for sizes), Chapter 55 other equipment.\n- Part 6 inspection and testing – Chapter 64 initial verification (Table 64 for IR).\n- Part 7 special installations or locations – 701 bathrooms, 722 EV charging, and the rest.\n- Appendices – Appendix 4 current-carrying capacity and voltage drop, Appendix 6 model forms, Appendix 15 ring and radial arrangements.\n**Use the index.** The index is the fastest route for a keyword question. Look up the noun, not the verb (\"bonding\", not \"connect\"). If an index entry gives several regulation numbers, the one with the most specific wording is usually the one.\n**Know where the key tables are** so you can go straight to them in the copies provided: Table 41.1, the max Zs tables, Table 54.7, Table 54.8, Table 64, Part 7 sections, Appendix 4. In GN3, the test sequence and the IR and RCD sections. In the OSG, the tables of max Zs corrected for temperature and the cable sizing tables. Use your own tabbed copies to practise beforehand.\n**Time management in the test.** Read each question once properly. If you know it, answer it. If you need to look it up and it is taking more than a couple of minutes, flag it and move on. Go back to flagged questions at the end. Never leave a question unanswered if the test allows you to guess.\n**The practical days.**\n- Read the brief twice before you touch anything: once for the overall picture, once with a pen, highlighting quantities, heights, device ratings and anything unusual.\n- Build a timeline: know roughly where you should be at lunch on the installation day.\n- Keep your bay tidy – it is safer, faster, and the assessor notices.\n- If something on the drawing is unclear, ask. Asking a question is not a failure; guessing wrong can be.\n- If you make a mistake, fix it properly rather than hoping it will not be seen.\n**Kit to take** (check the pack for what the centre supplies):\n- Photo ID and your booking details.\n- GS38 voltage indicator and proving unit, lock-off kit, padlock, labels.\n- Insulated screwdrivers, torque screwdriver, side cutters, strippers, knife, SWA tools, tape measure, spirit level, marker, pencils.\n- PPE: safety boots, glasses, gloves.\n- Calculator, pens, highlighter, water and food.\n**Staying calm.** Nerves make experienced people skip steps they would never skip on site. Slow down on the safety-critical bits: isolation, testing, energising. If you feel rushed, stop for thirty seconds, re-read the brief, and pick the next single task.","worked":[{"q":"Knowledge test question: 'What is the maximum disconnection time for a 230 V final circuit rated 20 A in a TN system?'","steps":["Think: This is shock protection – Chapter 41, disconnection times table.","Do: Go to Table 41.1, find TN, 230 V column, final circuit ≤32 A.","Check: The answer is 0.4 s (Table 41.1 applies to final circuits up to 63 A with socket-outlets and up to 32 A fixed only, so 20 A is covered)."],"answer":"0.4 s – Table 41.1."},{"q":"Knowledge test question on minimum main protective bonding size for a PME supply with 25 mm² supply neutral.","steps":["Think: Bonding sizes are in Chapter 54, and PME has its own table.","Do: Go to Table 54.8, find the row for the supply neutral up to 35 mm².","Check: 10 mm² copper."],"answer":"10 mm² – Table 54.8."},{"q":"Installation day: at lunch you realise you are well behind on the containment.","steps":["Think: Nothing can be terminated until containment and cables are in; testing time is fixed.","Do: Re-prioritise: finish containment for the circuits that feed the most later tasks, keep quality up, do not skip grommets or bushes.","Check: Tell the assessor if you think you are running out of time – but don't cut safety corners."],"answer":"Re-plan around dependencies; keep quality and safety, no shortcuts."}],"keys":["Know the Parts of BS 7671 – half the speed in an open-book test.","Use the index by noun; know where the key tables are in the copies provided.","Flag and move on; come back to hard questions.","Read the brief twice – second time with a highlighter.","Check the candidate pack for kit, books and timings.","Slow down on isolation, testing and energising – that's where nerves bite."],"ref":"NET AM2/AM2E candidate guidance (netservices.org.uk); BS 7671:2018+A2:2022 (structure, Reg 411.3.2.2, Tables 41.1, 54.7, 54.8, 64); IET Guidance Note 3; IET On-Site Guide"}];
